Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether you’ll likely itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and general state) forms you’ll need. I do have two quick clarifying questions near the end so my recommendations are accurate.

Summary / headline
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for federal taxes (married filing jointly) because your only clear itemizable amount is the $1,400 charitable gift and that is far below the standard deduction. 
- Your home office, the self‑employment income/expenses, and student‑loan interest all affect your federal tax return even if you take the standard deduction — because the home office and business expenses are reported on Schedule C and the student‑loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (if eligible).
- You will need Form 1040 plus Schedule C (profit/loss from self‑employment), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule 1 (adjustments to income), and possibly Form 8829 (if you use the regular home‑office method). Also collect W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, and 1099‑INT/1098‑E as applicable. You’ll also file a Massachusetts resident return.

What is deductible (Federal) — item-by-item
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital $84,900): fully reported as wage income on Form 1040 (W‑2 required).
- Self‑employment income ($8,900 from graphic design): report on Schedule C. You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including a home office allowance, supplies, business portion of internet, software, etc.).
- Interest from Marcus and Charles Schwab ($920 + $320 = $1,240): taxable interest income (reported on Form 1040). Expect Form 1099‑INT from each institution if over reporting thresholds.
- Student loan interest ($4,800): caution — only interest paid in the tax year (and reported on Form 1098‑E) is potentially deductible up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line) on Schedule 1, subject to AGI phaseouts. You need the Form 1098‑E for the specific year to claim it. (See clarifying question below.)
- Charity $1,400 to Northwest Harvest: deductible if you itemize (reported on Schedule A). You said you have a receipt — good. If you do not itemize you cannot deduct it on the federal return (there were special rules some years, but generally charitable gifts are only deductible if you itemize).
- $890 “unreimbursed expenses”: whether these are deductible depends on what they are:
  - If these are employee unreimbursed business expenses (you as a W‑2 employee), miscellaneous employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
  - If they are expenses related to your self‑employment / graphic design work, include them as business expenses on Schedule C (deductible there).
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; home is 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo): deductible only if used in connection with your self‑employment (Schedule C) and used regularly and exclusively for business (and principal place of business). There are two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft = 155 × $5 = $775 deduction (reported on Schedule C).
  - Regular (actual expense) method: allocate a percentage of actual home costs to the office. Your office is 155/780 = ~19.9% of the home. If you rent at $2,100/month → annual rent = $25,200 → business portion of rent = 19.9% × $25,200 ≈ $5,010. You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., to increase the deduction. Using the regular method you complete Form 8829 to figure business‑use‑of‑home (for taxpayers who are self‑employed). In your situation the regular method will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method (assuming your rent is the primary home expense you want to allocate).
  - Important: the home office deduction is only allowed for self‑employed filers (Schedule C). If you are an employee using a home office for your W‑2 job, you generally cannot claim this deduction (exceptions are rare).
- Self‑employment tax: you will owe self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3%) on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You may deduct one half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

Will you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Short answer: almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Why: your known potential itemized deductions are just $1,400 charitable + any state/local taxes and mortgage interest if applicable. If you are renting and don’t have large mortgage interest or big state/local taxes, itemized total will be well below the standard deduction for married filing jointly.
- Note: the home office deduction is reported on Schedule C and does not depend on whether you itemize. So you get the benefit of the home office/business deductions even if you take the standard deduction on Form 1040.

Estimated numbers (example calculation)
(Assumptions: the $890 are business expenses; the $4,800 student loan interest is actually interest paid in the tax year — see clarifying questions below.)
- Gross income: 84,900 (W‑2) + 8,900 (self‑employment) + 1,240 (interest) = $95,040.
- Home office (regular): ~19.9% of annual rent $25,200 ≈ $5,010. If you claim that as a Schedule C expense plus the $890 other business expenses, net self‑employment profit ≈ 8,900 − 5,010 − 890 = $2,? ~ $3,000.
- Self‑employment tax: applied to ~92.35% of net SE income. For net ≈ $3,000, SE tax ≈ $400–450; half is deductible on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 deduction (if you paid that much in the year and are under the AGI phaseout). With your income level (approx AGI before student loan interest under $120k), you should be eligible for most or all of the $2,500 deduction (but I need to confirm the exact phaseout thresholds for the tax year you’re filing).
- After those business and adjustment deductions, you would then subtract the standard deduction (if you choose it) and compute tax on the remainder.

Forms you will need (Federal)
- Form 1040 — main individual income tax form.
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- Schedule C (Form 1040), Profit or Loss From Business — to report your graphic design income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home, if you use the regular method for the home office. If you use the simplified method you do not need Form 8829.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to claim adjustments to income such as student loan interest (and to carry Schedule C net profit/loss into Form 1040).
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — only if you decide to itemize (probably not needed).
- Form 1099‑INTs for interest; Form 1098‑E for student loan interest (gives amount eligible to deduct); any 1099‑NEC if a client issued one (reportable on Schedule C).
- Keep receipts for charitable giving, business expenses, home office documentation showing exclusive use, and rent/utility bills.

State filing (Massachusetts)
- You will file a Massachusetts resident return (Form 1). Massachusetts rules differ from federal rules in some respects (for example, what adjustments/deductions are allowed and how student loan interest is treated). Check the MA Department of Revenue instructions or ask if you’d like me to look up specifics. If you live in Boston and are MA resident, you’ll need to include your federal AGI and then apply MA‑specific adjustments.

Other practical notes and recommendations
- Keep good records: 1098‑E for student loan interest (yearly), receipts for the $1,400 donation, invoices/receipts for business expenses, and documentation showing the exclusive regular use of the home office (floorplan/photos, a log, and bills showing home square footage/rent).
- If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are employee expenses (not self‑employment), they are not deductible for 2018–2025. If they were business expenses for your graphic design work, include them on Schedule C.
- If you don’t have enough tax withheld to cover income plus SE tax, you may need to make estimated tax payments quarterly (to avoid penalties).
- Consider using tax software that supports Schedule C and Form 8829 or consult a tax preparer if you want to maximize use of the home office deduction and ensure state rules are handled correctly.

Quick clarifying questions so I can give final, precise advice
1) Is the $4,800 student loan interest amount the amount you paid in the single tax year you’re filing (i.e., interest paid in 2024) and do you have a Form 1098‑E for that year? Or is $4,800 the total interest paid across several prior years? (Only interest paid in the tax year can be deducted.)
2) Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” related to your self‑employed graphic design work (i.e., business expenses), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
3) Do you have any mortgage interest, property tax, or other large deductible expenses not listed? (That could change the itemize vs standard decision.)

If you want, I can:
- Run a more exact “what‑if” tax calculation for federal tax (estimate taxable income, SE tax, and estimated federal tax) if you confirm the clarifying points above.
- Prepare a checklist of the exact forms and line placements you’ll need to complete in tax software or for a tax preparer.

