Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll cover what’s deductible (federal), whether you’ll likely benefit from itemizing or taking the standard deduction, how the home‑office deduction works, what forms you’ll need, and a few follow‑up questions so we can finalize numbers.

Quick summary up front
- Based on the amounts you gave, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for federal taxes (unless you have substantial other itemizable amounts you didn’t list — see questions below).  
- You can deduct some student‑loan interest as an above‑the‑line deduction (up to the statutory limit, subject to income phaseouts) only for interest paid in the tax year you’re filing. If the $4,800 is interest from prior years (2016–2020) you cannot deduct it on your 2024 return.  
- Your graphic‑design income is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C; that is also the vehicle to take a home‑office deduction and any business expenses (including the $890 if those are business expenses). You’ll also owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE) and can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.  
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040; Schedule 1; Schedule C; Schedule SE; either Form 8829 (regular home office method) or the simplified home office calculation entered on Schedule C; Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) if you claim the QBI (20%) deduction; Schedule A only if you itemize (probably not). Also bring your W‑2 and 1099‑INT(s) and any 1099‑NEC if you received them.

Detailed breakdown

1) Income to report (federal)
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $84,900 — report on Form 1040 (W‑2).  
- Self‑employment (graphic design) income: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (gross receipts), subtract business expenses to get net profit. If no 1099‑NEC, you still must report the income.  
- Interest income: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Charles Schwab) = $1,240 — report as taxable interest on Form 1040.

2) Student‑loan interest
- Only interest you actually paid in the tax year (2024) is potentially deductible on your 2024 return. The deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1), so you can claim it whether you itemize or take the standard deduction.  
- The maximum student‑loan interest deduction is limited (historically $2,500) and phases out at higher incomes. Given your W‑2 + other income (~$95k before adjustments), you will likely be under the phaseout for MFJ and eligible for up to the allowed limit, but you need to confirm the exact year’s phaseout thresholds.  
- Important: if the $4,800 represents interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020) that wasn’t deducted then, you generally cannot deduct that old interest on 2024 returns. You can only deduct interest paid in 2024.

3) Charitable contribution: $1,400
- Cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct these on your federal return (except in certain temporary rules that applied in past years — generally not applicable). Keep your receipt.

4) Unreimbursed expenses: $890
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (that is, you incurred them as an employee for Riverside Hospital), those are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 due to the TCJA suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions.  
- If the $890 are business expenses related to your graphic‑design self‑employment work, report them on Schedule C as ordinary/necessary business expenses (they reduce your self‑employment net income).

5) Home office (155 sq ft exclusive) — you’re a renter: how to treat it
- You appear eligible for a home‑office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment graphic‑design work (i.e., not for your hospital job). Because you have self‑employment income, you can take it.  
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft (up to 300 sq ft). For 155 sq ft that’s 155 * $5 = $775. No Form 8829 needed; report on Schedule C.  
  b) Regular (actual expense) method: allocate the business percentage of your home expenses. Business % = (home office sq ft) / (total home sq ft) = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. If your rent is $2,100/month = $25,200/year, the home‑office share of rent = 25,200 * 0.1987 ≈ $5,006. With the regular method you can also allocate a share of utilities and renters insurance, which increases the deduction. To use the regular method, you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.  
- Which to choose? The regular method gives a much larger deduction here because your rent is high relative to the simplified rate. The regular method will reduce your Schedule C net profit substantially (and reduce both income tax and SE tax); it also requires good recordkeeping. Use Form 8829 for the regular method.

6) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- You will owe self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You may owe quarterly estimated tax if you expect to owe self‑employment tax/underwithheld tax.  
- You may deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1. Also, some self‑employed deductions (health insurance premium deduction, retirement contributions, etc.) may be available.

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction
- You may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A) for qualified business income from your Schedule C. Because your self‑employment income is relatively small, you would likely qualify; QBI reduces taxable income (drawn on Form 8995 or 8995‑A depending on complexity). Example: if net QBI after expenses is ~$3,900, the QBI deduction could be about 20% of that (~$780). This is an additional benefit of a Schedule C deduction.

8) Itemize vs standard deduction
- You listed $1,400 charitable donations and (possibly) $890 unreimbursed expenses (likely not deductible unless business). That $1,400 alone is far less than the standard deduction for married filing jointly. You did not list mortgage interest, substantial state and local taxes, or large medical expenses. So most likely the standard deduction will be larger and therefore the right choice for federal taxes.  
- Standard deduction (for MFJ) is generally substantially higher than a small amount of itemized deductions. If you have other itemizable items (large property tax, state income tax, mortgage interest, high medical expenses, large charitable gifts), re‑compute; otherwise choose standard.  
- Note: student loan interest deduction is above the line and does not depend on itemizing.

9) State (Massachusetts) filing
- You will need to file a Massachusetts resident return (Form 1). MA tax rules differ from federal; you report W‑2, interest, and your self‑employment/net income. Massachusetts generally taxes most interest income, and has its own rules for deductions and exemptions. You may not get the same deductions as federal (and MA has different treatment of student loan interest). Check MA Form 1 instructions or consult your preparer for exact MA treatment. If you have MA income tax withheld on your W‑2, that helps cover state liability.

10) Likely forms to prepare and attach (federal)
- Form 1040 (main form)  
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction, one‑half SE tax, etc.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report graphic design income and business expenses, including home office (or use Form 8829).  
- Schedule SE (self‑employment tax)  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the regular method for home office. If you choose the simplified method, no 8829 is needed—enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C.  
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) — if you claim the qualified business income deduction.  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize. Probably not needed in your case.  
- Keep (but don’t attach) 1098‑E (student loan interest statement), 1099‑INTs (interest), W‑2, any 1099‑NEC / records of client payments, receipts for charitable contributions and business expenses.

Practical next steps / questions for you
1. Did you actually pay $4,800 of student‑loan interest during tax year 2024, and do you have a 1098‑E for 2024 showing that amount? Or does the 2016–2020 note mean those payments were in prior years? (If payments were in earlier years, they cannot be deducted on 2024 returns.)  
2. Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses related to your self‑employed graphic‑design work (i.e., business supplies, software, travel for clients)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job? If they are business expenses, we’ll put them on Schedule C. If they’re employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.  
3. Do you have any other itemizable amounts not listed (mortgage interest, property tax, large state income tax payments, medical expenses above threshold, larger charitable contributions)? If not, standard deduction will almost certainly be best.  
4. Do you want to use the simplified home‑office method ($775) or the regular method (estimated ~$5,000 from rent plus share of utilities)? I recommend regular method given the rent amount, but it requires Form 8829 and documentation.

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers for you if you confirm (a) whether the $4,800 is 2024 interest (and/or provide the 1098‑E amount), (b) whether the $890 is business expense, (c) any state tax/property tax/mortgage interest amounts, and (d) whether you want the simplified or regular home‑office calculation.  
- Or I can prepare a checklist of documents to collect before you file.

Disclaimer
This is general guidance based on the facts you provided; tax law and thresholds change and state rules differ. For detailed tax planning or if your situation has other complexities, consider consulting a CPA or tax professional.

