Short answer — probably take the standard deduction for 2024, treat the home office as a Schedule C business expense (you’ll likely get a larger deduction using the actual-expense method than the simplified method), report the $8,900 as self-employment income on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax on the net, and gather your W‑2, any 1099s/1098s and receipts. Below are details, calculations, required forms, and next steps.

1) Which income goes where
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital ($84,900): report on Form 1040 (W‑2 box wages).
- Graphic-design income ($8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Do not net it with W‑2 wages — it’s business income.
- Interest ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): report as interest income on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INT/1099‑B as applicable).

2) Home office
- You said 155 sq ft exclusive use out of 780 sq ft total. Percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
- Because you’re self‑employed, the home‑office deduction goes on Schedule C. Two methods:
  - Actual-expense method (likely larger here): allocate business percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Example using rent $2,100/month = $25,200/year → business share ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,009. You’d include that as an expense on Schedule C; if you use actual expenses for depreciation or mortgage interest you'd use Form 8829 to compute allowable home business use (Form 8829 is used for business use of home).
  - Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 (no Form 8829 needed). Because your rent-based allocation (~$5,000) is much larger than $775, the actual method will likely produce a bigger deduction.
- Requirements: exclusive and regular use for business and principal place of business (or used to meet clients). Keep measurements, a simple diagram, lease/rent records, utility bills, and notes showing exclusive business use.

3) Self‑employment tax & deductions
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax. SE tax is computed on Schedule SE (roughly 15.3% on 92.35% of net self‑employment earnings). You can deduct half of your SE tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.
- Business expenses (including home‑office share and any ordinary and necessary costs for your graphic design work) reduce Schedule C net income and therefore reduce SE tax.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- Only interest YOU PAID in the tax year (2024) is potentially deductible, and the lender should send Form 1098‑E showing interest paid for 2024. You cannot deduct interest paid in prior years on your 2024 return.
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500 maximum) but phases out at higher incomes. Check your Form 1098‑E and your MAGI — if you didn’t actually pay student loan interest in 2024, there’s nothing to deduct for 2024.

5) Charitable donation ($1,400)
- Cash gift to a qualified charity (receipt available) is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200, $1,400 of charitable contributions by itself is unlikely to make itemizing better than the standard deduction.
- Keep the receipt and charity name/confirmation.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, those are not deductible on federal returns (suspended under TCJA for most employees).
- If they are business expenses for your graphic design (supplies, software, mileage, advertising, etc.), include them on Schedule C — they reduce your self‑employment net income.

7) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- Standard deduction (2024, married filing jointly): $29,200.
- Your likely itemizable deductions based on what you provided: charitable $1,400 (plus any state & local taxes, mortgage interest, etc. — you didn’t list mortgage interest). Unless you have other significant itemizable items (large state/local taxes up to the SALT cap, mortgage interest, large medical expenses over the AGI threshold, etc.), the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than your itemized total. So you will likely take the standard deduction.
- Note: home office deductions are taken on Schedule C (reduce AGI) irrespective of whether you itemize or take the standard deduction.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments above-the-line, e.g., half of SE tax or student loan interest)
- Schedule C (report self-employment income and business expenses)
- Schedule SE (compute self-employment tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for the home-office deduction. Not needed for the simplified method.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (seems unlikely here).
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC or records of self‑employment receipts (even if you did not receive a 1099, you must report the income).
- 1099‑INT, brokerage statements, W‑2, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest, if applicable).

9) Massachusetts state tax
- You will also file a MA state return (Form 1). State rules differ from federal rules — e.g., treatment of deductions or credits may vary. If you want, I can look up specific MA treatment for student loan interest or home office effects on state taxable income (or you can consult MA DOR / a tax preparer).

10) Action items / checklist for you
- Confirm whether the $4,800 in student loan interest was paid in 2024. If yes, find 2024 Form 1098‑E from MOHELA.
- Confirm whether the $890 is business expense for the graphic design work (if yes, list receipts).
- Gather: W‑2 from Riverside; any 1099‑NEC or client payment records; 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV from banks/brokers; lease/rent statements showing monthly rent and address; receipts for charitable contribution and business expenses; measurement/diagram of home office.
- Decide whether to use actual method or simplified method for home office (I can compute both side‑by‑side if you want).
- If you want me to estimate taxes, tell me which of the following apply: actual student interest paid in 2024 (amount), confirm $890 are business expenses, list any other deductible items (state taxes paid, mortgage interest, major medical expenses, etc.).

11) A few quick examples/calculations (approximate)
- Home‑office percent: 155/780 ≈ 19.87%.
- Annual rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Home office share (actual) ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,009.
- Simplified home‑office deduction: 155 × $5 = $775.
- If you use actual method, that ~ $5,000 reduces your Schedule C gross of $8,900 to roughly $3,900 before other business expenses — this significantly reduces self‑employment tax and overall taxable income.

12) Disclaimer
I’m not a CPA or tax attorney. This is general guidance based on the facts you provided. For definitive tax planning or filing, consider using professional tax software or a tax preparer/cpa — especially if you want to maximize the home office deduction while meeting all IRS substantiation rules.

