Thanks — I can walk you through this. Below is a clear summary of what you can and cannot deduct, how to treat the home office, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, and which forms you’ll need. I also list a few questions at the end so I can give a more precise calculation.

Quick summary of your numbers (what you reported)
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $84,900
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $8,900
- Taxable interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240
- Home: 155 sq ft exclusive home office; total rented space 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month
- Student loan interest: $4,800 (you wrote 2016–2020 — see question below)
- Charitable cash gift: $1,400 (receipt)
- Unreimbursed expenses: $890 (need to know whether these are employee or business)

How each item is treated
- W-2 wages: Report on Form 1040 (attach W-2). Withholding shown on the W-2 reduces tax owed.
- Self‑employment income (graphic design): Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Any ordinary and necessary business expenses should be on Schedule C (see below).
  - Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) applies to net Schedule C income; you’ll compute it on Schedule SE. You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Taxable interest: Reported on Form 1040 (from your 1099‑INTs).
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890):
  - If these are business expenses for your graphic design work (supplies, software, client-related costs), include them on Schedule C and they reduce your self‑employment net income.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job, those are not deductible for federal tax (suspended since 2018).
- Home‑office (155 sq ft exclusive) — you can claim a deduction if you’re self‑employed and the space is used regularly and exclusively for business:
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5.00 × square footage = 155 × $5 = $775 (no Form 8829).
    - Regular (actual expense) method: prorate your shared home expenses by the business-use percentage (155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%). For rent only: annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 → home‑office portion of rent ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,010. If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, you can allocate their business portion too.
  - Important limits and rules:
    - The home‑office deduction cannot create a business loss for the year that offsets other income beyond limits. In practice you cannot deduct more than your net income from that business (Schedule C) for the year. Any disallowed excess under the regular method is carried forward.
    - For renters, you cannot take depreciation for the dwelling (depreciation typically applies to homeowners).
    - If you choose the regular method you’ll use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) along with Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you just report it on Schedule C and do not file Form 8829.
- Student loan interest ($4,800):
  - The federal student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1) of up to $2,500 for interest paid in the tax year. It is limited by your MAGI (modified adjusted gross income) and phases out for higher incomes. You must have actually paid the interest in the tax year and typically have a Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer.
  - Clarification needed: the deduction is for interest paid in the tax year — if $4,800 is interest paid in 2024, the maximum deduction is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseout). If $4,800 refers to interest accrued in prior years or a different period, it may not be deductible now.
- Charitable contribution ($1,400):
  - Deductible only if you itemize (reported on Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you do not deduct this contribution (unless a temporary above‑the‑line rule applies for a specific year — generally not applicable now).
- State & Local Taxes (SALT):
  - If you itemize, SALT (state income taxes + property taxes) is deductible but capped at $10,000 (total for married filing jointly). You didn’t list property tax or total state tax paid; that figure matters a lot for the itemize vs standard decision.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- For most married couples filing jointly the standard deduction will be larger than a small itemized total (only $1,400 charitable + maybe SALT). Because you didn’t list mortgage interest or large property tax payments, it’s likely the standard deduction will be higher.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI) whether you itemize or not — so you’ll still get that up to limits.
- Rough decision rule: total Schedule A itemized deductions (charity + SALT up to $10k + mortgage interest + other eligible items) must exceed the standard deduction to itemize. If you want I can compute this with exact standard deduction for 2024 once you confirm whether you have mortgage interest, property tax, and how much SALT you paid.

Estimated rough numbers (to illustrate)
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200
- Home office % ≈ 155/780 ≈ 19.87%
- Regular home office (rent only) ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,010
- Simplified home office = $775
- If your net self‑employment income before home office is small (e.g., 8,900 gross − $890 business expenses = $8,010), you can take the ~$5,010 home‑office deduction using the regular method (subject to the rule that it cannot exceed net business income). That would reduce your self‑employment taxable income and lower SE tax and income tax.
- Student loan interest deduction will be capped at $2,500 (if you paid that much in the tax year) and phased out depending on MAGI (I can check the exact phaseout if you give your expected AGI).

Forms you will need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for student loan interest deduction and other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the graphic design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home office
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (charitable donations, SALT etc.)
- Form 1099‑INTs (interest) and Form 1099‑NEC (if your clients issued one) — keep for records and to attach/enter information
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement from MOHELA (if you have one) to substantiate the student interest deduction
- Form 1040‑ES — if you need to make quarterly estimated tax payments for self‑employment income (likely recommended if you have ongoing self‑employment income)

Action items / questions so I can give exact numbers and a recommendation
1. Did you actually pay $4,800 of student loan interest in the tax year you’re filing (i.e., in 2024)? Or does the $4,800 represent interest accrued earlier (2016–2020)? If you have a 1098‑E for the filing year, what amount is on it?
2. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses for your graphic design business (self‑employment) or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
3. Do you have any other deductible items that would go on Schedule A (mortgage interest, property tax, large state income tax, large medical expenses above the floor, etc.)? How much state income tax and property tax did you pay in the year?
4. Do you have utility bills / renter’s insurance / internet amounts (annual totals) you want included in the home‑office regular method calculation? If not, I can calculate using rent only.
5. Do your two clients issue 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC? (If not and you still earned $600+ from a client, they may issue one — but you report income regardless.)

If you want, I can:
- Run two scenarios (standard deduction vs. itemize) using your confirmed numbers and show expected taxable income, estimated federal income tax, and self‑employment tax. Or
- Draft an email or a checklist you can send to your tax preparer with all relevant documents.

Which of the questions above can you answer? If you want me to compute the likely tax impact right now, confirm (1) whether the student loan interest amount is for the tax year, and (2) whether the $890 expenses are business-related; also tell me whether you want me to use the regular home‑office method (rent only) or the simplified method for a baseline.

