I’m not a CPA, but here’s a practical, step‑by‑step look at how your 2024 return will work and what to watch for. If you want definitive answers for your situation (phaseouts, exact AGI, or planning), consider sending these details to a tax pro.

1) Quick summary of your income (based on what you gave)
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $84,900  
- Self‑employment (graphic design, 2 clients): $8,900  
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Charles Schwab) = $1,240  
Estimated total gross income = $95,040

2) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusive of a 780 sq ft residence)
- Business use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%  
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200  
- Allocated rent (actual‑expense method) = 19.9% × $25,200 ≈ $5,007
Options:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (no Form 8829 needed).  
  b) Actual expense method: allocate a portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.; use Form 8829 to report (attach to Schedule C). The allocated rent alone (~$5,007) is large and will be limited to your net profit from the business (see below).
Important limits:
  - The home‑office deduction is only available for self‑employment (Schedule C) or certain business owners — not for W‑2 employees (except a few categories).  
  - The actual method amount cannot exceed your net profit from that business for the year. Any excess is carried forward to future years. It does reduce both income tax and self‑employment tax because it lowers net Schedule C profit.

3) Self‑employment tax and related items
- Report the graphic design income and business expenses on Schedule C. Business expenses include legitimate costs of running the business (software, supplies, marketing, the business share of your home expenses, etc.). Clarify whether your $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the graphic design work — if so, these go on Schedule C; if they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they generally are not deductible (suspended for most taxpayers through 2025).
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit (calculated on Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800 you paid)
- The student loan interest deduction (above‑the‑line) is limited to $2,500 per return in most cases and phases out at higher MAGI levels. You must have actually paid the interest in 2024 and it must be qualified student loan interest. MOHELA is a loan servicer, but confirm that the interest is for qualified student loans and that the $4,800 was paid during the tax year. You may only deduct up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts).

5) Charitable donation ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest)
- Charitable contributions are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, this $1,400 doesn’t give you extra benefit on federal taxes. Keep the receipt for substantiation.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are business expenses related to your self‑employment, deduct them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are generally not deductible for most taxpayers under current law.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- The standard deduction for married filing jointly for 2024 is $29,200 (confirm this when you file).  
- Likely you will take the standard deduction because your known itemizable amounts (charitable $1,400, possible state income tax up to SALT cap, etc.) are probably well under $29,200. You’d only itemize if total deductible expenses (mortgage interest, property tax, SALT up to $10k, charitable gifts, large medical expenses above threshold, etc.) exceed the standard deduction. Based on the figures you provided, standard deduction is most likely better.

8) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)  
- Schedule 1 (to report additional income and adjustments — e.g., student loan interest adjustment, half SE tax)  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $8,900 graphic design income and business expenses  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deduction for half of it  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for the home office (not needed for the simplified method)  
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (to claim charitable contributions and other itemized deductions)  
- Possibly Form 8995 (Qualified Business Income deduction) — if you want to claim the 20% QBI deduction (depends on amounts and whether you qualify)  
- Keep 1099‑NEC/1099‑K if you received them from clients, and 1099‑INTs for interest.

9) Practical recommendations / next steps
- Confirm whether your $890 are self‑employment business expenses or unreimbursed employee expenses. If they’re business expenses, include them on Schedule C.  
- Confirm the $4,800 student loan interest: was all of it paid in tax year 2024 and is it qualified interest? Expect at most a $2,500 deduction (and MAGI phaseout may reduce/limit it).  
- For the home office, consider which method you prefer:
   - Simplified: easier, $775 deduction (for 155 sq ft).  
   - Actual: could allocate ~$5,007 of rent; however, that amount cannot exceed net profit from the business and excess carries forward. If you want to maximize current‑year deductions and you have sufficient net profit, actual may save more. If the actual home‑office allocation would exceed net business profit, only the amount up to net profit can be used this year.  
- Make estimated tax payments if your W‑2 withholding plus credits won’t cover income and self‑employment tax; paying quarterly can avoid penalties. Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe additional tax beyond what’s withheld from the W‑2.
- Keep documents: receipts for donations, MOHELA interest statement (Form 1098‑E if provided), 1099‑NEC/1099‑K from clients, 1099‑INT from banks, records that prove exclusive business use of the home office (measurements, photos, explanation of use), and receipts for business expenses.

10) If you want, I can:
- Run a quick rough tax calculation showing estimated taxable income, SE tax, and whether itemizing helps (I’d need any state tax/property tax paid, other deductions, and exact interest payment timing).  
- Draft an email to your CPA with these details and the clarifying questions above.  
- Help you prepare a Schedule C draft (I can draft a simple one with the numbers you provide).

Would you like me to run a rough calculation/estimate of your federal tax and self‑employment tax? If yes, please confirm:
- Were the $4,800 student loan interest payments all paid in 2024?  
- Are the $890 expenses business expenses for the graphic design work?  
- Do you know how much state income tax you paid in 2024 (or state tax withheld on W‑2)?

