Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s (likely) deductible, how to treat the home office, whether you’ll probably itemize or take the standard deduction, and which federal/state forms you’ll need. If you want exact dollar recommendations I can run the numbers if you provide a couple more items (MA state tax withheld or property tax paid, any mortgage interest, and whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are job-related or business-related).

Summary of your situation (what you gave)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (Boston, MA)
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $84,900
- Self-employment (graphic design): $8,900 (2 clients)
- Interest income: Marcus $920 + Charles Schwab $320 = $1,240
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (MOHELA; loans 2016–2020)
- Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 (have receipt)
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: $890
- Home used exclusively for business: 155 sq ft of 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo

What you can (generally) deduct on your federal return

1) Self-employment (graphic design)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (supplies, software, marketing, etc.).
- Home office (see below) is deductible as a business expense on Schedule C.
- Self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) applies. You’ll compute it on Schedule SE; half of your SE tax is an “above-the-line” deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

2) Home office
- Exclusive business space: 155 / 780 = 19.87% of the home (≈19.9%).
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.
  - Actual expense method: allocate actual allowable home expenses by the business-use percentage. For you, annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 → rent portion for business ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,008. If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, internet portion, repairs, etc., allocate the business share too. Because your rent-share alone (~$5k) exceeds the $775 simplified amount, the actual method will almost certainly give a higher deduction.
- If you use the actual method, you generally use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the allowable deduction and then enter it on Schedule C. If you use the simplified method you do not use Form 8829.

3) Student loan interest
- Federal: up to $2,500 of student loan interest is deductible as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts based on modified adjusted gross income (MAGI). You paid $4,800, but the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher MAGI). For married filing jointly the phaseout starts at a fairly high MAGI (check the IRS table for the tax year you’re filing) — if your MAGI is well below the phaseout range you can take the full $2,500; above the range you may get none.
- You should receive Form 1098-E (or a similar statement) from MOHELA showing interest paid.

4) Charitable contributions
- The $1,400 cash gifts are deductible if you itemize and you have the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you do not separately deduct these for federal taxes (no longer a small universal above-the-line credit for cash gifts).
- Keep the receipt(s) for substantiation.

5) Interest income
- The $1,240 from savings/investments is taxable interest income and is reported on Form 1040. Expect 1099-INTs from the banks/brokerage.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890)
- Federal: miscellaneous unreimbursed employee business expenses are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension). If these are expenses related to your W-2 job (not your self-employment), they’re not deductible on your federal return.
- If those expenses are actually business expenses for your self-employment (graphic design), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.

7) SALT (state and local taxes)
- State income tax and property taxes are deductible as itemized deductions on Schedule A but capped at $10,000 total for SALT (federal limit). You didn’t provide your MA state tax withheld or property tax totals; include those when deciding to itemize.

Standard deduction vs. itemize (federal)
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction is substantial. (Check the exact dollar for the tax year you’re filing — if you want I’ll look it up or use the IRS amount for the correct year.)
- Compare: your likely itemizable items would include charitable deductions ($1,400) + SALT (state income tax withheld + property tax up to $10k combined) + any deductible mortgage interest (not provided) + other allowable items. Because your rent-share home office is a business expense on Schedule C (not an itemized deduction), it does not factor into Schedule A.
- Based on the items you listed (only $1,400 charitable and unknown SALT/mortgage interest), you will probably take the standard deduction — unless you have significant state taxes withheld or mortgage interest or other itemizable amounts that push your total itemized deductions above the standard deduction.
- If you want a precise recommendation I can calculate it if you give me: MA state tax withheld (from W-2), property tax paid in 2024 (if any), and mortgage interest paid (if any).

State (Massachusetts) issues
- You’ll file the Massachusetts resident return (Form 1). MA rules differ from federal in some areas (for example, MA may treat some deductions differently). Student loan interest and unreimbursed employee expenses have different state treatments — check MA instructions or I can look up the current MA treatment for you if you’d like. Also, MA has a flat income tax rate and different deductions/exemptions.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — Individual Income Tax Return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (if you take the student loan interest deduction or other adjustments).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report your $8,900 business income and business expenses, including home office).
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (compute SE tax on your net self-employment income).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual-expense method for the home office). If you use the simplified home office method, you do NOT file Form 8829, but still enter the home office deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — only if you decide to itemize deductions.
- Keep and attach any 1099s/1098s as required (1099-INT for interest; 1098-E for student loan interest; 1099-NEC if any of the clients issued it).

Massachusetts state
- Massachusetts Form 1 (resident return), plus any state schedules required for deductions/credits.

Other items to watch
- Keep good records: receipts for charitable gifts, business receipts and invoices for graphic design, 1099s, 1098-E, bank statements for interest, rent and utility bills for home office allocation, and any documentation for unreimbursed employee expenses (for MA review).
- Estimated taxes: if you expect to owe tax (due to self-employment tax or insufficient withholding), you may need to make estimated quarterly payments. With $8,900 gross self-employment income you will owe some SE tax; whether you need to make estimated payments depends on your total withholding and expected tax liability.
- Half of your SE tax is deductible above the line — remember that reduces AGI.

Next steps / offer
- If you want, I can:
  - Compute a draft federal tax picture using the exact standard deduction amount for the tax year and giving a side-by-side of standard vs. itemized if you provide state tax withheld and any mortgage interest/property tax.
  - Prepare a checklist of documents to gather for filing.
  - Explain how to enter the home office deduction on Schedule C (actual vs simplified) and show the computations.

Which of those would you like me to do next? If you want a dollar-by-dollar recommendation now, please give: MA state tax withheld (from W-2), any mortgage interest paid (Form 1098), and property tax paid in 2024 (if any).

