Thanks — this is good detail. Below I’ll (1) explain what’s deductible, (2) show how to treat the home office (with quick math), (3) tell you whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, (4) list the federal (and a note about MA) forms you’ll need, and (5) ask a couple quick clarifying questions that matter for the answers.

Short summary / bottom line
- You’ll very likely take the federal standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (standard deduction is much larger than your likely itemized deductions).  
- For your graphic-design income, report it on Schedule C and you can claim a home-office deduction (regular or simplified) and ordinary business expenses there; self-employment tax (Schedule SE) applies and half of that is an above-the-line deduction.  
- Student loan interest (if paid in the 2024 tax year and you meet the income limits) is an adjustment to income (up to $2,500) whether or not you itemize.  
- Charitable donations are deductible only if you itemize (and you’ll need the receipt/acknowledgement for cash donations of $250 or more).

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- W-2 wages: report on Form 1040 (no deduction — wages are income).  
- Self‑employment income (graphic design): report gross income and deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. That includes:
  - Home-office deduction (see next section) if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
  - Business supplies, software, advertising, internet portion, client expenses, etc. (the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” — see Qs below — are deductible on Schedule C only if they are business expenses, not unreimbursed employee expenses).
- Self-employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (approx. 15.3%) on net Schedule C profit; half of the SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1 (an adjustment to income).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 as an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1 if you (a) paid the interest in the tax year and (b) your modified adjusted gross income is below the phaseout threshold. You mentioned $4,800 — only the interest paid in the tax year can be used, and the statutory limit is $2,500.
- Charitable cash gift ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible only if you itemize. For cash gifts of $250 or more, keep a contemporaneous written acknowledgement from the charity (receipt suffices).
- Interest from Marcus ($920) and Charles Schwab ($320): taxable interest income on Form 1040 (these get reported and increase AGI). If total interest/dividend is > $1,500 you’d also file Schedule B; yours totals $1,240 so Schedule B isn’t required.

What you cannot deduct (under current law)
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for your W‑2 job) — these are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 for most employees because of TCJA suspension. If those $890 are business expenses for your self‑employment, they are deductible on Schedule C; if they are employee expenses from your hospital job, they are not deductible.

2) Home office — which method to use and quick math
You can claim a home office deduction only for the self-employed portion (your graphic-design work) if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business and is your principal place of business.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775.
- Regular method: allocate actual expenses by business % of home.

Your numbers:
- Office size: 155 sq ft; home size: 780 sq ft → business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87% (≈19.9%).
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Business portion of rent ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,010.
- On the regular method you would also pro-rate utilities, renters insurance, maybe internet (business portion), etc. Add those prorated amounts to the rent portion for the total home-office deduction.

Notes/limits:
- The regular method will likely give you a larger deduction here (≈$5,010 just from rent allocation vs $775 simplified). Use Form 8829 to compute and carry forward any unallowed deductions.
- Home‑office deductions reduce your Schedule C net income (so they reduce income tax and SE tax base).
- If net profit from the business is very small, some home-office amounts may be limited and could carry forward — Form 8829 handles limitations.

3) Itemize vs. standard deduction (federal)
- For 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is large (about $29,200). Based on the items you listed:
  - Charitable cash: $1,400
  - You might have state income tax withheld or property taxes (SALT) but SALT cap is $10,000.
  - Mortgage interest (none mentioned).
Given that, unless you have significant other deductible items (large property tax, big state tax payments, large medical expenses exceeding the AGI threshold, or substantial mortgage interest), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing. Your single charitable gift of $1,400 won’t come close to the standard deduction threshold.

4) Federal forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (main return).  
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax deduction, etc.).  
- Schedule C — profit or loss from business (graphic design). Report gross receipts ($8,900) and all business expenses (home office, supplies, the $890 if related to the business, etc.).  
- Schedule SE — self-employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the regular home‑office method). If you use simplified method, you do not file 8829.  
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) — you may qualify; which form depends on your taxable income level.  
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement (if MOHELA issued one for interest paid over $600). If no 1098-E you can still claim the deduction if you have records.  
- Form 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC — if your clients sent you 1099s for the $8,900; include those amounts on Schedule C.  
- (Schedule B only if total interest/dividends > $1,500 — not required with $1,240 interest).

Massachusetts (state)
- Massachusetts resident return (Form 1). MA starts from federal AGI and then applies state adjustments. Treatment of deductions and the student loan interest adjustment may differ — check MA instructions or ask a tax pro. MA has its own exemptions/standard deduction rules and a SALT cap consideration; in any case, file Form 1 and attach required schedules.

5) Documents to gather
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099-NEC (or records) for graphic-design income; detailed business expense receipts (including the $890 and other business-related receipts).
- 1099-INTs for Marcus and Charles Schwab (interest).
- 1098-E or records showing student loan interest paid in 2024 (dates and amounts).
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills to support the home-office allocation.
- Charity receipt/acknowledgement for the $1,400 gift (you said you have the receipt — good).
- Any estimated-tax payment records (if you made quarterly payments).

6) Questions I need you to answer
- Were the $4,800 in student‑loan interest payments paid in tax year 2024, or are those amounts from earlier years (2016–2020)? Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on the 2024 return.
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” related to your graphic-design self-employment (Schedule C) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job at the hospital? If they’re employee-related, they’re not deductible for federal tax purposes (under current law).
- Are you renting (I assumed $2,100/month rent) or is that mortgage payment? If it’s mortgage, the mortgage interest component is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize (and would change the itemize vs standard decision). If it’s rent, use the rent amount as shown above.
- Did you receive 1099-NEC/1099-MISC for the $8,900 (or did clients pay via 1099-K, etc.)?

7) Other practical notes
- If your net self-employment tax is modest (because business income is only $8,900 minus expenses), the overall additional federal tax from self-employment will likely be modest, but you still must file Schedule C and Schedule SE.
- Consider the QBI (20% deduction) — it can reduce taxable income for your business portion; I can run rough numbers if you want.
- If you expect similar self-employment income during 2025, think about quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties (or increase withholding from your W-2 employer).

If you want, I can:
- Run sample calculations showing estimated federal tax, SE tax, and whether itemizing would ever beat the standard deduction given an assumed state tax amount. Or
- Draft a checklist/email you can send to a tax preparer including the items above. Or
- Walk through completing Schedule C and Form 8829 with your numbers.

Which would you like next?

