Thanks — this is enough to give concrete, practical guidance. Summary first, then details and next steps/questions so I can run exact numbers for you if you want.

Quick summary
- Report all income:
  - W-2 wages: $84,900 (Form W-2 → Form 1040).
  - Self-employment (graphic design) $8,900 → Schedule C (Form 1040).
  - Interest $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 → report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if total taxable interest > $1,500 or other triggers).
- Home office: you can deduct it against the self‑employment income. You can use either:
  - Actual method (preferred here — see example below) using Form 8829, or
  - Simplified method ($5 × 155 sq ft = $775) reported on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest: annual deduction is limited (maximum $2,500) and phases out at higher MAGI — you cannot deduct $4,800 unless that amount is interest paid in one tax year and you meet income limits. The deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1 (Form 1040).
- Charitable gift $1,400: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this on federal (unless special one‑year rules apply; generally they do not now).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: NOT deductible for W-2 employees under current law (TCJA) — unless those $890 are business expenses for your self‑employment work, in which case include them on Schedule C.
- Likely decision: with only $1,400 in charitable gifts and no mortgage interest or big state/property taxes shown, you will probably be better off taking the standard deduction for the federal return. The home‑office deduction still reduces your self‑employment income regardless of whether you itemize for Schedule A.
- Forms you will likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if using actual home‑office method), Schedule A (only if you itemize), and MA state return (Massachusetts Form 1). Also include any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC and 1099‑INTs you received.

Details and calculations to help you decide

1) Income reporting
- W-2 wages → Form 1040, attach W-2 info.
- Self‑employed income $8,900 → Schedule C. Report gross receipts (1099‑NEC if you got them) and subtract allowable business expenses (home office portion, supplies, business portion of phone/internet if applicable, the $890 if it’s business-related, etc.).
- Interest → report on Form 1040; only prepare Schedule B if taxable interest > $1,500 or other triggers.

2) Home office treatment
- You said the space is exclusive and 155 sq ft of a 780 sq ft home: business-use percentage = 155/780 ≈ 19.87% (≈19.9%).
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Business portion of rent (actual method) ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,006.
- Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775.
- Which to pick: the actual method will give you ~ $5,006 just from rent (plus any allocable utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.), so actual method is likely much more favorable here. To use the actual method you’ll use Form 8829 to allocate allowable home expenses to the business. If you rent, you do not take depreciation for the structure, but you still allocate rent and utilities.
- The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit. That in turn reduces your income subject to income tax and to self‑employment tax. Note: if your home‑office deduction creates a net loss on Schedule C, some limits may apply (losses can be subject to at-risk or hobby rules, but with modest income it’s typically fine).

3) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The student loan interest deduction limit is $2,500 per year (check current-year law for any change). Also the deduction phases out as your modified AGI rises (limits for married filing jointly mean higher earners cannot take the deduction).
- Important: the deduction applies to interest paid in the tax year. If $4,800 is interest paid only in 2024, you can only deduct up to the yearly maximum (subject to phaseouts). You should have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid for the year. The student loan interest deduction is taken on Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) of Form 1040.

4) Charitable contributions ($1,400)
- Deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you claim the standard deduction, you cannot deduct it on federal return. Keep the receipt (you have it) — good practice and needed if you itemize.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are unreimbursed W‑2 job expenses → not deductible on federal Form 1040 under current law.
- If they are business expenses for your graphic design work (supplies, software, client travel, etc.), include them on Schedule C and they reduce your self‑employment income.

6) Self‑employment tax and related items
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net earnings from self‑employment. Compute this on Schedule SE. You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (Form 1040).
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction on qualified business income — whether you qualify depends on taxable income and type/level of business income. With relatively small net business income, you are likely to be eligible for a QBI deduction equal to about 20% of your qualified business income (but I can check after we compute net profit).

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Federal standard deduction (married filing jointly) is usually large and for most households with modest charitable gifts and no mortgage interest, the standard deduction will be larger than itemized.
- Your itemizable items shown so far: $1,400 charitable + potentially state income taxes/property taxes up to SALT cap ($10,000). You haven’t listed mortgage interest, substantial medical expenses, or big property taxes, so it’s likely the standard deduction will be larger.
- BUT the home office deduction is NOT an itemized deduction — it reduces Schedule C net income regardless of whether you itemize on Schedule A. So you can still benefit from the home office even if you take the standard deduction.

8) Massachusetts state return
- Massachusetts has different rules and tax forms (Form 1). Some federal adjustments/deductions differ for MA. You’ll need to report W‑2, interest, and business income on the MA return too; I recommend checking MA specifics for student loan interest and other adjustments or consult a state resource/tax preparer.

9) Checklist of documents/forms you should gather
- Form W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for the two graphic design clients (if issued); records of gross receipts if no 1099s.
- Receipts for the $890 expenses and clear notes whether business or employee.
- 1099‑INTs from Marcus and Charles Schwab (or other statements).
- Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing student loan interest paid for the tax year.
- Receipt for $1,400 cash donation to Northwest Harvest.
- Rent statements, utility bills, renter’s insurance (for home‑office allocation).
- Any records of estimated tax payments you made during the year.

10) Forms you’ll likely file (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — student loan interest deduction, half SE tax)
- Schedule C (profit/loss from business)
- Schedule SE (self-employment tax)
- Form 8829 (if you choose actual home-office method)
- Schedule A (only if you decide to itemize)
- Schedule B only if interest > $1,500 or other triggers

