Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly = $29,200) unless you have significant additional itemizable amounts (large state/local taxes paid, big medical expenses, or mortgage interest) you haven't listed.
- Key deductions you do have access to regardless of itemizing: student‑loan interest (above‑the‑line, up to limits) and ordinary business deductions for your self‑employment income (including a home‑office deduction if you qualify).
- You’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and (depending on the method) either Form 8829 (actual home‑office method) or the simplified home‑office calculation on Schedule C. If you itemize, you’d use Schedule A. You may also need Schedule B (if interest > $1,500) and Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the QBI deduction.

Detailed breakdown and steps

1) Income you must report
- W-2 wages from Riverside Hospital: $84,900 (report on Form 1040).
- Self‑employment / freelance graphic design: $8,900 (report on Schedule C).
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 (report as interest income on Form 1040; Schedule B only required if interest and ordinary dividends total > $1,500).

2) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Self‑employment income is taxable for income tax and self‑employment (SE) tax. You’ll compute net profit on Schedule C (gross income less business expenses). Then use Schedule SE to calculate Social Security & Medicare (SE) tax.
- You may deduct half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income (an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Form 1040 / Schedule 1).
- Business expenses: you can deduct ordinary and necessary expenses related to your graphic design work (software, supplies, advertising, a portion of phone/internet used for business, 1099/recordkeeping). Clarify the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” — if those are business expenses for the freelance work, they belong on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, those generally are NOT deductible for most taxpayers (suspended under current law).

3) Home‑office deduction — how to treat it
You said: home office = 155 sq ft exclusive; total home 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month ($25,200/year).
- The space appears to meet the basic tests: regular and exclusive business use and principal place of business (required for the deduction). This applies only to self‑employment (Schedule C). W‑2 employees generally cannot take a home office deduction now.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that = 155 × $5 = $775. You claim this directly on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
  b) Actual method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, maintenance, depreciation for owners, etc.). Your allocation percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9% (~20%).
     - Example (rent only): 25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,010 of rent attributable to the office, plus allocable utilities, etc.
     - Important limit: the allowable home‑office deduction cannot exceed the net profit from that business (before the home‑office deduction). Any disallowed (excess) actual home‑office deduction may be carried forward in certain circumstances.
- Given your relatively small freelance income ($8,900) and the large rent, actual method rent portion alone (~$5,000) may be limited by net profit. So the simplified method is often simpler and may be preferable here — it gives $775 that you can use without triggering complex carryforward rules or depreciation recapture. If you want to try the actual method you must keep detailed records (rent, utilities, insurance) and use Form 8829 to compute the allowable deduction.

4) Student‑loan interest ($4,800 stated)
- The student‑loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (you claim it even if you take the standard deduction) up to $2,500 per return, subject to income phaseouts. You cannot deduct $4,800 in full in a single tax year — the maximum deduction is $2,500 for the tax year, if you meet the income limits.
- You need Form 1098‑E (or your loan servicer statement) showing interest paid during the tax year. If your modified AGI for MFJ is below the phaseout start (for 2024 the phaseout thresholds apply — given your stated income, you will likely be under the phaseout and eligible for the full $2,500, but we should compute AGI precisely to be certain).

5) Charitable contribution $1,400
- Cash donations to qualified charities are deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). You must have the receipt you mentioned.
- If you take the standard deduction, you do not separately deduct cash gifts (except for a special rule that applied in some pandemic years; not generally applicable for 2024). So this $1,400 only helps if your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction threshold.

6) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If these are business expenses for your freelance work, include on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they’re generally not deductible now.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction — which to choose?
- 2024 standard deduction (MFJ): $29,200.
- Your explicit itemizable amount shown here is $1,400 charity — well below the standard deduction. Student‑loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction and would not affect the itemize vs standard decision.
- Unless you have significant mortgage interest, large medical expenses that exceed 7.5% of AGI, or large state and local taxes (SALT) approaching the $10,000 cap, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger. In short: likely take the standard deduction.

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a qualified business income (QBI) deduction (generally up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C). Eligibility and amount depend on taxable income and the type of business; with relatively modest taxable income you will most likely qualify for some or all of the 20% QBI deduction. This is calculated on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A). We can estimate this if you want.

9) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments to income such as student‑loan interest — many preparers still show these on Schedule 1).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report freelance income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax).
- If using the actual home‑office method: Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). If using simplified method, report directly on Schedule C (no 8829).
- If itemizing: Schedule A (Itemized Deductions).
- If interest + dividends > $1,500: Schedule B.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A for QBI deduction (if applicable).
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) and any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for freelance income (if you received them), 1099‑INTs for interest, and your W‑2.

10) Massachusetts state return
- You will also file a Massachusetts individual return (Form 1). MA has its own rules for deductions and credits (and may not follow federal treatment exactly). Keep state tax withheld amounts from your W‑2 and any state tax paid records. If you want, I can outline MA specifics once you provide the state tax withheld and other state items.

11) Practical next steps I recommend
- Confirm whether the $890 is business expense (Schedule C) or unreimbursed W‑2 employee expense.
- Confirm the actual student loan interest you paid during the 2024 tax year (and whether you received 1098‑E).
- Decide whether you want to use the simplified home‑office method ($775) or collect rent + utility bills and other home expenses if you want to try the actual method (I can help estimate whether the actual method would be limited by business net profit).
- Collect: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INTs, 1098‑E, donation receipts, and records of rent/utilities/home expenses for 2024.
- If you want, I can run rough calculations (AGI, taxable income, self‑employment tax, likely federal tax owed/refund, QBI estimate) if you confirm the items above and tell me whether $890 is business expense and the exact student‑loan interest paid in 2024.

Caveat
I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent. This is general guidance based on typical 2024 rules. For complex situations (home‑office carryforwards, QBI limits, state specifics, or if you want to minimize tax strategies), consider consulting a tax professional or using a reputable tax software / preparer to prepare and review your return.

