Short answer up front
- You should report the $8,900 freelance (graphic design) on Schedule C and can take a home‑office deduction for the self‑employment work. That also triggers Schedule SE (self‑employment tax) and an adjustment for half of the SE tax.
- Student loan interest is an “above the line” adjustment but the maximum deductible amount is $2,500 (not $4,800); you can only deduct up to $2,500 subject to income phaseouts.
- Your $1,400 cash gift to the food bank is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- The $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are deductible only if they were business expenses for your freelance work (put them on Schedule C). If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible (suspended under current law).
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (2024 standard deduction = $29,200) unless your total itemizable deductions (charity + SALT up to $10k + mortgage interest, large medical >7.5% AGI, etc.) exceed $29,200.

Details and how to treat each item
1) W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital $84,900)
- Report on Form 1040 from the W‑2 as usual.

2) Self‑employment income ($8,900)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including business supplies, software, a portion of the $890 if those are business expenses, client expenses, fees, mileage, etc.).
- If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those amounts; even if you didn’t get a 1099, you still report the income.

3) Home office
- You can claim a home‑office deduction only for the self‑employment work. It is not available for W‑2 employee work in almost all cases.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.
  b) Actual method: allocate actual home expenses by business percentage (business % = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%). For example, rent $2,100/month = $25,200/year × 19.87% ≈ $5,007. You could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by that same percentage. (Form 8829 is used to compute the deduction if you use the actual method.)
- Use whichever method gives the larger deduction, but with the actual method you must have records (rent, utilities, etc.). Note the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business for the year (excess may have special carryover rules).

4) Student loan interest ($4,800 paid)
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 per year (adjustment on Form 1040). There is an income phaseout for married filing jointly (if your modified adjusted gross income is above the phaseout range you may lose part or all of this deduction). With your reported income (W‑2 + freelance + interest) you’re likely under the phaseout threshold and should get the full $2,500.

5) Charitable contribution ($1,400)
- Deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction ($29,200), this $1,400 does not give extra tax benefit.

6) Interest income ($920 + $320 = $1,240)
- Taxable interest; report on Form 1040. Schedule B is only required if total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 or other conditions apply. Your total is under $1,500, so Schedule B likely isn’t needed.

7) The $890 “unreimbursed expenses”
- If these are expenses related to your freelance graphic design (supplies, software, client travel, etc.), they belong on Schedule C and reduce the self‑employment net income.
- If they are unreimbursed expenses from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.

8) State and local taxes (SALT)
- You can deduct state income tax and property tax on Schedule A but deduction is limited to $10,000 total for SALT (Married filing jointly). This limit often makes it unlikely to itemize solely for SALT unless you have large mortgage interest or big charitable/medical expenses.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) = $29,200.
- Add up your itemizable expenses (charity $1,400 + SALT up to $10,000 + mortgage interest if any + large medical >7.5% of AGI). If that total > $29,200, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.
- Based on the items you listed (charity $1,400 and likely SALT much less than $29,200 unless you have significant state income tax withholding + property tax), you will most likely take the standard deduction.

Forms you will need (common set for this situation)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as student loan interest deduction and the deductible half of self‑employment tax (reported from Schedule SE)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report freelance income and business expenses (including home‑office deduction or include the result of Form 8829)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — calculate self‑employment tax and the deductible half of that tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for the home‑office deduction. If you use the simplified method, you do NOT need Form 8829; enter the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize deductions
- Schedule B — only if your taxable interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (not required here based on the amounts you gave)

Other practical notes
- The home‑office deduction can materially reduce taxable self‑employment income here — if you use the actual method, your rent allocation (~$5,007) is larger than the simplified $775. But remember home‑office deductions cannot exceed the net profit from that business; if your net business profit is small, the deduction may be limited.
- You will owe self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on net self‑employment income; half of that SE tax is deductible above the line.
- Keep receipts and good records for the home office, charitable donation receipt, student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E from MOHELA), and any business expenses.
- If you expect similar freelance income in future years, you should consider making quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

Quick checklist I recommend you confirm / send me if you want a tighter estimate
- Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses for your freelance business? If yes, give a short list of what they were and totals.
- Do you have any mortgage interest or property taxes in 2024 (do you own a home)?
- How much state income tax was withheld or paid in 2024 (from W‑2 or estimated payments)?
- Any other large deductible expenses (medical >7.5% AGI, big charitable gifts, etc.)?

If you want, I can:
- Estimate your federal tax liability both ways (standard deduction vs itemizing) using the numbers you’ve given and reasonable assumptions, or
- Draft a checklist email you can send to a CPA with the items they’ll need.

