Thanks — I can walk you through this step‑by‑step and give concrete estimates so you can decide whether to itemize and how to report everything.

Quick summary up front
- Most likely you should take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) because your itemizable deductions shown here are small.
- Your graphic‑design income is self‑employment income and should be reported on Schedule C; that lets you claim a home‑office deduction (either actual or simplified) and ordinary business expenses, and it creates a self‑employment tax liability (Schedule SE).
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (Schedule 1) up to the allowed limit (generally $2,500; eligibility phases out at higher MAGI — at your estimated income you should be eligible for the full amount).
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1, and possibly Form 8995 (QBI) if you want the Qualified Business Income deduction. For Massachusetts, file MA Form 1.

Detailed walkthrough and calculations

1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital: $84,900 (reported on Form 1040).
- Self‑employment (graphic design) gross: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (you’ll subtract business expenses there).
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Charles Schwab) = $1,240 — report as interest income on Form 1040 (Schedule B is not required unless total interest > $1,500).

2) Home‑office deduction (graphic‑design business)
You gave:
- Home size = 780 sq ft
- Home office = 155 sq ft (exclusive use)
- Rent = $2,100/month → annual rent = $25,200

Business‑use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.
- Actual method (your rent only, assuming you don’t allocate utilities here): business percentage × rent = 19.9% × $25,200 ≈ $5,005.

If you have other home costs (utilities, renter’s insurance, internet portion), those can increase the actual method amount. For a renter, depreciation is generally not available (that’s for owners). Because the actual method on rent alone gives a much larger deduction (~$5,005) vs simplified ($775), the actual method will likely be better for you — as long as you keep documentation of the rent and how you computed square footage.

Note: The home‑office deduction is taken on Schedule C as a business expense and reduces your net business profit.

3) Business expenses and unreimbursed expenses
- If the $890 in “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your graphic design work (supplies, software, client costs, travel for clients, etc.), include them on Schedule C. They reduce net profit and lower self‑employment tax and income tax.
- If the $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, those are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended under current law) for most employees.

4) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- After allowable Schedule C expenses (including home office and the $890 if business expenses), your net self‑employment income is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings). You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1).
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income deduction (up to 20% of QBI) — that’s computed after you determine net Schedule C profit. With your approximate income, you’re likely below the threshold where complex limits apply, so you probably qualify for at least some QBI deduction. Form 8995 (simple) or 8995‑A (complex) is used.

5) Student loan interest
- You reported $4,800 paid in student loan interest (MOHELA for 2016–2020, but you said you paid $4,800 — I assume that’s 2024 payments). The student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 per year (and phases out at higher incomes). Given your approximate total income (~$95k), you will likely be eligible for the full $2,500 (verify MAGI thresholds). This deduction is an adjustment on Schedule 1, not an itemized deduction.

6) Charitable contribution
- $1,400 to Northwest Harvest (you have the receipt) — charitable contribution is an itemized deduction on Schedule A. It’s deductible if you itemize (or if you qualify for any special above‑the‑line charitable rules but those are limited).

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- You gave $1,400 charitable and no mortgage interest or large state/local tax detail (you didn’t list SALT, mortgage interest, or large medical expenses). That makes your itemized deductions small.
- The standard deduction for married filing jointly (2024 tax year) is substantially larger than $1,400, so in almost all cases with numbers like these you will be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: Likely take the standard deduction. Itemize only if you have additional deductible items (large mortgage interest, SALT up to $10k cap, large unreimbursed medical expenses > 7.5% of AGI, or other itemizable items you haven’t listed).

8) Forms you will use (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments: student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your graphic design income and business expenses, take the home office deduction here
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and half‑SE deduction
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (Qualified Business Income deduction) — if you claim QBI
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (probably not)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received any for your freelance work) and 1098‑E (student loan interest statement from MOHELA), 1099‑INT for interest income

State filing
- Massachusetts Form 1 (MA resident return). MA has its own rules for deductions and taxable income — student loan interest and federal itemizations can differ. Check MA instructions or consult a tax preparer for MA specifics.

9) Documents to collect
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099‑NEC / records (invoices, bank deposits) for the $8,900 freelance income
- Records/receipts for the $890 business expenses (or note if those are employee-related)
- Rent receipts/lease and documentation of square footage for home office
- 1098‑E (student loan interest) or statements from MOHELA showing interest paid
- Charitable donation receipt(s)
- 1099‑INTs from Marcus and Charles Schwab (or statements)

10) Estimated tax / withholding
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and additional income tax. If not enough is withheld from your W‑2 (or you don’t make estimated payments during the year), you could owe taxes and possibly underpayment penalties. Consider making quarterly estimated payments if your freelance income continues.

Illustrative quick math (rough)
