- Interest: $920 (Marcus) and $320 (Charles Schwab) — report total interest income on Form 1040. If your total taxable interest & ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 or you meet other conditions, you also file Schedule B (but your amounts are below that threshold).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment income; calculate on Schedule SE. Half of your SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction (adjustment to income).

Deductions and where they go
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year (2024) is eligible. The student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 (subject to income phase‑outs and eligibility rules). You should have a Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for interest paid in the year. If the $4,800 you listed is interest paid across multiple past years, only 2024 interest counts for your 2024 return. This deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1), so you do not have to itemize to claim it — but it phases out at higher MAGI levels.
- Charitable gift $1,400 to Northwest Harvest: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). A qualified food bank is typically an eligible charity. Keep the receipt.
- Unreimbursed expenses $890: determine whether these were business expenses (related to your graphic design work). If they are business expenses, report them on Schedule C and they reduce self‑employment net income. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job, those are not deductible on the federal return for tax years since the TCJA (2018–2025), unless you’re in a specific category of employee (very rare).
