Summary (high level)
- Report all income: W-2 wages ($84,900), self-employment income ($8,900) on Schedule C, and interest ($920 + $320 = $1,240) on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if total interest/dividends > $1,500 or other triggers).
- Home office is deductible for self-employment income (you can use either the actual-expense method or the simplified method).
- Student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment (up to $2,500 limit, subject to income phaseouts) — you won’t deduct the full $4,800 if that $4,800 is interest paid in the tax year; the limit is $2,500 and phaseouts may reduce it.
- Charitable gifts ($1,400) are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- “Unreimbursed employee expenses” (the $890) are generally not deductible for most employees because miscellaneous itemized deductions have been suspended (TCJA) through 2025, unless you’re in a specific category that still allows them (e.g., certain reservists, performing artists, etc).
- You’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment earnings and can deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction on your net Schedule C income (rules and phaseouts apply).

Home office — specifics and quick math
You said the home office is 155 sq ft exclusive, total home is 780 sq ft, rent $2,100/month.

- Home office percent = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200.
- Actual-expense deduction (rent portion only, not counting utilities, insurance, etc): 25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,005.
- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775.

Because your actual rent portion (~$5,005) is much larger than the simplified-method amount ($775), the actual-expense method likely gives a bigger deduction. Under the actual method you can also allocate related utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Use Form 8829 (or follow Schedule C instructions) to calculate and report the deduction. The home office deduction is a business expense on Schedule C and reduces your net self-employment profit (and thus SE tax).

Self-employment treatment
- File Schedule C to report the $8,900 gross receipts and deduct ordinary & necessary business expenses (home office portion of rent, any supplies, software, marketing, etc.).
- If net profit remains positive, compute self-employment tax on Schedule SE (approx. 15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit). You then deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- If your business expenses (including home office) exceed business income, you’ll show a net loss on Schedule C which offsets other income (subject to hobby/business rules and at-risk/passive activity rules in certain situations).

Student loan interest
- Up to $2,500 of student loan interest can be claimed as an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (you do not have to itemize). There are income phaseouts — if your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) is above certain thresholds the deduction is reduced or eliminated. Provide your MAGI if you want me to check phaseout.

Charitable donations
- $1,400 cash donation is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If your total itemized deductions (charity + state and local taxes + mortgage interest + qualifying medical expenses > 7.5% of AGI, etc.) exceed the standard deduction, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.

Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Generally not deductible for most employees under current law (TCJA) through 2025. So your $890 is likely nondeductible unless you qualify under a special exception.

Interest income
- Report $1,240 interest on Form 1040. You only need Schedule B if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 or other triggers (e.g., foreign accounts).

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- That depends on the total of your itemizable amounts. Typical itemizable things are: state & local income tax paid (or sales tax), property tax, mortgage interest, charitable gifts, large medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI.
- From the items you gave: charity $1,400 looks small relative to the married-filing-joint standard deduction. Unless you have sizable state income tax or property tax payments or other large deductible items you probably will take the standard deduction. (If you give me the amount of state tax withheld on your W-2 and any other deductible items, I can compute which is better.)

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax show up as adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report graphic design income and business expenses (including home office)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for the home office (if you use simplified method, you just enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — only if interest + dividends exceed $1,500 or other triggers
- Keep 1099-NEC (if you received them) and 1099-INT or statements from Marcus and Charles Schwab (report interest). MOHELA should issue Form 1098‑E if you paid student loan interest in the year.

Massachusetts state filing
- You’ll need to file MA Form 1 (Massachusetts resident return) and report your W-2 wages, business income, and interest. MA has its own rules for deductions and exemptions; self-employment income is taxable in MA (SE tax is federal only). If you’d like, I can summarize MA-specific credits/deductions once you confirm the tax year.

