Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical picture. I’ll cover (1) what you can/don’t can deduct, (2) whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office, (4) what federal forms you’ll need, and (5) a short checklist and next steps. I’ll also flag places where I need one quick clarification.

High‑level summary / recommendation
- For federal taxes you will almost certainly take the standard deduction (married filing jointly). With the amounts you listed, your itemizable expenses (charity $1,400 plus any state/local taxes or mortgage interest you haven’t listed) are very unlikely to exceed the 2024 standard deduction — so standard deduction is probably best.
- Report your graphic design income on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). Claim the home‑office deduction on Schedule C — with 155 sq ft of exclusive use in a 780 sq ft home, the actual‑expense method (allocating part of your rent) will likely give a substantially larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Student‑loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (adjustment to income) but limited to $2,500 maximum and subject to income phaseouts. You’ll need Form 1098‑E (from MOHELA) showing interest paid in the tax year; only interest paid in the tax year is deductible.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $890) are not deductible on your federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension), so they won’t reduce federal taxable income (there may be limited state rules — see MA notes below).

Details

1) Income reporting
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: report on Form 1040 (you’ll get a W‑2).
- Graphic design income ($8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those amounts; if not, you still must report the income.
- Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): report on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if required — Schedule B is required if you have over $1,500 of taxable interest or certain foreign accounts; you’re under that threshold so you can generally enter interest directly on Form 1040).

2) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C profit. You calculate SE tax on Schedule SE and can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income (on Schedule 1).
- You can also deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (software, supplies, advertising, professional fees, portions of utilities/rent attributable to the home office, etc.).

3) Home office treatment
- You meet the basic rules if the 155 sq ft area is used regularly and exclusively for your graphic‑design business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775 deduction.
  b) Actual (regular) method: allocate actual home expenses by percentage of home used for business. Your business‑use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%.
     - You can deduct the business share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Example: rent $2,100/month = $25,200/yr × 19.9% ≈ $5,010 of rent allocated to the business (plus shares of utilities, internet, etc.). That is likely much larger than the simplified amount.
- For self‑employed people, if you use the actual method you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and transfer the allowable deduction to Schedule C. For the simplified method you just compute the amount and enter it on Schedule C without Form 8829.
- Recommendation: use the actual method here (because ~$5,000 from rent allocation vs $775 simplified), but keep good records and use Form 8829 if you do the actual method.

4) Student loan interest
- Deduction is for interest you actually paid in the tax year (you’ll receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA if they reported it).
- Maximum federal deduction per return is $2,500 (even if you paid more).
- There is an income phaseout for married filing jointly — for 2023 the phaseout started around $145k MAGI (check current 2024 thresholds if close to those amounts). Based on your income here you’re likely under the threshold and can claim the allowed amount (up to $2,500).
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1), so it reduces AGI even if you take the standard deduction.

5) Charitable contribution
- $1,400 to Northwest Harvest is an itemized deduction on Schedule A if you itemize. It does not reduce your federal tax if you take the standard deduction.
- Keep the receipt and the organization’s EIN if possible.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (the $890) are not deductible federally for most employees in tax years 2018–2025. They are not reported on Schedule A.
- Check MA rules separately — some states still allow different treatment.

7) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule C (self‑employment income/expenses for graphic design).
- Schedule SE (self‑employment tax).
- Form 8829 (if using actual home‑office method).
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student‑loan interest and deduction for half SE tax).
- Schedule A (only if you choose to itemize — likely you will not).
- Form(s) 1098‑E (student loan interest), 1099‑INT (interest), any 1099‑NEC if you received them (you still report income even if no 1099).
- Keep W‑2 for Riverside Hospital.

8) Massachusetts (state) notes
- You’ll file MA Form 1 (Massachusetts resident return). MA has its own rules on deductions and exemptions. Some things (e.g., student loan interest) may be treated differently — check MA instructions or ask if you want me to look into MA specifics. If you paid significant state/local taxes or have property tax, that could affect itemizing on your MA return but generally does not change the federal decision here.

9) Documentation to keep
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099‑NEC / business income records and receipts for expenses.
- Proof of rent paid and proof that the claimed home office area is exclusive (floor plan, photos) and the computation showing 155/780.
- Receipts for the $1,400 donation.
- Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for whatever interest was paid in the tax year.
- 1099‑INTs from Marcus and Schwab.
- Records supporting the $890 unreimbursed expenses (for state purposes or future questions).

10) Quick estimated numbers (illustrative)
- Business gross: $8,900.
- If you take actual home‑office rent allocation ≈ $5,010 you’d have net business income ≈ $3,890 (before other business expenses).
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% of ~92.35% of net → roughly $550 (very rough).
- Half of SE tax deductible (~$275) as adjustment.
- Student loan interest deduction limited to $2,500 (even if you paid $4,800).
- With W‑2 $84,900 + net business ~$3,890 + interest $1,240 → total income ≈ $90,030 before above‑the‑line adjustments; after half SE tax and student loan interest your AGI will drop (see above).

Next steps / questions for you
