Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll likely need. I’ll flag places where I need more numbers if you want a tighter estimate.

Summary (short)
- Report your $8,900 graphic-design income on Schedule C (self‑employment). That income is taxable and subject to self‑employment tax.
- You can take a home‑office deduction for the 155 sq ft exclusive business space (either simplified or actual method) on Schedule C. If you use the actual-expenses method you generally file Form 8829.
- Student loan interest: you paid $4,800 but the federal student-loan interest adjustment max is $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts) — you’ll likely be eligible for the full $2,500 given the income numbers you provided.
- Charitable gift ($1,400) is deductible only if you itemize. With the MFJ standard deduction for 2024 (~$29,200), you will most likely take the standard deduction unless you have substantially more itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state taxes, big charitable donations, etc.).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890) are not deductible on your federal return (suspended under current law for employees).
- Key federal forms: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student loan interest and half of SE tax), and possibly Form 8829 (if using actual home-office expenses). You’ll also report interest income (1099‑INTs) on 1040; report any 1099‑NEC you receive for the design clients.
- State: You’ll file a Massachusetts resident return (MA Form 1). State treatment can differ in details — consider checking MA rules or asking if you want a state-specific walkthrough.

Details and how to handle each item

1) Self‑employment (graphic design) — what to do
- Report your gross self‑employment income ($8,900) on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (examples: software subscriptions, supplies, advertising, business insurance, part of phone/internet if used for business, fees, etc.).
- Self‑employment tax: net profit from Schedule C is subject to SE tax (calculated on Schedule SE). You get to deduct half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

2) Home‑office deduction
You qualify only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business and is your principal place of business.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5 per sq ft (2024 simplified rate) up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that’s 155 * $5 = $775. No Form 8829 required.
- Actual-expense method: compute the business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, part of repairs, etc.). Example just for rent: $2,100/month = $25,200/year × 19.9% ≈ $5,013 deductible as a business expense on Schedule C.
- If you use the actual method you generally must file Form 8829 to compute the deduction (and depreciation if applicable). If you take the simplified method you do not file Form 8829.

Note: For a W‑2 employee, the home office deduction is not available under federal law (except for certain categories like performing artists, etc.). But because you are self‑employed for your design work, you can take it on Schedule C.

3) Student-loan interest ($4,800 paid)
- Federal student-loan interest deduction limit is $2,500 per year (an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1) and is phased out at higher MAGI levels. Based on the income you listed (W-2 $84,900 + self‑employment ~8,900 + interest ~1,240 = ~95k before adjustments) you’re well under phaseout thresholds, so you should be able to deduct up to $2,500.
- You cannot deduct more than $2,500 even if you paid $4,800. Keep your MOHELA statements/1098‑E (if provided) for proof.

4) Charitable contribution ($1,400)
- Deductible only if you itemize. For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is roughly $29,200. With only $1,400 of charitable gifts and no mortgage interest listed, itemizing will almost certainly be smaller than the standard deduction — so you will probably not get extra tax benefit from this donation on your federal return unless you have other substantial itemizable items.
- Keep the donation receipt (you have it) for documentation.

5) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890)
- These are not deductible on the federal return for employees under current law (TCJA suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions through at least 2025). If any of these were expenses directly for your self‑employment work, move them to Schedule C instead.

6) Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240)
- Report this interest on Form 1040 as interest income. Because total interest < $1,500, you generally do not need to file Schedule B. Keep 1099‑INT statements.

7) Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- Based on what you listed (only $1,400 charitable + no mortgage interest, unknown SALT and property tax), the standard deduction for MFJ (~$29,200 for 2024) is almost certainly larger. So you will likely take the standard deduction.
- If you have additional itemizable items you didn’t list (large state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, high charitable gifts, casualty losses, etc.), add them up and compare to the standard deduction.

8) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and the adjustment for half of SE tax flow here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your graphic design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax due
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual-expense method for the home office (not required for the simplified method)
- Possibly Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) if your interest/dividends exceed threshold or you have foreign accounts (does not appear required with the amounts given)
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC/1099‑INT: report these on your return if received (you still report the income even if you didn’t receive a 1099)

9) Massachusetts (state) considerations
- File MA Form 1 as a resident. MA has its own rules and may treat certain items differently (state deductions, credits, itemization rules). If you want, I can walk through MA specifics if you tell me state tax withholding, property taxes or other MA‑specific items.
- Note: Some states allow deductions for unreimbursed employee expenses or have differences in treatment — check MA rules or ask me to look into them.

10) Estimated tax and withholding
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and additional income tax. If you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file, you should consider making estimated tax payments during the year or increasing withholding on your W‑2 job to avoid underpayment penalties. With only $8,900 gross SE income, this may be modest but still worth checking.

Records to keep
- MOHELA statements showing interest paid (and any 1098‑E)
- 1099‑INTs from Marcus and Schwab
- Records/receipts for the $1,400 charity
- Receipts and records for all business expenses, home‑office calculation (floorplan sq ft), rent statements, utilities, insurance if using actual method
- Records of client payments (invoices, bank deposits) and any 1099‑NECs

Would you like any of the following next?
- A rough tax estimate (federal tax owed/refund) using the numbers you gave (I’ll need state withholding and any other deductions/credits).
- I can prepare a checklist of documents to gather for filing.
- A walkthrough of the MA state return specifics.

If you want a quick estimate, tell me: state tax withheld (MA withholding), any mortgage interest or property tax, and whether you prefer simplified or actual home‑office method (and whether you want me to compute both).

