Key facts you gave (I’ll use these in the examples below)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (Boston, MA).  
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $84,900  
- Self-employment income (graphic design): $8,900 (2 clients)  
- Interest income: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240  
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (MOHELA)  
- Charitable cash contributions: $1,400 (Northwest Harvest, receipt in hand)  
- Home: rent $2,100/month for 780 sq ft; exclusive home office 155 sq ft  
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $890 — need to clarify if these are business expenses for the graphic design or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job.

What you can and cannot deduct (federal)
- Student loan interest: Maximum deduction is $2,500 per year (an “above-the-line” adjustment that reduces AGI), subject to income phaseouts. You cannot deduct $4,800 in full — the excess is not deductible. Whether you get the full $2,500 depends on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI); based on the income numbers you gave, you are likely under typical phaseout thresholds, but confirm with your exact MAGI.
- Charitable contributions: $1,400 cash to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no above-the-line deduction for general charitable cash gifts for 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: Not deductible on the federal return for tax years after 2017 (suspended by the TCJA). If the $890 are business expenses used to earn your graphic design income (i.e., you are self-employed and they are ordinary and necessary business expenses), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses from your W-2 job, they are not deductible federally.
- Interest income: Report $1,240 interest on Form 1040. Schedule B is required only if interest + dividends exceed $1,500 (you’re under that threshold).
- Home office: If you are self-employed for the graphic design work, you can claim a home office deduction because you said the 155 sq ft is exclusive use. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775.
  - Actual-expense method (likely better in your case): prorate actual home expenses. Your business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Pro-rated rent portion = 25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,008. (You may also pro-rate utilities/internet if you paid them and include related, allowable home expenses.)
  Because your rent-share (~$5,008) is much larger than the simplified $775, the actual-expense method will likely give you a larger deduction. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use actual method.
- Self-employment tax: Your net self-employment profit (Schedule C) is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security/Medicare). You then get to deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1 of Form 1040.
- State (Massachusetts) treatment: MA has its own rules (some federal adjustments/deductions differ or aren’t allowed). You’ll file MA Form 1. I recommend checking MA DOR guidance or asking a tax pro for state-specific impacts (e.g., whether any adjustments behave differently).

Do you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200. (You only itemize if your total itemized deductions exceed this.)
- Your only listed federal itemizable deduction is $1,400 charitable cash. You did not list mortgage interest or significant property taxes. So based on the items you listed, you should take the standard deduction ($29,200) — itemizing for $1,400 would not help.
- Note: the home-office deduction is NOT an itemized deduction; it’s a business expense on Schedule C, so it does not factor into the Schedule A itemizing decision.

Forms you will typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest up to limit, half of self-employment tax deduction, etc.).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $8,900 self-employment income, and business expenses (home office deduction goes here / Form 8829 if you use actual expenses).
- Form 8829 — if you choose the actual-expense method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you won’t file 8829; report the simplified home office expense directly on Schedule C.
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (unlikely given your numbers).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividends exceed $1,500 (not needed based on $1,240).
- Attach W-2 and any 1099-NEC or 1099-INTs as required.
- Massachusetts: MA Form 1 for the state return.

Quick illustrative math (estimates, round numbers, assumes the $890 are business expenses; if not, see notes below)
- Gross self-employment income: $8,900
- Business expenses: home office (actual) ≈ $5,008 + other business expenses $890 = $5,898
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ 8,900 − 5,898 = $3,002
- Self-employment tax: taxable portion = 92.35% × $3,002 ≈ $2,773. SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 2,773 ≈ $424. (You’d report that on Schedule SE.)
- Deduction for half SE tax ≈ $212 (this is an adjustment on Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: you can deduct up to $2,500 (not $4,800). That reduces AGI (if eligible based on MAGI).
- Standard deduction: $29,200 (MFJ).
- Itemized deductions (your listed item): $1,400 (so standard deduction is better).
This is illustrative — exact numbers will vary with final verified expenses and any other credits/deductions.

Questions I need you to confirm
