Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll summarize what is (likely) deductible, whether you should itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office and self‑employment income, and which IRS forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions are at the end so I can make the numbers exact.

Quick numbers (based on what you gave)
- Gross income: W‑2 $84,900 + freelance $8,900 + interest $920 + $320 = $95,040.
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $29,200.

What you can deduct (and how)
1. Student loan interest
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line), subject to income phaseouts.
- Important: the deduction applies to interest paid in the tax year you’re filing for. Do you mean you actually paid $4,800 in interest during 2024, or are those payments from 2016–2020 that you didn’t previously deduct? If the latter, you cannot deduct those older payments on your 2024 return (you would have had to claim them on those years or amend those returns).
- If you paid $2,500+ in 2024 and your MAGI is below the phaseout threshold for MFJ, you can claim the full $2,500.

2. Self‑employment (graphic design) income ($8,900)
- Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). Business income minus business expenses = net profit (or loss).
- Business expenses: If the $890 you listed are expenses related to your graphic design work (supplies, software, advertising, travel for clients, etc.), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment profit. If the $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible (those miscellaneous itemized deductions were suspended).
- Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) is computed on Schedule SE. You pay SE tax on net earnings from self‑employment. You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

3. Home‑office
- Only available if you’re self‑employed (or a statutory employee). You must use the space regularly and exclusively for business — you said 155 sq ft exclusive so that sounds like it qualifies.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified: $5.00 per square foot (up to 300 sq ft). For 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (easy, no Form 8829).
  b) Actual expense method: percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.). Your business-use percentage = 155 / 780 = 19.87% (≈19.9%).
     - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Home‑office share of rent ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,007.
     - You can also apply the 19.87% to utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. (document everything).
- Important limitation: the home‑office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from the business for the year. It’s limited to the net profit from that business (gross business income minus other business deductions). If the home‑office share would push your business into a loss, the excess home‑office deduction is carried forward until you have net income in a future year (Form 8829 tracks carryforwards).
- Given your numbers, using the actual method on rent alone would reduce your business taxable profit a lot: $8,900 − $5,007 (home office rent share) − $890 (other business expenses, if indeed business expenses) ≈ $3,003 net profit. That means the home‑office amount is allowable up to that limit.

4. Charitable contributions ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest)
- Cash gifts to a qualified charity are deductible if you itemize. You have a receipt — good.
- But keep in mind: if you claim the standard deduction ($29,200), you don’t separately deduct charitable gifts. With the amounts you listed, itemizing will very likely produce a smaller deduction than the standard deduction (see Itemizing vs Standard, below).

5. Interest income ($920 + $320)
- Report bank/broker interest as ordinary income on Form 1040. You should receive 1099‑INT(s) or 1099‑DIV for interest from Schwab. Schedule B is only required if total taxable interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 or there are certain other conditions; your interest total ($1,240) is below $1,500, so Schedule B likely isn’t required.

Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Standard deduction for MFJ (2024): $29,200.
- Likely itemizable deductions you listed: charitable $1,400; possibly state income tax paid (withheld on your W‑2). You didn’t list mortgage interest or large medical expenses.
- Estimation: MA state tax on ~$95k income is roughly in the $4k–$5k range (depends on withholding, deductions), which added to the $1,400 charitable could bring itemized deductions to maybe ~$5k–6k — still well below $29,200. So you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: Take the standard deduction unless you have significant other itemized deductions (large mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses above the AGI threshold, large state & local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, etc.).

Other items to watch
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: you may be eligible for up to a 20% deduction of qualified business income from your self‑employment (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). For your relatively small net profit from the design work, you’d likely get a modest QBI deduction (it’s computed after determining net business income). It’s relatively straightforward at your income level but I can estimate it if you want a number.
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self‑employment income, you owe self‑employment tax and may owe additional income tax. If your W‑2 withholding doesn’t cover your tax liability, you should either make estimated quarterly payments or increase withholding on your W‑2 so you avoid underpayment penalties.

Forms you will need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction, half of self‑employment tax deduction, etc.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — your $8,900 freelance income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for the home office. NOT required for the simplified method.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — QBI deduction (depending on complexity and thresholds)
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (likely you won’t)
- 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (if clients issued them for your freelance income), 1099‑INT (bank/broker interest), W‑2 (Riverside Hospital), and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if applicable.

Records to keep
- W‑2, all 1099s, bank statements, 1098‑E(s)
- Receipts and documentation for business expenses ($890) and any other business costs
- Copies/photos of home‑office measurements, photos showing exclusive business use, rent receipts/lease and utility bills
- Charity receipts for the $1,400 donation
- Any correspondence from MOHELA/loan servicer showing interest paid in the tax year

