Thanks — I can walk you through what’s likely deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few follow-up questions so I can be more precise.

Key assumptions and quick summary
- Tax filing status: Married filing jointly (federal).
- W-2 wages: $84,900.
- Self‑employment (graphic design) gross: $8,900 (you said 2 clients).
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Charles Schwab) = $1,240 (reportable as interest income).
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year for a 780 sq ft home.
- Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive use.
- Charitable gifts: $1,400 (you have receipts).
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $890 — need to confirm whether these were business expenses for the graphic design work (if so, they belong on Schedule C; if they are employee unreimbursed expenses they are not deductible under current law).
- Student loan interest: you wrote $4,800 (MOHELA, 2016–2020). Important: only interest paid in the tax year you’re filing can be deducted on that year’s return. See questions below.

What you can generally deduct (federal)
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 as an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (Schedule 1) if you actually paid interest in the tax year and your modified AGI is below the phaseout thresholds. Based on your income level (W-2 + self-employment + interest ~ ~$95k before adjustments), you’re likely under the phaseout and would qualify — but I need to confirm the actual interest paid in the tax year.
- Business (graphic design) expenses: Report on Schedule C. This includes ordinary and necessary expenses like supplies, software, advertising, business mileage, etc. If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office deduction: Available only for self-employed (Schedule C) for the portion of your home used exclusively and regularly for business. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that’s 155 × $5 = $775.
  - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs) by business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Rent portion alone = 19.9% × $25,200 ≈ $5,005 (plus prorated utilities etc.). The regular method is much more favorable here because of your relatively large home-office share and high rent.
  - Note: you must meet the exclusive and regular use test and be able to substantiate calculations/expenses.
- Self‑employment tax: Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment tax (Schedule SE). You may deduct one-half of self-employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: You may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income (Form 8995 or 8995-A) depending on taxable income and type of business. With relatively small net business income, you’re probably eligible for some QBI benefit — but that’s a bit technical and depends on your final taxable income and wages/expenses.

What you cannot deduct (under current law, generally)
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (the miscellaneous itemized deductions suspended through 2025). If those $890 are employee expenses for a W-2 job, they’re not deductible.
- Charitable contribution of $1,400 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct that gift on your federal return.

Standard deduction vs itemize (federal)
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly (2024): $29,200.
- Likely itemizable items you listed: charitable $1,400 + state and local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 (I don’t have your exact state tax or property tax amounts) + any mortgage interest (you didn’t list any). With only $1,400 in charitable donations and unknown SALT/mortgage interest, it’s likely the standard deduction will be larger — so you’ll probably take the standard deduction.
- Important: the home office deduction is a business deduction (on Schedule C) and does not affect the choice to itemize or take the standard deduction for Schedule A — you can take the standard deduction and still claim the home office.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — to report student loan interest deduction (above-the-line) and half of self-employment tax.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $8,900 gross and business expenses (including home office deduction if self-employed).
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax.
- Form 8995 (or Form 8995-A) — for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (if applicable).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (seems unlikely based on amounts you provided).
- Form 1098-E — student loan servicers (MOHELA) typically issue this to show interest paid; you’ll need that for the student loan deduction.
- Schedule B — only required if you have over $1,500 in interest income or certain other conditions (your combined interest is $1,240, so Schedule B likely not required, but still report the interest on 1040).
- Massachusetts state return (MA Form 1) — state rules differ; student loan interest and other adjustments may be handled differently at the state level.

Illustrative rough numbers (to show impact — please confirm actuals)
Assumptions: $8,900 business income, $890 business expenses, use regular home office method (rent portion ≈ $5,005), interest $1,240.
- Schedule C gross: $8,900
- Less business expenses: $890
- Less home office (regular): $5,005
→ Schedule C net business income ≈ $3,005
- Self-employment tax (approx 15.3% of $3,005) ≈ $460 → half deductible ≈ $230
- Add to W-2 84,900 + interest 1,240 + net business 3,005 = total income ≈ $89,145
- Subtract half SE ≈ 230 → AGI before student loan interest ≈ $88,915
- Student loan interest: if you paid interest in the year, you can deduct up to $2,500 (but only the actual interest paid, and subject to phaseouts). If you qualify and claim full $2,500, AGI ≈ $86,415.
- Standard deduction (MFJ) $29,200 → taxable income ≈ $57,215 (very rough).

If you used the simplified home office instead ($775):
- Schedule C net ≈ 8,900 - 890 - 775 = 7,235, SE tax higher, AGI higher. Regular method looks better in your case because of the rent amount.

Follow-up questions I need to be exact
1. The $4,800 student loan interest — was that interest you paid in the tax year you’re filing (2024)? Or are those older totals (2016–2020)? Only interest paid during the tax year is deductible. Also, do you have a 1098-E from MOHELA for that year?
2. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your graphic design work? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W-2 job?
3. Do you have any mortgage interest, property tax payments, or other big deductible items that would affect itemizing? Also, how much state income tax was withheld or paid (or property tax) in the year? SALT is capped at $10,000.
4. Do you pay utilities and renter’s insurance separately (helpful to compute the regular home-office allocation)? Any repairs or direct office expenses that were solely for the office?
5. Did you receive any 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC from clients? (Either way, you still must report the income.)

Recommended next steps
- If the $890 are business expenses and you paid rent/utilities, plan to use the regular home-office method — it likely gives you a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Gather documentation: W-2, 1098-E (student loan interest), client 1099s (if any), bank statements showing interest income, receipts for charitable giving, receipts/invoices for business expenses, monthly rent and utility amounts, and notes showing exclusive use of home office.
- If you want, I can run a more precise estimate if you provide: actual student interest paid in the tax year, confirmation whether $890 is business-related, amounts paid in state income tax and property tax, and whether you have mortgage interest.

If you’d like, I can prepare a short checklist of documents to gather before you file or help you compute exact numbers if you share the answers to the follow-ups above. Which would you prefer?

