Thanks — I can walk through what’s likely deductible, how the home‑office works, whether you should itemize, and which federal (and MA) forms you’ll typically need. I’ll also call out a few questions I need to give precise answers.

Summary up front
- Most likely you’ll take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($29,200) — unless you have a lot more itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, big charitable gifts, etc.). From the items you listed, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than your federal itemized deductions.
- Your 8,900 self‑employment (graphic design) income is reported on Schedule C and can use a home‑office deduction (since it’s self‑employment income). That deduction reduces your Schedule C profit and your self‑employment tax.
- Interest from Marcus/Schwab ($920 + $320 = $1,240) is taxable interest income and goes on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest is only deductible for interest actually paid in the tax year (and limited to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). Interest you paid in 2016–2020 cannot be claimed on your 2024 return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible on your federal return (suspended through 2025). If the $890 is self‑employment business expense, it belongs on Schedule C and is deductible there.

Details and how each item is treated

1) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $29,200.
- Itemize on Schedule A only if your total itemized deductions exceed $29,200.
- From what you listed, your likely itemizable items for Schedule A are the $1,400 charitable gift and any state/local income taxes or property taxes you paid in 2024. You didn’t list mortgage interest or large SALT items, so it’s likely the standard deduction is larger. Note: home office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it’s a business deduction on Schedule C (so it doesn’t affect the “itemize vs standard” decision).

2) Self‑employment income ($8,900, graphic design)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (software, supplies, internet portion, advertising, mileage, etc.). If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the graphic design work, put them on Schedule C.
- You will owe self‑employment tax on net Schedule C profit; compute with Schedule SE. You get an “above‑the‑line” deduction for half of your self‑employment tax on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

3) Home office (155 sq ft exclusively; home 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/mo)
- You qualify for the home‑office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 for 2024 (cap 300 sq ft).
  b) Actual expense method: allocate business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. If rent is $2,100/mo → $25,200/year; home‑office share ≈ 19.87% × $25,200 ≈ $5,004. You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., using that percentage. The actual method often gives a bigger deduction for renters if rent is high.
- If you use the actual method, use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the deduction and attach it to Schedule C. The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus income subject to income tax and self‑employment tax).
- Reminder: employees cannot claim home office on 1040 for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended). This deduction is available only because you’re self‑employed.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800 MOHELA, 2016–2020)
- Only student loan interest you actually paid during 2024 can be claimed on your 2024 return, up to $2,500 and subject to MAGI phaseouts. Past‑year payments (2016–2020) are not deductible on the 2024 return.
- If you did pay student loan interest in 2024, check Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer for the amount they report. If your modified AGI is above the phaseout threshold, the deduction may be reduced or eliminated.

5) Charitable contribution ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest)
- Charitable cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. You said you have the receipt — keep the written acknowledgement (required for cash gifts of $250+).
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct the $1,400 on your federal return.

6) Interest income ($920 + $320 = $1,240)
- Report on Form 1040. Banks will issue 1099‑INT if interest ≥ $10 (or sometimes even if less). Include the total reported and any additional interest you received.

7) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses (from your W‑2 job), they are not deductible on the federal return for 2018–2025.
- If they are business expenses for your freelance work, include them on Schedule C.

8) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- You will likely owe self‑employment tax on net profit from Schedule C; compute with Schedule SE. You may deduct half of that SE tax as above‑the‑line on Form 1040.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). With relatively small net business income, you’ll generally qualify for the simplified 8995. QBI is computed after certain adjustments; we can estimate it if you want.

Forms you will typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — if you have adjustments (half SE tax, etc.)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your graphic design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expense method for home office
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for QBI deduction if applicable
- 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (if issued by clients) and any 1099‑INTs from banks — use these to report income
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (if you had interest paid in 2024)
- Massachusetts: Form 1 (MA resident return) — state-specific return and instructions (MA will have different rules and may not conform to all federal deductions; check MA Form 1 instructions or we can review specifics).

Practical next steps and questions I need to refine estimates
1. Did you actually pay any student‑loan interest in calendar year 2024? If yes, how much and do you have Form 1098‑E for 2024?
2. Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your freelance work, or are they employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
3. Are you renting (sounds like $2,100/mo rent) or do you have a mortgage? (Depreciation rules differ for homeowners.)
4. Do you have any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state taxes paid in 2024, other charitable gifts, unreimbursed business car mileage, etc.)?
5. Did your clients issue 1099‑NEC for the $8,900, and do you have records of your business expenses besides the home office?

Quick illustration (rough example if you use actual home‑office and treat $890 as Schedule C expenses)
- Gross: W‑2 $84,900 + interest $1,240 + gross self‑employment $8,900 = $95,040
- If Schedule C expenses include home office actual ≈ $5,004 + $890 other expenses = $5,894 → net self‑employment profit ≈ $3,006
- Self‑employment tax on $3,006 ≈ $460 (approx); half SE tax ≈ $230 adjustment
- Taxable income = W‑2 + net SE + interest − half SE tax − standard deduction (29,200) → you can see the standard deduction remains dominant.
(This is an example only; exact numbers depend on the precise expense totals.)

Final notes
- The home‑office deduction is a valuable benefit for self‑employment — you can take it and still use the standard deduction federally.
- Student loan interest is limited to actual interest paid in the tax year (max $2,500) and phases out by MAGI.
- If your situation is more complex (e.g., depreciation, prior‑year carryovers, business losses), consider a tax preparer or CPA — I can help estimate numbers if you supply the answers to the questions above.

If you want, I can:
- Run a rough tax estimate if you confirm: (a) whether the $890 is SE expense, (b) whether you paid any student loan interest in 2024 and how much, and (c) whether you rent or own. Or
- Prepare a checklist of documents to collect before filing.

