Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — below is a clear, practical plan and what I need from you to give specific numbers. I’ll cover (1) which items are deductible, (2) how to treat the home office, (3) whether to itemize vs take the standard deduction, (4) what forms you’ll need, and (5) next steps/questions for me.

1) Quick facts from what you gave
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (MA resident).
- W-2 wages: $84,900 (Riverside Hospital).
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $8,900 (presumably 1099/independent contractor).
- Interest income: $920 (Marcus), $320 (Charles Schwab) — taxable interest.
- Rent: $2,100/month for a 780 sq ft home.
- Home office: 155 sq ft used exclusively for business.
- Charitable gifts: $1,400 to Northwest Harvest (you have a receipt).
- Student loan interest: you listed $4,800 (need clarification; see below).
- Unreimbursed expenses: $890 (need clarification whether these are employee expenses or business expenses).

2) What’s deductible (federal) — summary and rules
- Self‑employment income/expenses (Schedule C): Your $8,900 of freelance design is taxable. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (supplies, software, phone/time allocation, travel, marketing, part of rent via home office, etc.). If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your freelance work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (related to your W-2 job), those generally are NOT deductible for most taxpayers after the 2017 tax law (subject to limited exceptions).
- Home office: Deductible if you use part of your home exclusively and regularly for the business and you are self‑employed. See section 3 for details.
- Self‑employment tax: You pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings; one-half of that self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: The “student loan interest deduction” is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1) up to $2,500 per year, subject to income limits and only for interest paid in the tax year. If the $4,800 is interest paid in prior years (2016–2020) rather than paid in 2024, it is not deductible now. Confirm the tax year(s) of those payments and whether you received Form 1098‑E for 2024 (if you paid interest in 2024).
- Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize (must be to a qualified charity and you have the receipt).
- Interest income: The $920 and $320 are taxable interest and reported on Form 1040; if your total interest > $1,500 you’ll need Schedule B (but even below that you still report it on the 1040).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: Generally not deductible (subject to narrow exceptions). If instead they relate to your freelance work, include them on Schedule C.

3) Home office — how to treat it and examples
You qualify for a home office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your business activity (graphic design). You have exclusive-use 155 sq ft of a 780 sq ft home -> business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft: 155 × $5 = $775. No Form 8829, easier.
- Actual expense method: Apply the business percentage (~19.9%) to your actual home expenses. Since you rent, your deductible portion would include 19.9% of rent and a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Example: Rent $2,100/mo = $25,200/year. 19.9% × $25,200 ≈ $5,010. That $5,010 is a business expense on Schedule C (subject to the limitation that total business expenses cannot exceed your business income — it reduces your net profit). Because your freelance income is $8,900, an actual home office rent allocation of ~$5,010 is plausible, but remember it will reduce net self‑employment income (and thus reduce income tax but not eliminate the need to pay self‑employment tax on net). If you use the actual method you generally file Form 8829 to calculate the deduction (and you might also depreciate part of the home if you own the home — not applicable if you rent).

