- Home office: 155 sq ft, exclusive use
- Deductions/other amounts: $4,800 student loan interest (payments from 2016–2020?), $1,400 charitable to Northwest Harvest (have receipt), $890 unreimbursed expenses (clarify source)

How each item is treated

1) W‑2 wages and interest income
- W‑2 wages: Report on Form 1040 (you’ll attach your W‑2).
- Interest income: Report on Form 1040 (Schedule 1) — include the 1099‑INTs from Marcus and Schwab.

2) Self‑employment income (graphic design)
- Report gross income and business expenses on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 and is subject to income tax and self‑employment (SE) tax.
- SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) is calculated on Schedule SE (approx 15.3% on net SE earnings). You may deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

3) Home‑office deduction (you qualify if it’s used regularly and exclusively for your graphic design business)
You have two methods:

a) Simplified method:
- $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- For 155 sq ft: 155 × $5 = $775 deduction.

b) Actual expense method (likely larger in your case):
- Business percentage = office sq ft / total home sq ft = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
- You can deduct the business percentage of allowable home expenses (for renters that includes rent, utilities to the extent allowed, renter’s insurance, etc.). Using rent alone:
