Below is a practical, step‑by‑step summary of what looks deductible, whether you’ll likely itemize, how the home‑office works, and the federal/state forms you’ll need. I’ll also list a few questions at the end so I can give a tighter estimate or run the math for you.

1) Quick summary (what you can and cannot deduct)
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital $84,900): report on Form 1040 (via your W-2).
- Self‑employment (graphic design $8,900 from 2 clients): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work on Schedule C.
- Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): report as interest income on Form 1040. (You only need Schedule B if interest/dividends > $1,500.)
- Student loan interest ($4,800 paid): the student‑loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (maximum $2,500) and phases out at higher MAGI. You cannot deduct more than the $2,500 limit (subject to the phaseout). Given the incomes you listed (W‑2 + self‑employment + interest), you are likely under the phaseout threshold and would be eligible for up to $2,500 of deduction — but confirm your exact MAGI to be sure.
- Charitable donation ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890): whether deductible depends on who paid them and the context:
  - If these were expenses for your self‑employment (graphic design), include them on Schedule C as business expenses.
  - If they were unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, those are not deductible for federal income tax (TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized employee business expenses through 2025).
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive use; you pay $2,100/month for 780 sq ft): special rules — see next section.

2) Home office: how it works and what to pick
- Qualification: To claim a home‑office deduction for self‑employment, the space must be used regularly and exclusively for your business (you said 155 sq ft exclusive — that sounds like it qualifies).
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft = 155 * $5 = $775 deduction. No Form 8829 needed; you report the deduction on Schedule C.
  - Actual expense method: you allocate a percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.) to the business. Your business percentage = 155 / 780 = 19.87% (≈ 19.9%).
    - Example using rent only: annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. 19.87% of $25,200 ≈ $5,006. Add your share of utilities, insurance, repairs likewise.
    - Important limitation: the home‑office deduction (actual method) cannot exceed your net income from that business (gross profit minus other business expenses). Any disallowed amount is carried forward to future years (Form 8829 handles this).
- Which to choose: compare the simplified $775 to your actual‑expense allocation. If your allocated share of rent + utilities etc. (actual method) is larger and you have sufficient net business profit to absorb it, the actual method will give a bigger deduction. If your self‑employment profit is small or the actual allocation exceeds your business profit, simplified may be simpler and sometimes better.
- Where to report: home office deduction is reported on Schedule C. If using the actual method, you compute Form 8829 and carry the result to Schedule C; using simplified you simply enter the allowed amount on Schedule C (no Form 8829).

3) Self‑employment tax and other tax effects
- You will owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). SE tax rate ~15.3% on net earnings (with the 0.9235 adjustment applied first). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Home‑office and business expenses reduce your net self‑employment income, thus reducing income tax and SE tax.
- If your self‑employment tax or underpayment of federal tax is large, you may need to make estimated tax payments quarterly to avoid penalties.

4) Itemize vs Standard deduction (federal)
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction is substantial (recent years have been in the high‑$20k range). With only $1,400 of charitable gifts disclosed and no mortgage interest or large SALT/property taxes mentioned, your itemized deductions would almost certainly be much smaller than the standard deduction.
- Therefore, it’s very likely you should take the standard deduction for federal purposes.
- If you have significant mortgage interest or large state/local taxes or other itemizable expenses you did not list, that could change the decision.

5) Forms you’ll typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (e.g., student loan interest deduction, half of self‑employment tax enters here).
- Schedule C — report self‑employment income and business expenses (graphic design).
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you choose actual‑expense method). If you use the simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not in your case).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend income > $1,500 (not needed here given $1,240 interest).
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if any clients issued them), 1099‑INT, W‑2, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement from MOHELA), charity receipts.

6) Massachusetts state tax
- You’ll file Massachusetts Form 1 (MA resident return). MA generally starts with your federal AGI and makes MA-specific adjustments. Self‑employment income and interest are taxed by MA. The details of MA deductions/credits differ from federal; check MA instructions or ask a preparer for any state-specific treatments. (If you need, I can look up MA specifics or guide you through MA Form 1.)

7) Records to gather / practical checklist
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if any) from your two graphic design clients, or other records showing $8,900 received.
- 1099‑INTs (Marcus, Schwab).
- Form 1098‑E or account statement from MOHELA showing $4,800 interest paid.
- Receipts for charitable donation (Northwest Harvest).
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses (the $890 and any others).
- Home expense records: rent receipts/lease, utilities, renter's insurance, repairs for actual method; measure and confirm the 155 sq ft and 780 sq ft.
- Any estimated tax payment records or withholding info.

8) Practical next steps I can help with
- If you want, I can run estimated federal tax calculations for you (compare: standard vs. itemized; simplified vs. actual home‑office) — I’ll need:
  - Whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are for the self‑employment work (graphic design) or W‑2 job.
  - Whether you have mortgage interest or property taxes or sizable state tax payments (SALT).
  - Any 1099s/other income not listed.
- Or I can draft a one‑page summary/packet you can send to a CPA (with the numbers you gave) so they have everything in one place.

9) A few cautions
- Student loan interest is capped at $2,500 and phases out by MAGI — verify your exact MAGI if you’re near phaseout limits.
- The actual home‑office method is limited to net profit from the business; excess carries forward.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are generally not deductible for federal income tax if they relate to a W‑2 job (unless you have a specific uncommon exception).

If you want me to run the numbers now (project a federal refund/amount due and compare itemize vs standard and simplified vs actual home office), tell me:
- Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses for your graphic‑design business or for your W‑2 job?
- Do you pay property tax or mortgage interest or other large itemizable expenses? If yes, how much (annual)?
- Do you want me to assume you rent (I’ll use the $2,100/mo) and to test both simplified and actual home‑office methods?

