Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s likely deductible, whether itemizing will make sense, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and a few MA) forms you’ll need. A couple of quick clarifying questions before I compute exact estimates:

- Is the $4,800 you listed student-loan interest paid during the 2024 tax year, or is that an accumulated total from 2016–2020? (Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible.)
- The $890 “unreimbursed expenses” — were those out-of-pocket business expenses for your graphic design work (supplies, software, travel, etc.), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a job? (If employee unreimbursed, they generally aren’t deductible on the federal return for 2018–2025.)
- Do you rent (I assume yes since you list monthly payment $2,100) and do you pay utilities that you want to allocate to the home office?

Preliminary analysis and steps (based on your numbers and assuming the $4,800 is NOT prior-year interest but interest paid in 2024 — please confirm):

1) Which deductions are available to you
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is deductible as an above-the-line adjustment on Schedule 1 (so it reduces your AGI). There is an income phase-out (for MFJ you are well below the typical phase-out threshold with the income numbers you gave), but you must confirm the $4,800 was paid in 2024 — only interest paid in the tax year counts. If you paid $4,800 in 2024, you can only deduct up to $2,500.
- Charitable contributions: Your $1,400 gift to a qualifying charity (Northwest Harvest) is deductible if you itemize (keep the receipt). If you take the standard deduction you do not get this separate deduction.
- Self-employment (graphic design) related expenses: If the $890 are business expenses, they belong on Schedule C and reduce your business net income. Qualifying business expenses (software, supplies, internet portion used for business, etc.) are deductible.
- Home office: You can deduct a home-office expense if you’re self-employed and use the space exclusively and regularly for business. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft this is 155 * $5 = $775.
  - Actual expense method: Deduct the business-use percentage of actual home expenses. Your business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. If rent is $2,100/month = $25,200/year, the allocable portion of rent alone ≈ $25,200 * 19.87% ≈ $5,005. You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, internet portion, etc., which increases the deduction. If you use actual-expense method you should use Form 8829 to calculate and claim the home office deduction (or complete the Schedule C portions if applicable). Note: the home-office deduction reduces self-employment net income (Schedule C).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: If these $890 are unreimbursed expenses for an employee (not self-employed) they are generally not deductible on your federal return for tax years 2018–2025.
- Interest and investment income: $920 (Marcus savings account) and $320 (Charles Schwab) are taxable interest/dividends and reported on Form 1040 (and you should receive 1099-INT / 1099-DIV statements).

2) Standard deduction vs. itemize (quick conclusion)
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200. (IRS inflation adjustments.)
- Your itemizable amounts that you listed: charitable = $1,400. You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, large state/local taxes or other itemizable medical expenses. Unless you have additional itemizable deductions (e.g., mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, higher charitable contributions), your itemized total will be well below the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: You will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction (unless you have additional unreported itemizable deductions). The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment and is helpful whether or not you itemize.

3) How to treat the home office (practical choices)
- If you are self-employed for the graphic design work, claim the business income and expenses on Schedule C. Claim the home-office deduction on Schedule C either via:
  - Form 8829 (actual-expense method) — you compute the business percentage and apply it to rent, utilities, insurance, etc.; or
  - Simplified method ($775 for 155 sq ft).
- If you use actual expenses, your rent allocation alone (≈ $5,005) will likely give you a much bigger deduction than the simplified method. That will reduce your Schedule C net profit (which reduces both income tax and self-employment tax), but make sure you have documentation of rent payments and utility bills to support the allocation.
- Note: home office deduction is only available for business use by the self-employed. If your graphic design income is from W-2 employment (and you are an employee), you generally cannot take the home-office deduction.

4) Self-employment taxes and QBI
- With self-employment income you’ll owe self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net earnings (Schedule SE). You can deduct one-half of SE tax on Schedule 1.
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 8995) for pass-through/self-employed business income depending on taxable income and business type. Given the low business net income shown, you will likely qualify; it reduces taxable income (not SE tax).

5) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 — Additional income and adjustments (student loan interest deduction, half of self-employment tax adjustment, other adjustments)
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report graphic design income of $8,900 and business expenses)
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (compute SE tax)
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home, if you use the actual-expense method (not required if you choose the simplified method)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income Deduction (if you claim QBI)
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you decide to itemize; likely you will not)
- Attach W-2 and include 1099-INT (bank), 1099-DIV (brokerage), 1099-NEC (if you received one from clients) information on Form 1040 (you don’t physically “attach” all 1099s when e-filing but you should have them available and report the amounts).
- For Massachusetts: File Massachusetts Form 1 (resident return). MA tax rules differ in places — you’ll report MA sources and withholdings there. MA may have different treatment of some deductions/credits.

6) Documents to gather
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099-NEC or records of the $8,900 graphic design receipts
- 1098-E statement from MOHELA showing student loan interest paid in 2024 (to support deduction)
- 1099-INT / 1099-DIV from Marcus and Schwab
- Receipts for charitable donation to Northwest Harvest
- Rent ledger or canceled checks and utility bills to support actual home-office allocation (if using actual-expense method)
- Records/receipts for the $890 business expenses (or clarification if they are employee unreimbursed)
- Any estimated tax payments or state withholding info

7) Rough numeric illustration (approximate — I can refine with your answers)
Assumptions: student loan interest paid in 2024 is at least $2,500 (you can only deduct up to $2,500); $890 is business expenses; actual home-office rent allocable ≈ $5,005.

- Total income: 84,900 (W-2) + 8,900 (self-employed gross) + 920 + 320 = 94,040.
- Schedule C net (approx): 8,900 - (5,005 home office rent allocable) - 890 business expenses = 3,005 net self-employment income.
- Self-employment tax (approx): net * 0.9235 * 15.3% ≈ $425 (very rough).
- Half of SE tax deductible ≈ $212 (Schedule 1 adjustment).
- Student loan interest adjustment (max) ≈ $2,500 (if you paid that much in 2024 and meet limits).
- AGI rough: 94,040 - 2,500 (student loan) - 212 (half SE tax) = about 91,328 (ignoring other adjustments).
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2024) = $29,200 → taxable income ≈ 62,128 (very rough).
- These are rough numbers to illustrate how the home office actual method can materially reduce your self-employment income. If you choose the simplified home office ($775) your Schedule C net and SE tax would be larger.

8) Additional points & recommended next steps
- Confirm the student loan interest amount was paid in 2024; if not, only 2024 interest is deductible (and you might have a 1098-E from MOHELA).
- Confirm whether the $890 are business expenses. If they are employee unreimbursed, they aren’t deductible federally.
- Decide whether to use actual home-office method (Form 8829) or simplified method. If you have rent and utilities to allocate, actual method likely gives the bigger deduction here.
