Quick summary / recommendation
- Likely take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly = $29,200) unless you can document more than $29,200 in itemizable deductions (charity, SALT, mortgage interest, etc.). With the numbers you gave, standard deduction will almost certainly be larger.
- Treat the $8,900 graphic‑design income as self‑employment income (Schedule C). Report all related business expenses there (the $890 and any other business costs). That allows you to claim a home‑office deduction for that business use.
- For the home office, because you have an actual measurement (155 sq ft exclusive use of a 780 sq ft home) you can choose either:
  - Actual method (use business percentage of rent, utilities, etc.) — this will likely give you the bigger deduction here, or
  - Simplified method ($5 per sq ft × 155 = $775).
  Based on the rent you reported, the actual method looks more beneficial for you.
- Student‑loan interest: you can claim the student‑loan interest deduction up to $2,500 (it’s an adjustment to income on Form 1040 Schedule 1). You paid $4,800 but the deduction is capped at $2,500.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are not deductible under current federal law. If the $890 is business expenses for your freelance work, deduct them on Schedule C instead.
- Expect to file Form 1040 with Schedule C (business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method), and possibly Form 8995 for the QBI deduction. Also keep your Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) and any 1099s/1098/1099‑INT.

Detailed reasoning with numbers (approximate)
Assumptions from your numbers:
- W‑2 wages: $84,900
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $8,900
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year
- Home size: 155 sq ft home office / 780 total sq ft → business percentage = 155 ÷ 780 ≈ 19.87% (≈19.9%)

Home‑office deduction options
- Simplified: 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction (no Form 8829).
- Actual method (example using rent only; you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. if you have amounts):
  - Rent portion allocable to business = $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,008.
  - If you have utilities, internet, renter’s insurance etc., allocate the business share and add them here. Form 8829 helps compute this.

Which method looks better?
- If you treat your $890 unreimbursed expense as business expense and use actual home‑office (rent portion ≈ $5,008), your Schedule C math would be roughly:
  - Gross self‑employment receipts: $8,900
  - Less: home office (actual): ≈ $5,008
  - Less: other business expenses: $890
  - Net self‑employment profit ≈ $3,002
- Using simplified: 8,900 − 775 − 890 = net ≈ $7,235

So the actual method gives you a larger business deduction and therefore lower taxable income in this case.

Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net business earnings (Schedule SE). SE tax ≈ 15.3% on net earnings (technically on 92.35% of net profit), and you may deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Example using actual method net profit ≈ $3,002:
  - Net earnings for SE = 3,002 × 92.35% ≈ $2,773
  - SE tax ≈ $2,773 × 15.3% ≈ $424
  - Half SE tax deductible ≈ $212 (adjustment to income)

Student‑loan interest
- You paid $4,800, but the federal student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (and phases out at higher MAGI). With the income levels you reported, you should be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction. This is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $29,200.
- From the numbers provided, your likely deductible itemized items shown are:
  - Charitable: $1,400
