User: I want to file my 2024 taxes correctly. I'm married filing jointly in Boston MA. W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $84,900. Additional income: $8,900 (graphic design, 2 clients), $920 from Marcus savings account, $320 from Charles Schwab. Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive; I pay $2,100/month for 780 sq ft. Deductions: $4,800 student loan interest (MOHELA, 2016–2020); $1,400 to Northwest Harvest (food bank) have the receipt; $890 unreimbursed expenses. Can you tell me what I can deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and what forms I need?
Assistant: Thanks — I can walk you through how to treat each item, whether you should itemize, how the home‑office deduction works, and which federal (and a few state) forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also show approximate numbers so you can see the tax impact.

Quick summary / recommendation
- Likely take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly = $29,200) unless you can document more than $29,200 in itemizable deductions (charity, SALT, mortgage interest, etc.). With the numbers you gave, standard deduction will almost certainly be larger.
- Treat the $8,900 graphic‑design income as self‑employment income (Schedule C). Report all related business expenses there (the $890 and any other business costs). That allows you to claim a home‑office deduction for that business use.
- For the home office, because you have an actual measurement (155 sq ft exclusive use of a 780 sq ft home) you can choose either:
  - Actual method (use business percentage of rent, utilities, etc.) — this will likely give you the bigger deduction here, or
  - Simplified method ($5 per sq ft × 155 = $775).
  Based on the rent you reported, the actual method looks more beneficial for you.
- Student‑loan interest: you can claim the student‑loan interest deduction up to $2,500 (it’s an adjustment to income on Form 1040 Schedule 1). You paid $4,800 but the deduction is capped at $2,500.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are not deductible under current federal law. If the $890 is business expenses for your freelance work, deduct them on Schedule C instead.
- Expect to file Form 1040 with Schedule C (business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method), and possibly Form 8995 for the QBI deduction. Also keep your Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) and any 1099s/1098/1099‑INT.

Detailed reasoning with numbers (approximate)
Assumptions from your numbers:
- W‑2 wages: $84,900
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $8,900
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year
- Home size: 155 sq ft home office / 780 total sq ft → business percentage = 155 ÷ 780 ≈ 19.87% (≈19.9%)

Home‑office deduction options
- Simplified: 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction (no Form 8829).
- Actual method (example using rent only; you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. if you have amounts):
  - Rent portion allocable to business = $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,008.
  - If you have utilities, internet, renter’s insurance etc., allocate the business share and add them here. Form 8829 helps compute this.

Which method looks better?
- If you treat your $890 unreimbursed expense as business expense and use actual home‑office (rent portion ≈ $5,008), your Schedule C math would be roughly:
  - Gross self‑employment receipts: $8,900
  - Less: home office (actual): ≈ $5,008
  - Less: other business expenses: $890
  - Net self‑employment profit ≈ $3,002
- Using simplified: 8,900 − 775 − 890 = net ≈ $7,235

So the actual method gives you a larger business deduction and therefore lower taxable income in this case.

Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net business earnings (Schedule SE). SE tax ≈ 15.3% on net earnings (technically on 92.35% of net profit), and you may deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Example using actual method net profit ≈ $3,002:
  - Net earnings for SE = 3,002 × 92.35% ≈ $2,773
  - SE tax ≈ $2,773 × 15.3% ≈ $424
  - Half SE tax deductible ≈ $212 (adjustment to income)

Student‑loan interest
- You paid $4,800, but the federal student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (and phases out at higher MAGI). With the income levels you reported, you should be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction. This is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $29,200.
- From the numbers provided, your likely deductible itemized items shown are:
  - Charitable: $1,400
  - Possibly state and local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 — but you didn’t list the exact amount paid in MA income tax or property tax, so we can’t know if itemizing would exceed $29,200. With rent and no mortgage interest, it’s unlikely you’ll surpass $29,200 unless you have large state taxes or other deductible items not listed.
- Conclusion: Most likely take the standard deduction. You should only itemize (Schedule A) if your total itemizable amounts exceed $29,200.

Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a Section 199A QBI deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income). With small net business income, you may get a small QBI deduction — reported on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A if more complex). I recommend preparing Schedule C first and then checking Form 8995.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — individual return
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments (student loan interest, half SE tax if applicable)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report freelance/graphic income and business expenses (including home office if you don’t use the simplified method) 
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual home‑office method)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — QBI deduction (if applicable)
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (charitable, SALT, etc.)
- Keep: Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement), any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT(s) for the bank/Schwab interest

Massachusetts state return
- File MA Form 1 (or the appropriate Massachusetts return). MA rules differ about some deductions; student‑loan interest treatment and other items may be different on the state return — check MA guidance or ask a local preparer.

Practical next steps
1. Gather documents: Form W‑2; any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC; 1099‑INTs; Form 1098‑E for student loan interest; receipts for business expenses (the $890 and any others); rent statements and utility bills to support the home‑office allocation; charity receipts.
2. Prepare Schedule C and compare the home‑office simplified vs actual method (calculate both). Use Form 8829 for the actual method.
3. Compute SE tax on Schedule SE and apply half of that as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
4. Apply the $2,500 student‑loan interest adjustment on Schedule 1.
5. Compare itemized total vs $29,200 standard deduction; choose the larger.
6. Prepare Form 1040 with the above inputs, and consider Form 8995 for the QBI deduction.
7. If you expect similar self‑employment income next year, consider quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

A few extra notes
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job are not deductible on the federal return under current law. Only business expenses for your self‑employment can be deducted on Schedule C.
- If your freelance income was paid to an LLC or through an employer, or you received 1099s, keep those records and make sure you report all income even if no 1099 was received.
- Keep documentation for the home office (sketch of floor plan showing the 155 sq ft exclusive space, lease/rent statements, utility bills) in case of audit.
- If you want, I can run the numbers again with exact rent/utilities, any state tax amounts, other charitable gifts, mortgage interest (if any), and any other expenses — that will let us confirm whether the standard deduction is best and produce a more precise tax estimate.

Would you like me to:
- calculate more precise amounts if you provide state tax or utility/mortgage interest figures, or
- draft a checklist of documents you should collect before preparing the return?

