Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’m not a CPA, so treat this as general guidance and confirm with a tax pro or the IRS if you need a definitive ruling. I’ll explain what’s generally deductible, how the home‑office works for a self‑employed side gig, whether you’ll probably itemize or take the standard deduction, and which tax forms you’ll use. I’ll also flag a few questions I need to give more precise answers.

Summary up front
- Your W‑2 wages ($84,900) and interest income ($920 + $320 = $1,240) are reported on Form 1040.
- The $8,900 from graphic design is reported on Schedule C (self‑employment). You’ll pay self‑employment tax on the net business profit (Schedule SE) and may claim the home‑office portion as a business expense on Schedule C (using Form 8829 if you use the actual‑expense method).
- Charitable gift of $1,400 is an itemized deduction, but with the amounts you listed you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction for married filing jointly (see reasoning below).
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (Form 1040 adjustment) but is limited — only interest paid in the tax year reported on Form 1098‑E and subject to income rules; the max annual student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (subject to phaseouts).
- Keep records/1099s/1098‑E and receipts (charity, home‑office calculations, business expenses).

Details and calculations

1) Self‑employment income and business deductions
- Report the $8,900 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (supplies, software, advertising, the $890 you called “unreimbursed” if those are business expenses — clarify below). Those reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- Self‑employment tax: you pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on 92.35% of your Schedule C net profit; you then deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) — typically 20% of your qualified business income from the Schedule C (reported on Form 8995 or 8995‑A). With a small net profit, QBI would be small but still applicable.

Example rough math using your numbers (illustrative)
- Gross self‑employment = $8,900
- Business expenses (example): $890 (you said unreimbursed expenses) => preliminary net = $8,010
- Home‑office deduction (actual method example, see below) might further reduce net (see home‑office). If net profit after all business deductions = $3,004 (example below), then:
  - Taxable portion for SE tax = 92.35% * $3,004 ≈ $2,774
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% * $2,774 ≈ $425
  - Half SE tax deductible ≈ $212 (adjustment to income)
  - QBI (≈20% of net) ≈ $601 (approximate)

(These are illustrative to show how the pieces interact. I can run precise numbers once you confirm which expenses are business and whether you’ll use actual home‑office or the simplified method.)

2) Home‑office deduction (you’re self‑employed, so you can take it)
You said: home office = 155 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft; rent = $2,100/month.

- Office % = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%.
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Rent portion allocable to the home office ≈ 19.9% × $25,200 ≈ $5,006.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × square feet = $5 × 155 = $775 (simple, no Form 8829).
  b) Actual expenses method: allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to the office. Use Form 8829 to compute allowable deduction. This usually gives a larger deduction here (≈$5,006) than the simplified method.
- Important limit: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business. If your business net (before the home office) is less than the home‑office amount, you can’t deduct the excess in the current year — it may carry forward.
- Because you’re self‑employed, the home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit, lowering both income tax and self‑employment tax (good).

3) Student loan interest ($4,800 you listed)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year is deductible, and you should receive Form 1098‑E from the servicer for interest paid that year. If the $4,800 was paid over prior years (2016–2020), you can’t deduct past years’ interest on the 2024 return.
- Annual deduction limit: up to $2,500 (above‑the‑line), subject to MAGI phaseouts for married filing jointly. Also, if you file Married Filing Separately, you generally can’t take the deduction.
- You’ll need to confirm how much interest you paid in 2024 (the amount on 1098‑E) and your MAGI to determine how much of that (if any) is deductible.

4) Charitable contribution ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest)
- If you itemize, this is deductible on Schedule A (must be to a qualified charity and you should keep the receipt).
- For MFJ, itemized deduction includes charitable gifts, state & local taxes (SALT, limited to $10,000), mortgage interest, medical expenses over threshold, etc.
- Given the items you listed (no mortgage interest and only $1,400 charitable giving shown), your total itemized deductions will likely be much less than the standard deduction for married filing jointly, so you’ll probably take the standard deduction.

5) Standard deduction vs itemize
- For married filing jointly you should compare:
  - Standard deduction (for the tax year you’re filing; check IRS for the exact amount for tax year 2024), versus
  - Total itemized deductions (charity $1,400 + any state/local taxes up to $10,000 + any mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc.)
- Based on what you’ve listed (no mortgage interest, low charity, small other items), you will most likely be better off taking the standard deduction. If you have other itemizable items (large state taxes, medical expenses over the floor, big charitable donations, mortgage interest), that could change the calculation.

6) Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (for adjustments: student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax if applicable)
  - Schedule C (report graphic design income and business expenses)
  - Schedule SE (self‑employment tax)
  - Form 8829 (if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office; simplified method does not use 8829)
  - Form 8995 or 8995‑A (if claiming the QBI deduction; usually Form 8995 for simpler situations)
  - Schedule A (only if you choose to itemize)
  - 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (income statements you received from clients, if any)
  - 1099‑INT (for Marcus and Schwab interest)
  - 1098‑E (student loan interest statement)
- State (Massachusetts):
  - MA Form 1 (Massachusetts individual income tax return)
  - Keep records for any local requirements (Boston does not have a city income tax, but confirm any local filings you may need)
- Records to keep: receipts for charitable contributions, business expenses, rent and utility bills for home‑office allocation, 1099s and 1098‑E, any client invoices and bank records.

Questions I need to be precise / offer to calculate
1. Was the $4,800 student loan interest paid in 2024 (and do you have Form 1098‑E for 2024)? If not, we can’t deduct prior years’ interest on the 2024 return.
2. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the graphic design work (supplies, software, travel, etc.)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job (which are not deductible for 2018+ employees)?
3. Do you (or your spouse) have any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, large state taxes withheld or paid, big medical expenses, IRA/HSA contributions, tax credits)?
4. Do you want me to run the approximate tax effect (estimate AGI, taxable income, SE tax, and compare itemize vs standard) using the assumptions I described?

If you confirm the items above (especially whether the $890 are business expenses and whether the $4,800 is interest paid in 2024), I’ll run a detailed numeric estimate and show exactly which forms and lines will be affected.

