Thanks — I can walk you through this. Below I’ll cover (1) what is deductible, (2) whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home‑office, (4) what federal and state forms you’ll typically need, and (5) recommended next steps and a few questions so I can be more precise.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital $84,900): report on Form 1040 (your W‑2 will show withholding).
- Self‑employment (graphic design $8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work on Schedule C. That includes:
  - The $890 unreimbursed expenses — if those were business expenses for your graphic‑design work, put them on Schedule C. If instead they are unreimbursed employee expenses (expenses you paid while working as a W‑2 employee), those are generally NOT deductible for most taxpayers under current law.
  - Home‑office deduction (see section 3).
  - Any other business costs (software, supplies, marketing, internet % used for business, etc.) — keep receipts.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C profit (calculated on Schedule SE). You may deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 of Form 1040.
- Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): report on Form 1040 (usually on Schedule B only if total interest/dividend > $1,500). Amount is taxable.
- Student loan interest ($4,800 you reported): student‑loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (adjustment to income) up to $2,500 per year, subject to income (MAGI) phaseouts. You can only deduct interest paid in the tax year and only if you meet the income limits and other rules; you’ll normally receive Form 1098‑E from the servicer showing interest paid. If the $4,800 is a total of past years (2016–2020) rather than interest paid in the tax year, only the current‑year interest paid is deductible.
- Charitable gift ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible if you itemize (requires receipt). Cash contributions are reported on Schedule A if you itemize.
- State & local taxes (SALT): state income tax (withheld or estimated) and property tax are deductible on Schedule A but SALT deduction is limited to $10,000 total (federally).

2) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For married filing jointly (MFJ) most recent standard deduction amounts have been relatively large, so most households with only small charitable gifts and no mortgage interest or large property taxes will take the standard deduction.
- Based on the items you listed, your likely itemizable deductions appear small: charitable $1,400 + whatever SALT (state income tax + property tax) you actually paid (limited to $10,000). Unless you have sizable mortgage interest, large property taxes, big medical expenses, or other deductible items you haven’t listed, you will probably take the standard deduction.
- To decide: add up all possible Schedule A items (charity, SALT up to $10k, medical over 7.5% of AGI, mortgage interest, casualty losses if any, etc.). If that total exceeds the standard deduction for MFJ in the tax year, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.

3) Home‑office treatment (important)
- Because you have self‑employment income, you can take a home‑office deduction for the exclusive, regular use space used for your graphic design business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot (for tax years when the simplified method is $5 — confirm current amount), up to 300 sq ft. For your 155 sq ft that would be 155 × $5 = $775.
  b) Actual‑expense method: prorate actual home expenses by the home‑office percentage. Your office is 155 sq ft ÷ 780 sq ft = 19.87% of the home. For example, annual rent is $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; the rent portion allocable to the office ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,007. You could also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, maintenance, etc. (as allowed). Since you rent, depreciation is not applicable (depreciation applies to homeowners).
- Which to choose? Based on the rent numbers you gave, the actual method looks substantially larger than the simplified method (≈$5,007 vs $775), so actual method will likely give a bigger deduction — but it requires records and Form 8829 (for homeowners) or entry on Schedule C (for renters you still document the business use). Use the actual method if you have documentation for the expenses.
- Note: the home‑office deduction is only available for self‑employment (Schedule C) or certain business owners. W‑2 employees generally cannot claim it.

4) Forms you’ll typically need (federal + state)
Federal:
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax deduction).
- Schedule C — report self‑employment income and business expenses (graphic design).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — business use of your home (if using actual‑expense method for home office; note some software handles this without a separate form for renters — confirm with software or preparer).
- Schedule A — itemized deductions (only if you choose to itemize).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividends > $1,500 (not required with $1,240).
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of QBI) — may apply to your Schedule C net income; depends on income and business type.
State (Massachusetts for Boston residents):
- Massachusetts Form 1 (resident income tax return) and any required schedules. Report W‑2 wages, interest, and business income on the state return. MA has its own rules for deductions and exemptions — check the MA Form 1 instructions or consult a preparer for specifics.

5) Other important items & tax consequences
- Self‑employment tax: net Schedule C income is subject to SE tax (~15.3% before applying the 0.9235 adjustment). You can deduct one‑half of SE tax on Schedule 1 as an adjustment to income.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income and likely insufficient withholding, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties.
- 1099 paperwork: you should receive 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC from clients who paid you $600+; 1099‑INT for interest; 1098‑E for student loan interest if the servicer reported >$600. Even if you don’t receive a 1099 you must report the income.
- QBI deduction (20%): you may be eligible for a 20% deduction on qualified business income; this is separate from Schedule C expenses and has rules and thresholds.
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable donations, business expenses, home‑office calculations and utility bills, rent receipts, Forms 1099/1098, and student loan interest statements.

6) Quick numerical comparison (home office example)
- Home office share = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
- Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200.
- Actual home office rent portion ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,007.
- Simplified method = 155 × $5 = $775.
- Example effect on Schedule C (approximate):
  - If you use actual method and treat the $890 business expenses + $5,007 rent share as business expenses: business expenses ≈ $5,897 → net self‑employment income ≈ $8,900 − $5,897 = $3,003.
  - If you use simplified method: expenses ≈ $890 + $775 = $1,665 → net ≈ $7,235.
  - That big difference shows why the actual method may be better here, given your rent.

7) Next steps / questions for more precise advice
Please confirm:
- Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for the graphic‑design work (or are they unreimbursed expenses from a W‑2 job)?
- Do you rent (I assumed yes given $2,100/mo), or do you own the home? (Depreciation rules differ for owners.)
- How much (if any) mortgage interest or property tax did you pay in the tax year?
- How much student loan interest was actually paid in the tax year you’re filing for? (The $4,800 figure you gave might be cumulative over several years — only the current‑year interest is deductible; do you have Form 1098‑E for the tax year?)
- Any other deductible items (large medical expenses, other charitable donations, retirement contributions to a SEP/SIMPLE/IRA, estimated tax payments paid, etc.)?
- Would you like me to prepare a short estimated tax worksheet (showing estimated federal tax, SE tax, and whether to make quarterly payments) using the numbers you gave?

If you want, I can:
- Estimate your federal tax (rough) and SE tax with the numbers you gave using both home‑office methods, or
- Draft a checklist of receipts/forms to collect before filing, or
- Recommend the exact forms to fill out based on your answers to the clarification questions.

Tell me which follow‑up you want (est tax estimate, checklist, or answer the clarification questions) and I’ll proceed.

