I can help — this looks straightforward but I need a few clarifying facts before finalizing numbers. First, quick summary of how the items you listed are generally treated, then what I need from you to give a precise recommendation and the forms you’ll use.

High‑level guidance (federal)

- W‑2 wages ($84,900): report on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Self‑employment (graphic design $8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary & necessary business expenses (supplies, software, business portion of phone/internet, etc.) on Schedule C.
- Interest ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): report as interest income on Form 1040 (1099‑INTs).
- Home office: If you use 155 sq ft exclusively and regularly for your graphic design work, you can take a home‑office deduction only for the self‑employment business. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 (easy, less recordkeeping).
  - Actual method: pro rata share of home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Your business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; business share of rent alone ≈ $5,009. Add business share of utilities, internet portion, etc. To claim actual method you use Form 8829. If you use the simplified method you do not file Form 8829.
  - Note: home‑office deduction applies to the business on Schedule C; it reduces self‑employment profit (and thus SE tax). If it creates a net loss for the business, there are some rules — typically losses from a sole proprietorship can offset other income, but there are exceptions; if this is a concern, mention it.
- Self‑employment tax: You’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You can deduct half of SE tax as an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Form 1040.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890): under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (through 2025), miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses are NOT deductible for most employees. If those $890 are business expenses related to your self‑employment, deduct them on Schedule C. If they relate to your W‑2 job and were unreimbursed, they generally aren’t deductible.
- Student loan interest ($4,800): only interest paid during the tax year is potentially deductible (up to $2,500 maximum) and the deduction phases out at higher MAGI levels. You’ll generally need Form 1098‑E from your servicer for the year showing interest paid. If the $4,800 is interest paid across multiple years (2016–2020), that is not deductible on your current year return — only interest you paid in the tax year counts.
- Charity ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible if you itemize. You need the receipt/proof. (Note: for tax years recent rules allowed limited universal charitable deductions for non‑itemizers in some years, but standard setup: cash gifts are itemized deductions.)
- Itemize vs standard: for married filing jointly the standard deduction is large. With the items you listed (charity $1,400 + student loan interest up to $2,500 if eligible = ~$3,900), that is far less than the typical MFJ standard deduction, so in most cases you will take the standard deduction rather than itemize. You would only itemize if you have significant other deductible items (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes, large medical expenses above the threshold, etc.).

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — if needed
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $8,900 and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual method for the home office (not needed for simplified)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you claim Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (possible 20% deduction on qualified business income; depends on taxable income and nature of business)
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (you should receive from MOHELA for the year(s) you paid interest)
- 1099‑NEC / records of 1099s if clients issued them (income still taxable even if no 1099)
- 1099‑INT for interest income

State (Massachusetts)
- Massachusetts resident return (Form 1) — report wages, interest, and self‑employment income on the MA return. MA has its own rules for deductions/credits; for example, student loan interest handling and the treatment of business income can differ. Check MA DOR guidance or I can look up specifics if you want.

Practical recommendations and next steps
1. Clarify items I need before final numbers:
   - Which tax year do you mean by “2024 taxes” — are you filing the return for tax year 2024 (filed in 2025) or filing in 2024 for tax year 2023? (Some rules, amounts, and forms differ by year.)
   - Is the $4,800 student loan interest the amount paid in the tax year, or is it total interest paid over the years 2016–2020? (Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible now.)
   - Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses for your self‑employment work or for your W‑2 job?
   - Do you have any other deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state tax payments, large unreimbursed medical expenses, casualty losses, etc.) that might push you to itemize?
   - Do you have 1099‑NEC or other records for the $8,900? Do you have 1099‑INT and 1098‑E for interest and student loan interest?

2. Home office method recommendation (based on the numbers you gave):
   - Because your self‑employment income is modest ($8,900), the simplified method ($775) is probably the simplest and less likely to trigger a complex calculation. If you have sizable utilities or want to claim ~19.9% of rent (~$5,009) and utilities and that produces significantly larger deduction and you have documentation, you could use the actual method (Form 8829). If you want, I can calculate both outcomes once you confirm utilities/other home expenses and whether you want to maximize the deduction.

3. Likely outcome (based on provided numbers, assuming student loan interest wasn’t paid in the current year or is limited):
   - You will likely take the standard deduction (MFJ) because your reported itemizable amounts are small compared with the standard deduction.
   - You will file Schedule C and Schedule SE for the $8,900 self‑employment income; claim business expenses (including home office) on Schedule C.
   - Expect some self‑employment tax (Schedule SE); you’ll get half of that as an adjustment to income.
   - Student loan interest: if you have a 1098‑E showing up to $2,500 interest paid in the tax year and your MAGI is below the phaseout threshold, you can claim up to $2,500 (reduced if you exceed phaseout). If you do not have 1098‑E for the tax year (or did not pay interest in the year), you can’t claim the full $4,800.

4. Records to gather now
   - W‑2 from Riverside Hospital
   - 1099‑NEC / records for the $8,900 self‑employment income
   - Receipts for any business expenses (the $890 if business expenses), home office square footage and rent amount and any utility bills
   - 1099‑INT(s) for interest
   - 1098‑E from MOHELA (student loan interest) for the tax year
   - Charity receipt for Northwest Harvest
   - Last year’s tax return (helpful)

5. Other items to consider
   - Estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES): if you expect to owe tax because of self‑employment, you may need to make estimated quarterly payments to avoid underpayment penalties.
   - State specifics: Massachusetts forms and rules — I can check MA treatment for student loan interest and home‑office if you want a state‑specific plan.
   - If you want me to prepare a draft calculation (estimate of AGI, taxable income, self‑employment tax, and whether standard deduction or itemize), confirm the clarifying questions above and I’ll run the numbers and list exact forms to file.

Would you like me to:
A) Run an estimated tax calculation now using the simplified home office method and assuming up to $2,500 student loan interest (if you paid that in the tax year), or
B) First confirm the clarifying items (which I recommended) and then produce exact filing guidance and a line‑by‑line form checklist?

Tell me which option and answer the clarifying questions you can, and I’ll proceed.

