I can walk you through this. Below I’ll (A) summarize what’s likely deductible, (B) show how the home-office deduction works (with quick math using the numbers you gave), (C) recommend whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (D) list the federal (and some state) forms you’ll likely need, and (E) ask a couple of clarifying questions. I’m not a tax professional — for anything firm or borderline, consider a CPA or EA (especially for state specifics in MA).

A. What you can likely deduct (federal)
- W-2 wages ($84,900): reported on Form 1040; no deduction there except payroll withholding and any pre-tax benefits already taken out.
- Self-employment (graphic design) income ($8,900): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against this income (see below).
- Business expenses ($890): If these are expenses for the graphic-design business, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, those are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended).
- Home-office: If the 155 sq ft is used exclusively and regularly for your design work, you can take a home-office deduction on Schedule C (either simplified or actual/regular method). See next section for numbers.
- Student loan interest ($4,800): The student-loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (up to $2,500 max for the year, subject to income phaseouts and other rules). Important: you can only deduct interest you actually paid during the tax year (you’ll usually have a Form 1098-E from your loan servicer showing interest paid). Also whether you qualify depends on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) and filing status.
- Charitable gift ($1,400): Cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.
- Interest income ($920 + $320 = $1,240): Report as interest income on Form 1040. You will usually receive Form 1099-INT from each institution. Because total interest is under $1,500, Schedule B likely not required.
- Self-employment tax: You must pay self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment earnings; half the SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

B. Home-office deduction — two options and quick math
You said: 155 sq ft exclusive home office; apartment is 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month (so annual rent $25,200).
- Business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.

1) Regular/actual method (likely larger for you)
- Rent portion for business = $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,010.
- You can also apportion utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., by the same percentage and deduct those on Form 8829 (or on Schedule C depending on form). If you use this method you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to calculate the deduction that flows to Schedule C.

2) Simplified method
- $5 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 (maximum 300 sq ft).
- No Form 8829 required; you enter the simplified deduction amount on Schedule C.

Which to use?
- Using the actual method in your situation looks to be much larger (~$5,010 just for rent) than the simplified $775. The actual method will likely reduce your Schedule C net income much more. Use actual if you have documentation for rent/utilities/etc.

Effect on self-employment tax and income tax
- The home-office deduction reduces net business profit on Schedule C, which reduces both income tax and self-employment tax (SE tax is computed on net profit × 92.35%).
- You also get to deduct half of any SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

C. Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction is almost always much larger than $1,400. (Check the official IRS standard-deduction amount for tax year 2024; it is almost certainly well above the single-digit-thousands.)
- Your likely itemizable amounts you listed: charitable $1,400. You didn’t list significant deductible mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, or large state/local taxes (SALT — note SALT caps apply federally). So your itemized total looks to be well below the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: You will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. The home-office deduction and business expenses still help you whether you itemize or take the standard deduction because they are above-the-line (on Schedule C) and reduce your AGI/Taxable Income.

D. Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income — e.g., student loan interest deduction, half SE tax; reporting other income/adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $8,900 gross, subtract business expenses, home-office deduction.
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — calculates SE tax; half of SE tax is an adjustment to income.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use if you choose the actual/home portion method (instead of the simplified method).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (likely you will not, based on the amounts you gave).
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividend > $1,500 or certain foreign accounts; your interest ($1,240) likely doesn’t require it.
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A). If you have net profit from your design work you may be eligible for up to 20% QBI deduction; with a small net profit this may be small but still possible.
- Keep: W-2, 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC from clients (if any), 1099-INT from banks, Form 1098-E from servicer (student loan interest), receipts for charitable contributions, records of rent/utility bills for home-office, business receipts.

State (Massachusetts)
- File MA Form 1 (Massachusetts resident return). MA starts from federal AGI but has its own rules for exemptions, deductions, and treatment of various items. Charitable deductions and student loan interest may be treated differently in MA than on federal — check MA instructions or ask your preparer.

E. Practical next steps and documents to gather
- Confirm whether the $4,800 student-loan interest is the amount paid in tax year 2024 (you can only deduct interest paid in the tax year). Get Form 1098-E (or a statement) from MOHELA showing interest paid in 2024.
- Confirm whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the graphic-design work or unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job.
- Gather:
  - W-2 from Riverside Hospital
  - 1099-NEC (if issued) or records of payments from the two design clients
  - 1099-INT from Marcus and Charles Schwab (or statements showing interest)
  - Rent receipts and utility bills (for home-office actual method)
  - Charitable receipt for $1,400
  - Business receipts for supplies, software, advertising, etc.
  - Any estimated tax payments you made in 2024
- Consider which home-office method you want to use (simplified or actual) — actual likely gives you the bigger deduction here if you have rent/utility records.

F. Quick example (illustrative approximate numbers)
- Total income: W-2 $84,900 + self-employment gross $8,900 + interest $1,240 = $95,040.
- If you deduct home office by actual method (rent portion ≈ $5,010) + $890 business expenses → net business profit ≈ $2,999.
- SE tax on that small profit will be modest (a few hundred dollars), and half of that reduces your AGI.
- Charitable $1,400 would only help if you itemize — but that’s likely less than the standard deduction, so you’d take the standard deduction instead.
(These numbers are illustrative; exact amounts depend on your final, documented expenses and actual interest/students payments in 2024.)

G. Recommendations
- Likely take the standard deduction for federal.
- Definitely report the self-employment income on Schedule C and claim business expenses and the home-office deduction (actual method likely preferable given your rent).
- Verify student loan interest was paid in 2024 and check MAGI phaseout rules — if you qualify, you can claim up to $2,500 (subject to limits).
- Keep good records and receipts. Consider a tax pro for MA-specific questions and to confirm optimal treatment (and to prepare Form 8829, QBI computation, and SE tax details).

If you want, I can:
- Walk through a more precise cash-flow-style calculation using the exact IRS 2024 standard deduction number (I can look that up for you if you want), or
- Draft a checklist email you can send to a CPA with the documents to bring, or
- Prepare a draft return outline showing where each item goes on Form 1040/Schedules.

Which of those would you like next? Also please confirm:
1) Was the $4,800 student loan interest paid in calendar year 2024 (and do you have a 1098-E)?  
2) Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your graphic-design clients (or were they for your W-2 employer)?  
3) Do you want me to use the actual home-office method numbers or the simplified method for a detailed estimate?

