Quick conclusions
- Most likely you’ll take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($29,200 for 2024) unless you have substantial additional deductible items (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes, large medical expenses, or more charitable giving).
- Your home office is a self-employment/business deduction (Schedule C + Form 8829 if you use the regular method) and is separate from the decision to itemize on Schedule A.
- Student loan interest: the maximum federal deduction is limited (generally up to $2,500 per year) and is subject to income phaseouts — you cannot deduct $4,800 in full for one tax year.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (post-TCJA) are not deductible on your federal return unless they are business expenses of a self-employed activity (they would belong on Schedule C if they relate to the graphic design work).

Detailed breakdown

Income to report
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital: $84,900 — report on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Self‑employment (graphic design) $8,900 — report on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). If you have business expenses, include them on Schedule C.
- Interest/dividends: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 — report on Form 1040 (you’ll receive 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV).

Home office
- You said the office is 155 sq ft exclusive and the whole home is 780 sq ft. Percentage = 155/780 ≈ 19.9%.
- Two methods:
  - Regular method (more paperwork): allocate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) to the home office by that percentage. For rent: $2,100/mo × 12 = $25,200 yearly; 19.9% of that ≈ $5,000 in deductible home-office portion (if that is the only home expense you allocate). You can include utilities, renters insurance, etc., pro‑rated similarly.
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 (easy, no Form 8829).
- Important limits: For self‑employed taxpayers, the home office deduction is claimed on Schedule C. If the regular-method home-office deduction would exceed your net business income, you must limit it — excess may be carried forward as unallowed expenses. In your case, if regular-method home-office expense (~$5,000) is less than your gross self-employment income ($8,900) and other business expenses are small, you can likely take the full amount.
- If you use the regular method you generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829.

Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The federal student loan interest deduction maximum is generally up to $2,500 per year (and there are income limits/phaseouts). You cannot deduct $4,800 in one year; only interest paid during the tax year and subject to the $2,500 cap and income limitations is deductible.
- You should have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the tax year. If the $4,800 is cumulative over multiple years, only the current-year interest paid (reported on 1098‑E) is relevant for this year.
- Phaseouts apply based on MAGI for MFJ — confirm your modified AGI to see whether you can take the full $2,500 or a reduced amount.

Charitable contribution ($1,400)
- This is an itemized deduction on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction ($29,200) you won’t separately deduct this on the federal return.
