- The 2024 standard deduction for MFJ is $29,200. Your known itemizable items listed = $1,400 charitable + possibly state/local taxes up to $10,000 SALT limit (you didn’t list property tax or state tax paid) + mortgage interest (none listed). Unless you have sizable other itemizable deductions (large state income/property taxes, mortgage interest, or large unreimbursed medical expenses exceeding thresholds), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Reminder: the home office deduction is not part of Schedule A — it reduces net self-employment income whether or not you itemize.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest deduction (if eligible) and other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your graphic design income and business expenses (including home office)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method to compute the home office deduction
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (probably you won’t, but include if you do)
- Forms you should collect/expect: W-2 (Riverside), 1099‑NEC or records for self-employment income, 1099‑INT (Marcus/Schwab), 1098‑E (student loan interest from MOHELA), receipts for charitable contributions.

Massachusetts (state) notes
- You’ll file MA Form 1. State rules for deductions and adjustments differ from federal rules. For example, MA may not follow all federal above-the-line deductions exactly. If you have significant state-specific questions (credits, rent deduction?), consider a tax preparer or I can summarize MA specifics if you want.

What I recommend you do next
