Summary / short answer
- You will file Form 1040 (married filing jointly). Report W-2 wages, interest, and self-employment income.
- Your graphic-design income (self‑employment) is reported on Schedule C and is eligible for the home‑office deduction and the self‑employment deduction/SE tax rules.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500 maximum, subject to income limits).
- Charitable gift ($1,400) is deductible only if you itemize; otherwise you take the standard deduction (for most MFJ taxpayers the standard deduction will be larger).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 work are generally NOT deductible (2018–2025 TCJA suspension), but if the $890 was a business expense for your graphic design work it should go on Schedule C.
- Likely outcome: you’ll take the standard deduction (usually larger than the $1,400 charitable gift plus any other itemizable expenses you didn’t list). If you want I can run rough math using exact numbers you give for other deductions (state tax withheld, property tax, mortgage interest, medical expenses, etc.).

Details and how to treat each item

Income
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital ($84,900): report on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Interest: Marcus $920 + Schwab $320 = $1,240 total interest — report on Form 1040. If total taxable interest > $1,500 you’d also complete Schedule B; below that you just report the amounts.
- Self‑employment (graphic design) $8,900: report gross receipts on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You may also receive 1099‑NEC(s) from clients; even without 1099s you must report the income.

Self‑employment specifics
- Schedule C: report the $8,900 and deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (supplies, software, business internet portion, client travel, and any of the $890 if those were business expenses).
- Self‑employment tax: net profit from Schedule C is subject to SE tax. File Schedule SE to compute Social Security and Medicare tax. You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes if withholding doesn’t cover your tax+SE tax.

Home‑office deduction (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; home is 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- You qualify for a home‑office deduction for the self‑employment (graphic design) work if the space is used exclusively and regularly for business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft = 155 × $5 = $775. Easy, no Form 8829 needed.
  - Regular method: allocate actual home expenses by business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Deduct that portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business portion), repairs allocable to office, etc. Example: annual rent 2,100 × 12 = $25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,013 just for rent portion (plus utility share). Use Form 8829 to compute this when using the regular method.
- Which to choose? Compare the two. With your rent level the regular method may give a larger deduction, but the regular method is more paperwork and can have implications for depreciation if you owned the home (you rent, so depreciation generally not applicable).
- The home‑office deduction reduces net Schedule C profit (and therefore income subject to income tax and SE tax).

Student loan interest
- You paid $4,800 to MOHELA 2016–2020 (I assume this is interest during those years or total interest paid?). The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1 to Form 1040) up to $2,500 per year, subject to modified AGI phaseouts. You can only deduct interest paid in the tax year (the lender issues Form 1098‑E). If you truly paid $4,800 in 2024, the maximum deductible is $2,500 (and phaseouts may reduce it further depending on your MAGI).
- Verify the Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the tax year.

Charitable contribution ($1,400)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you don’t separately deduct it.
- Keep your receipt — because it’s above $250, you’ll need written acknowledgment for the contribution. (IRS requires acknowledgment for cash gifts of $250+.)

Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are expenses you incurred for your W-2 job (employee unreimbursed expenses), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under federal law.
- If they are business expenses for your self‑employment (clients, supplies, advertising, software), report them on Schedule C and they reduce your net business income.

Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2024 MFJ the standard deduction is substantially larger than a single charitable gift. Unless you have significant deductible amounts you didn’t list (mortgage interest, large state+local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, or larger charitable gifts), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Quick check: your only itemized deduction shown so far is $1,400 charity. That’s much smaller than the 2024 MFJ standard deduction, so standard deduction is almost certainly better.

Potential additional federal considerations
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be able to claim up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C business (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). Eligibility and amount depend on taxable income and the type of business. Graphic design often qualifies as a non‑specified service trade (but there are rules and income thresholds). This is another reason to use tax software or a preparer if you want to maximize benefits.
- If you select the regular home‑office method and it generates a Schedule C loss, that loss can offset other income (subject to at‑risk and passive activity rules where applicable).

Forms you’ll likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — e.g., student loan interest deduction if applicable).
- Schedule B (only if interest > $1,500).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for $8,900 graphic design.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for home office deduction.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for the QBI (20%) deduction if you claim it.
- Keep the W-2, any 1099‑NEC(s), 1099‑INT(s) from Marcus and Schwab, and Form 1098‑E from MOHELA.

State (Massachusetts)
- You’ll file a Massachusetts return (Form 1 for residents). Report your federal adjusted gross income and make MA adjustments. MA rules differ from federal rules for some deductions and credits. If you want, I can outline what to expect on the MA return, but it’s best to confirm state-specific treatment of student loan interest and itemized deductions with MA guidance or a preparer.

Practical next steps I recommend
