Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll summarize what’s deductible, whether you’ll likely itemize, how to treat the home office, and what federal (and brief MA) forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag follow‑ups I need from you.

Quick bottom line
- You’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction on your federal return (much larger than the itemized deductions you listed).  
- Claim the graphic‑design income on Schedule C (you’re self‑employed for that income) and that’s where you take the home‑office deduction. You’ll also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).  
- Student loan interest: you cannot deduct $4,800 — the maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher MAGI). Based on the numbers you gave, you’re likely eligible for the full $2,500 (but confirm MAGI).  
- Report the bank interest ($920 + $320 = $1,240) on Form 1040 (Schedule B only needed if total interest > $1,500 or other conditions).

Details and calculations (estimates)
1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital: $84,900 (report on Form 1040)  
- Self‑employment (graphic design) gross: $8,900 — report on Schedule C. If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those forms (even if you didn’t, you still report it).  
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 — report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if > $1,500).

2) Business (graphic design) deductions and self‑employment tax
- If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your graphic design work, include them on Schedule C. If those are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are generally not deductible on the federal return (post‑TCJA). Please tell me which they are (business vs employee).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax on net Schedule C profit (typically ~15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings). Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.

3) Home office deduction (your 2 options)
You have a qualify­ing home office if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business.

- Size and percentage: 155 sq ft exclusive / 780 sq ft total = ~19.9% (about 20%).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 (no Form 8829).
  b) Actual expenses method: multiply the business use percentage (~19.9%) × allowable indirect expenses (rent, utilities, etc.). For rent alone: $2,100/mo × 12 = $25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,010. If you use actual method, you’d use Form 8829 to compute the deductible portion (and include any direct home office expenses in full).
- Which to choose: actual method almost certainly gives a larger deduction for you (because of high rent). The home‑office deduction is limited to net profit from that business (gross business income minus other business expenses). Based on the numbers you gave:
  - Gross business = $8,900
  - Less other business expenses (if $890 are business expenses) = $8,010
  - Home office actual ≈ $5,010 → allowed (doesn’t exceed business income after other expenses) → would reduce your Schedule C profit to roughly $3,000 (estimate).
  - If you instead use simplified, you’d only get $775.
- Note: actual method triggers Form 8829 and may involve depreciation calculations (if you claim depreciation on the home office); depreciation can create later recapture rules if you sell the home. Because you rent, depreciation on the dwelling isn’t applicable, but Form 8829 still documents the indirect expense allocation.

4) Student loan interest
- The federal student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 per return (even if you paid $4,800). It’s an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 (Form 1040) if you meet the income requirements. For married filing jointly, the deduction phases out at higher MAGI; with W‑2 wages ~$84.9k + modest net self‑employment, you should be under the phaseout and can claim the full $2,500 if those payments are qualified (get 1098‑E from MOHELA). Keep the 1098‑E/records.

5) Charitable gift
- $1,400 to Northwest Harvest: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the MFJ standard deduction is likely much higher than your itemized total (see below), you will probably not itemize just for this amount.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For tax year 2024 (filing 2025) the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is substantially larger than your listed itemized deductions (charity $1,400 plus any other deductible items). With only $1,400 of charitable donations and no mortgage interest or large state/property taxes listed, standard deduction is almost certainly the better choice.
- Note: the student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment and does not require itemizing.

7) Massachusetts state tax
- MA has different rules from federal. You still report federal adjusted gross income on MA Form 1 but check MA instructions for any addbacks or subtractions (for example, MA might not allow some federal adjustments). I can’t provide the full state conformity details here — consider MA Dept. of Revenue guidance or a tax pro for state‑specific treatment (especially for home office and student loan interest conformity).

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)  
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and adjustment for half of SE tax flows here  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report graphic design income and ordinary business expenses  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — calculate and report SE tax  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual expenses method for the home office  
