Short answer / quick recommendation
- You will almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) rather than itemizing — the charitable gift you listed ($1,400) is far smaller than the MFJ standard deduction.  
- Report the $8,900 graphic‑design income on Schedule C (self‑employment); the home office and business expenses belong on Schedule C. That income also triggers self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).  
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (up to $2,500, subject to income limits), so it reduces AGI whether or not you itemize — but it only applies to interest paid in the tax year.  
- You’ll file Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, and Schedule 1 (for adjustments). For state taxes file the Massachusetts return (Form 1). Consider estimated tax payments or higher withholding because of self‑employment income.

Details and reasoning

1) Which of your items are deductible (federal)
- W‑2 wages: report on Form 1040 (you’ll get a W‑2 from Riverside Hospital).  
- Self‑employment income (graphic design, $8,900): report on Schedule C. From Schedule C you deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (see below). Net profit flows to Form 1040 and Schedule SE.  
- Business expenses (the $890): if these are expenses related to your graphic‑design work, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are generally not deductible on the federal return (suspended through 2025 under current law). Clarify which they are.  
- Home office: deductible on Schedule C if you qualify (see next section).  
- Interest from Marcus ($920) and Charles Schwab ($320): taxable interest (report on Form 1040). Expect 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV statements.  
- Student loan interest: the student loan interest deduction is an adjustment on Schedule 1, up to $2,500 (not a Schedule A item). It is subject to income phaseouts. Important: only interest paid in the tax year is eligible. If you actually paid $4,800 in interest in 2024, you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 (subject to your MAGI). You should receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the tax year.  
- Charitable gift ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct this on your federal return. You should have the receipt for substantiation.

2) Itemize vs standard deduction (federal)
- The 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200. (Standard deduction amounts are adjusted for inflation each year.)  
- Your listed potential Schedule A items are small: $1,400 in charitable gifts and no apparent mortgage interest or large property taxes. Unless you have other large deductible items (significant state/local taxes up to the SALT cap, large medical expenses above threshold, mortgage interest, big charitable contributions), itemizing will be less than the standard deduction.  
- Student loan interest is an adjustment to income (not a Schedule A item) and does not affect the itemize decision.  
Conclusion: you will most likely take the standard deduction.

3) Home office deduction — how it works and the two methods
Qualification: to deduct a home office you must use the space exclusively and regularly for your business (you said 155 sq ft exclusive — that sounds like it could qualify). The home office deduction applies only to self‑employment (Schedule C). It generally cannot be used for W‑2 employee work.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that would be 155 × $5 = $775. Simple to calculate and minimal recordkeeping.  
- Regular (actual) method: you calculate the business percentage of your home (155 ÷ 780 ≈ 19.9%). Multiply that percentage by eligible home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs allocated to the office portion). Example with your rent: $2,100/month × 12 = $25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,008 attributable to the home office (plus any allocated utilities/insurance). That is much larger than the simplified $775.

Important limits and interactions:
- The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit; SE tax applies to that net profit.  
- The home office deduction cannot create or increase a self‑employment loss for the year (excess amounts may be carried forward as business deductions subject to passive/activity rules). If your Schedule C net before the home office is small (or negative), the deduction may be limited.  
- If you use the regular method you must keep records (rent statements, utility bills, how you calculated the square footage). The simplified method is easier but often smaller for renters with a large percentage of the home used for business, as appears in your case.

Given your numbers (rough estimate):  
- Gross self‑employment income = $8,900.  
- If the $890 are bona fide business expenses, net before home office = $8,010.  
- Regular home office portion of rent ≈ $5,008 → net business income ≈ $3,002 (still positive, so the larger deduction is usable).  
- Simplified method gives $775 → net ≈ $7,235.  
So the regular method likely gives you a larger deduction and reduces taxable income more, but you must keep supporting records.

4) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security and Medicare) on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). The SE tax rate is about 15.3% on net earnings, but you may deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.  
- Paying the home office and other business expenses reduces net self‑employment income and thus reduces SE tax.

5) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).  
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and deduction for one‑half of SE tax go here.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $8,900 (and business expenses, home office).  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (seems unlikely given the numbers you listed).  
- Form 1098‑E — you should receive this from MOHELA for student loan interest paid in the year (use that to support the student loan interest adjustment).  
- 1099‑INT / 1099 statements for interest from Marcus and Schwab, and W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.

State forms
- Massachusetts: file MA Form 1 (married filing jointly). State rules differ from federal for what can be deducted, so check MA guidance or ask your preparer about MA treatment of student loan interest, charity, and the treatment of self‑employment income.

6) Documentation to keep
- Receipts for charitable contributions (your Northwest Harvest receipt).  
- 1098‑E from MOHELA showing student loan interest paid in the tax year. (If you don’t have one but did pay interest, contact MOHELA.)  
- All 1099‑INT/1099 forms for interest income and W‑2.  
- Records for the home office: floor plan or calculations showing 155 sq ft, rent statements, utility bills, renter’s insurance, any repairs and date/amounts.  
- Business income/expense records: invoices from the 2 clients, bank deposits, receipts for supplies, the $890 expenses documentation, etc.  
- A record of estimated tax payments if you make them.

7) Estimated taxes / withholding
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments if you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file. Alternatively, you can increase withholding on your W‑2 job (submit a new Form W‑4 to your employer) to cover the tax and avoid quarterly payments.

8) Items I need you to confirm (before I give precise numbers or draft an email/return)
- Did you actually pay $4,800 of student loan interest in the 2024 calendar year, or is $4,800 a multi‑year total? Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible — you should have Form 1098‑E for the year.  
- Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your graphic‑design clients (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?  
- Do you rent the home the full year and pay $2,100 every month (so annual rent = $25,200)? Is the home office used exclusively for the business?  
- Any other deductions or large expenses (medical, state/local taxes you paid, mortgage interest, big charitable gifts, large unreimbursed losses) not listed here?

9) Next steps I can help with
- Run precise tax‑impact numbers (estimate AGI, taxable income, self‑employment tax) if you confirm the items above.  
- Draft a summary email or document you can send to your tax preparer with all the facts and documents listed.  
- Draft entries for Schedule C (I can prepare a draft with your numbers) or a checklist of documents to gather.

If you want, tell me the answers to the confirmation questions (especially whether the $4,800 interest is for 2024 and whether the $890 are self‑employment expenses). I can then run rough numbers showing (a) standard deduction vs itemizing, (b) taxable income and estimated federal tax and SE tax, and (c) recommend whether the simplified or regular home office method is better for you. Also say if you’d like a draft email to your tax preparer summarizing everything.

