Summary conclusions (short)
- You’ll almost certainly take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) rather than itemizing.
- Report the $8,900 graphic-design income on Schedule C (self‑employment). Use the actual home‑office method (Form 8829) — it appears to give a larger deduction than the simplified method and is allowable because you say it’s exclusive use.
- You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax on your Form 1040 (above the line).
- The $4,800 “student loan interest (2016–2020)” is not automatically deductible for 2024 — only student loan interest you actually paid in 2024 (with Form 1098‑E) is deductible (up to $2,500, subject to income limits).
- The $1,400 gift to Northwest Harvest is deductible only if you itemize. Because your likely itemized deductions are small, the standard deduction will probably be larger.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job are not deductible on the federal return (suspended under current law). If the $890 are business expenses for your graphic‑design work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible there.

Detailed breakdown and numbers (estimates)
Income
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $84,900
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $8,900 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Charles Schwab) = $1,240 (taxable interest reported on Form 1040; Schedule B only required if interest/dividend > $1,500)

Self‑employment and home office
- Business gross income: $8,900
- Business expenses you listed as $890 — if these are for your graphic design work, enter them on Schedule C (office supplies, software, etc.). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your federal 1040.
- Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive use; residence = 780 sq ft. Business percentage = 155/780 ≈ 19.87%.
  - Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year. Allocate 19.87% = about $5,008 of rent attributed to the business if using the actual expense method.
  - Using actual expenses appears better than the simplified method ($5 × 155 = $775), because the actual allocation (~$5,008) is larger and still less than your business gross after other business expenses.
- Example net profit (rough):
  - Gross self‑employment income: $8,900
  - Minus business expenses (if those $890 are business) = $8,010
  - Minus home office allocation (actual method) ≈ $5,008
  - Net Schedule C profit ≈ $3,002
- Self‑employment tax (estimate):
  - Net earnings subject to SE = profit × 0.9235 ≈ 3,002 × 0.9235 ≈ 2,773
  - SE tax ≈ 2,773 × 15.3% ≈ $424
  - Deductible half of SE tax (above‑the‑line on Form 1040 / Schedule 1) ≈ $212

Qualified Business Income (QBI)
- You likely qualify for a QBI deduction of up to 20% of your qualified business income (QBI) from the graphic design business. If QBI ~ $3,002, 20% ≈ $600 (reported on Form 8995 or 8995‑A as applicable). This reduces taxable income (not AGI).

Student loan interest
- Only interest actually paid in 2024 is potentially deductible (max $2,500 on Schedule 1 / Form 1040), and you need Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer. Payments/interest from 2016–2020 are not deductible on the 2024 return unless you actually paid interest in 2024 and received a 1098‑E for 2024.
- Deduction phases out at higher MAGI levels — check the Form 1040/IRS instructions or ask your preparer if your AGI is near phaseout thresholds.

Charitable contribution
- $1,400 to Northwest Harvest is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). Because MFJ standard deduction for 2024 is large, you’ll likely get more benefit from the standard deduction.

Standard vs itemizing (quick comparison)
- 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $29,200.
- Likely itemized deductions you provided: charitable $1,400 + any state income tax / property tax (SALT) up to $10,000. You didn’t list mortgage interest or big medical expenses, so your itemized total is almost certainly below $29,200. Conclusion: take the standard deduction.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (where half of SE tax and student loan interest (if eligible) are entered)
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report $8,900 and business expenses)
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (calculate SE tax)
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual home‑office method). The result flows to Schedule C.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — Qualified Business Income Deduction (if you claim QBI)
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize)
- Keep 1098‑E from MOHELA (if you have 2024 student loan interest reported), 1099‑INT from your banks (Marcus/Schwab), and any 1099‑NEC you received from clients (you report self‑employment income even if no 1099 issued).

State (Massachusetts)
- You’ll file MA Form 1 (Massachusetts resident return). MA rules differ from federal for itemized deductions, student loan interest, and home office (MA may not allow the same home office or above‑the‑line adjustments). Check MA instructions or consult a preparer for state treatment.

Action items / checklist for you
1. Confirm whether the $890 is self‑employment business expense (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expense (not deductible on federal).
