Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. Below is a clear, practical rundown of what you can and can’t deduct, whether itemizing will help, how to treat the home office, and what federal (and basic state) forms you’ll likely need. I also list the documents you should collect and a few clarification questions so I can run numbers for you if you want.

Quick summary (short):
- Likely better to take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($29,200) rather than itemizing, based on the amounts you gave.
- Your student loan interest payments are only deductible up to $2,500 (and only the interest paid in the tax year; phaseouts apply by income).
- If the $8,900 is self‑employment income, you report it on Schedule C and can take a home‑office deduction (actual or simplified). If you’re an employee doing work for Riverside (W-2) and the home office relates to that W-2 job, the home‑office deduction isn’t allowed for federal returns through 2025.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 work are generally not deductible federally after the 2017 tax law (unless you have a specific exception). If those $890 are self‑employment business expenses, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.
- You’ll likely need Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (if self‑employed), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and Form 8829 only if you use the actual home‑office method. Check your state return (MA Form 1) for state-specific rules.

Details and reasoning

1) Which deduction — standard or itemize?
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $29,200.
- Your known itemizable amounts: charitable cash $1,400; state income tax paid (you’ll have MA tax withheld/on your return) — MA tax on your income is likely a few thousand dollars but total itemized deductions still look well below $29,200 unless you have substantial mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or large unrevealed property tax. So most likely the standard deduction will be larger.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have more itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large property tax, big unreimbursed medical expenses, or additional large charitable gifts) that bring total > $29,200.

2) Student loan interest ($4,800 listed)
- Only interest actually paid in the tax year can be deducted. You should get Form 1098-E from your loan servicer (MOHELA) showing interest paid in the tax year.
- The federal student loan interest deduction limit is $2,500 per year (adjustment to income on Form 1040). There is an income phaseout — for MFJ it phases out at higher MAGI; based on your income (W-2 $84,900 + self‑employment $8,900 + interest ~ $1,240 ≈ $94k) you are likely under the phaseout and should be eligible for the full $2,500. But I need your exact MAGI to confirm.
- If the $4,800 number is interest from prior years rather than paid in 2024, that wouldn’t be deductible on 2024 returns.

3) Self‑employment / additional income ($8,900) and unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- Report self‑employment income on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). All ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work go on Schedule C and reduce net self‑employment income.
- Self‑employment income is subject to income tax AND self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Form 1040.
- If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are job (W‑2) related and not for your business, they are not deductible on your federal return under current law. If they are business expenses tied to your self‑employed graphic design (e.g., materials, software, mileage), put them on Schedule C.

4) Home office (155 sq ft exclusive use; total home 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- Two methods if you qualify (only applies if you are self‑employed and use part of your home regularly and exclusively for business):
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that’s 155 × $5 = $775 deduction. No Form 8829 required. Easy and often good for small offices.
  - Actual (regular) method: allocate actual expenses by business-use percentage. Business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. If rent is $2,100/month = $25,200/year, the home office share of rent would be about $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,011. You can also allocate utilities, renters insurance, etc., apportioned the same way. Actual method normally gives a larger deduction here.
- Important limits/details:
  - Home office deduction only available if you are self‑employed or if you have an eligible qualified home business. It is not allowed for employees’ W-2 jobs (through 2025).
  - Actual method requires Form 8829. The home office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business for certain passive activity rules; but typically for an active business it reduces net profit (which reduces both income tax and SE tax).
  - Because your self‑employment gross is $8,900, the actual method allocation of $~5,011 would materially reduce net self‑employment profit — could be beneficial but check limits and how it affects SE tax/other credits.
- Recommendation: If you are self‑employed on that income, compare simplified ($775) vs actual (about $5,011 of rent portion plus utility shares) — if you have other business expenses and net profit is positive, the actual method probably yields a larger tax benefit. If you want, I can run the exact Schedule C/SE numbers with the figures you provide.

5) Interest income ($920 Marcus, $320 Charles Schwab)
- Report both on Form 1040 (you’ll get 1099‑INTs). They increase gross income.

6) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments like student loan interest deduction reported there).
- Schedule C (if the $8,900 is self‑employment income).
- Schedule SE (self‑employment tax).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual home office method.
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC: check whether your clients issued 1099s; you still report the income even if no 1099 issued.
- Form 1098‑E for student loan interest (from MOHELA) — used to substantiate the student loan interest deduction.
- Form 1099‑INT for bank interest.
- If you receive any Form 1098 (mortgage interest) or property tax statements, gather those if they exist (for itemizing considerations).
- For Massachusetts: MA Form 1 (resident return). State rules differ; you may be able to deduct student loan interest on MA return or have different rules for business expenses — check MA DOR instructions or let me look up state specifics.

7) Documents and records to gather
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.
- Any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC for the $8,900 self‑employment income (or records of payments if no 1099).
- 1099‑INTs from Marcus and Charles Schwab.
- 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the tax year (not total balance).
- Receipts for charitable donation to Northwest Harvest ($1,400) — keep donation receipt.
- Receipts/invoices for the $890 expenses — indicate whether they’re business or employee-related.
- Lease/rent statements and records of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. showing amounts for the home office actual method.
- Evidence of exclusive and regular use of the 155 sq ft area for business (floorplan, photos, dates).
- Records of any other deductible expenses (medical, mortgage interest, property tax, big charity gifts) if you want to consider itemizing.

8) Next steps and options I can help with
- I can run a sample tax calculation comparing:
  - Standard deduction vs itemizing (I’ll need your state tax paid or withholding, mortgage interest/property tax if any).
  - Simplified vs actual home office method, showing net self‑employment income and estimated self‑employment tax.
- I can draft a checklist/email to your tax preparer with all the items above.
- If you want me to estimate your federal tax, tell me:
  - Were the $4,800 interest payments made in 2024 (and do you have Form 1098‑E)?
  - Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for the graphic design work or employee (W‑2) job expenses?
  - Do you have any mortgage interest or property taxes for 2024? What was the state tax withheld or estimated state tax paid?
  - Do you want me to assume actual home office method or try both?

If you want, tell me the answers to the clarification questions and I’ll calculate a side‑by‑side estimate (federal only, and a rough MA estimate) showing tax owed/refund and which choices (standard vs itemize, simplified vs actual home office) give the best outcome.

