Thanks — I can walk through how these items are treated, whether itemizing makes sense, what forms you’ll need, and what I’ll need to finalize numbers. A few quick clarifying questions first:

- Are the amounts (W‑2 $84,900, $8,900 design income, interest $920 + $320, student‑loan interest $4,800, donations $1,400, unreimbursed expenses $890, rent $2,100/mo, home office 155 sq ft of 780 sq ft) payments/receipts for tax year 2024?  
- Does your spouse have any income (W‑2, self‑employment, investments)? (You said married filing jointly.)  
- Were the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” employee expenses (for a job where you receive a W‑2) or business expenses related to your graphic design work?

If you confirm those, I can run a tighter estimate. For now I’ll assume the numbers are for tax year 2024, spouse has no other income, and the $890 are business-related (I’ll flag differences below).

Summary — what you can deduct and how to treat each item

1) Federal filing choice: standard vs. itemize
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $29,200. (Verify on IRS site when you file; this is the 2024 standard amount.)
- Your clearly itemizable amount from the info you gave is only the $1,400 cash charitable donation. Other common itemized amounts (mortgage interest, large state/property taxes, big medical expenses) were not provided.
- Conclusion: With only $1,400 in charitable gifts shown, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have additional significant itemized items (large state & local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest, big unreimbursed medical expenses, etc.). If you have more itemizable items, list them and I’ll re-evaluate.

2) W‑2 wages and investment interest
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital ($84,900): report on Form 1040 using the W‑2. Standard payroll withholding/benefits apply.
- Interest from Marcus ($920) and Charles Schwab ($320): taxable interest income; report on Form 1040 (you should get 1099‑INTs if amounts ≥ $10). These add to your gross income.

3) Self‑employment (graphic design) income — $8,900
- Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). Include all ordinary and necessary business expenses (software, supplies, mileage, internet portion if applicable, advertising, etc.). If the $890 unreimbursed expenses are actually business expenses, they belong on Schedule C — they reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- If you received any 1099‑NEC from clients, include them; if you didn’t receive a 1099, you still must report the income.

4) Home‑office deduction (155 sq ft exclusive use; 780 sq ft total; rent $2,100/mo)
- You can take a home‑office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your business (it appears to be). Since you’re self‑employed for the graphic design work, you are eligible.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 155 sq ft × $5 = $775.
  - Actual (recommended here since it’s larger): allocable portion of rent/utilities. Example calculation for actual method:
    - Annual rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200.
    - Business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
    - Rent portion deductible ≈ $25,200 × 0.1987 ≈ $5,008.
    - You can also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have those bills. (Because you rent, no depreciation on the home itself.)
- Important limitation: the home‑office deduction cannot create a Schedule C net loss. You cannot deduct more home‑office expense than your business gross profit (before the home‑office deduction). Any excess may carry over to the next year.
- If you use the actual method, you’ll file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with your Schedule C. For the simplified method you just report the simplified amount on Schedule C (no Form 8829).

5) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Net self‑employment earnings (Schedule C net) are subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) computed on Schedule SE. The SE tax rate is about 15.3% on net earnings (with the 92.35% adjustment applied).
- You may deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). That reduces your AGI.

6) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (adjustment to income) up to a maximum of $2,500 per return, subject to income (MAGI) phaseouts. You need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid in the tax year.
- Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible. If that $4,800 is interest paid in the tax year, you may be able to claim up to $2,500 (subject to your MAGI phaseout). If it’s interest paid in earlier years, it’s not deductible now.

7) Charitable contribution ($1,400)
- If you itemize (Schedule A), deductible as an itemized charitable contribution provided the charity is qualified and you have the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you do not get an additional deduction for this (no 2024 above‑the‑line cash charity deduction generally).

8) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890)
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses for a W‑2 job, they are no longer deductible (suspended under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act) for most taxpayers. If they are business expenses related to your graphic design (self‑employment), report them on Schedule C and they are deductible.

9) State return (Massachusetts)
- You’ll file a MA Form 1. Massachusetts taxes wage and interest income and self‑employment income. MA has its own rules for deductions and credits — you should report the same income amounts to MA, but confirm treatment of student loan interest and any state-specific deductions. If you had MA income tax withheld, report that on Form 1.

Quick illustrative estimate (rough)
Assuming:
- W‑2 wages: $84,900
- Interest: $1,240 total
- Self‑employment gross: $8,900
- Home‑office actual deduction ≈ $5,008
- No other Schedule C expenses except maybe the $890 if business-related

Then:
- Schedule C net profit ≈ 8,900 − 5,008 − 890 = 3,002 (if $890 is business expense). If $890 is NOT business expense, net ≈ 3,892.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × net ≈ roughly $400–$600 depending on exact net.
- One‑half SE tax deductible as adjustment.
- Student loan interest deduction (if eligible) up to $2,500 would reduce AGI.
- Given the standard deduction of $29,200, you’d very likely take the standard deduction rather than itemize (unless you have other large itemizable amounts).

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and other above‑the‑line items
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $8,900 graphic design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual method for home office
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — you should receive this from MOHELA if interest was paid in the year
- 1099‑INTs for bank interest (Marcus, Schwab) and any 1099‑NEC you received from clients

Other practical points
- Keep receipts for charitable donations, business expenses, rent/utilities, student loan payments, and any 1099s/1098s.
- If you expect similar self‑employment income during the year, consider making estimated quarterly tax payments to avoid penalties.
- If your spouse has income or you have other deductions (mortgage interest, significant state & local taxes, medical expenses), that could change whether to itemize — share those numbers and I’ll run a re-check.
- Because of rules/phaseouts (student loan interest, deduction limits, carryforwards) and MA state-specific items, consider using tax software or a tax preparer if you want precise tax owed/refund numbers.

Next steps I can do for you
- If you confirm the clarifying items (tax year, whether $890 are business expenses, spouse income, whether the $4,800 is interest paid in 2024 and whether you got a 1098‑E), I can run a more exact estimate (AGI, taxable income, estimated federal tax and self‑employment tax) and recommend whether to use actual home‑office or simplified method.

