Short answer / bottom line
- You will very likely take the 2024 standard deduction (MFJ = $29,200) rather than itemize. With only $1,400 in charitable gifts and no mortgage interest shown, your itemized total is unlikely to exceed the standard deduction.
- Your graphic-design income is reported on Schedule C (Form 1040). You can take a home-office deduction for the exclusive 155 sq ft space and deduct the $890 of business expenses on Schedule C as well.
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on the net Schedule C profit (file Schedule SE) and you can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Student loan interest is NOT fully deductible — the deduction is capped (see below) and is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments), possibly Form 8995 (QBI) and Schedule A only if you choose to itemize. For Massachusetts: Form 1 (resident return) — check MA rules for student‑loan and home‑office treatment.

Detailed walkthrough with numbers (using the figures you gave)
1) Income summary
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $84,900
- Self-employment (graphic design): $8,900
- Interest (Marcus + Charles Schwab): $920 + $320 = $1,240
- Total gross income ≈ $95,040

2) Home‑office deduction — two methods
You can choose either the simplified method ($5 per sq ft, up to 300 sq ft) or the actual (regular) method. Because your rent is relatively high, the actual method looks much better for you.

- Space fraction = 155 sq ft / 780 sq ft ≈ 19.87%
- Rent = $2,100/mo × 12 = $25,200/yr
- Actual method rent portion = 19.87% × $25,200 ≈ $5,008 (you can also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs that apply to the business space)
- Simplified method = 155 × $5 = $775

Recommendation: use the actual method (≈ $5,008) because it yields a much larger deduction than simplified.

3) Self‑employment profit (approximate)
- Self-employment revenue: $8,900
- Business expenses: home-office (actual) ≈ $5,008 + unreimbursed business expenses $890 = $5,898
- Estimated net profit on Schedule C ≈ $8,900 − $5,898 = $3,002

Because net profit > $400, you will pay self‑employment tax.

Rough self‑employment tax estimate
- Net earnings subject to SE tax = 92.35% × $3,002 ≈ $2,773
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $2,773 ≈ $424
- Half of SE tax (≈ $212) is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

Note: these are rough numbers — if you use the simplified home‑office method your SE tax will be higher because net profit would be larger.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction historically is $2,500 per return (this has been the statutory cap).
- The deduction is phased out for higher-income taxpayers (based on modified AGI). Whether you can take the full $2,500 depends on your MAGI and the phaseout thresholds for the year.
- In any case you cannot deduct $4,800 in full; expect at most $2,500 (subject to phaseout) as an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1).

5) Charitable donations ($1,400)
- Charitable cash donations are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction for MFJ in 2024 is $29,200 and your likely itemizable amounts are small, you will probably not benefit from itemizing just to deduct $1,400.
- Keep your receipts — if you have other itemizable items (large state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, unreimbursed casualty losses, etc.), re-check before filing.

6) SALT and state tax considerations
- State and local tax (SALT) deductions on Schedule A are capped at $10,000 for federal purposes. If you have MA tax withheld or paid that you’d count toward SALT, include that when deciding to itemize.
- Massachusetts has its own rules. File MA Form 1; check MA DOR guidance or your tax software/cpa for state treatment of student loan interest and home‑office allocations (state conformity can differ).

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI)
- You may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995 or 8995-A) on qualified pass-through income from your graphic design business. Because your net business income is modest, you likely qualify, but there are rules and wage/asset thresholds if your taxable income gets high. Consider calculating QBI when you prepare the return or ask a preparer to include it.

8) Forms you will use (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax (the exact lines vary by year)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report gross receipts, business expenses, home office deduction (on Form 8829 if using actual method for a home owned; for renters you typically compute allocation on Schedule C or Form 8829? Note: Form 8829 is for business use of home; renters still use it similarly for actual method — tax software will guide you)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 or 8995-A (QBI deduction) if applicable
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize
- For state: Massachusetts Form 1 (resident return) + any MA schedules as required

9) Recordkeeping and practical points
- Keep receipts for the $1,400 charitable gift (the receipt you have), the $890 of business expenses, rent records, utility bills, and documentation showing the exclusive use of the 155 sq ft area for business.
- Make sure you report the $8,900 self-employment income even if you didn’t receive 1099s.
- If you don’t have enough withholding to cover federal tax + SE tax, consider making estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties.
- If your student loan interest deduction is in question due to MAGI limits, compute your MAGI (after adjustments) before excluding/deducting.

10) Recommendation
- Likely take the 2024 standard deduction (MFJ $29,200).
- Use Schedule C and the actual home‑office allocation to reduce your self‑employment income (actual method gives a substantially larger deduction for your rent amount).
- Claim up to $2,500 student loan interest as an adjustment if your MAGI allows (you cannot deduct the full $4,800).
- File Schedule SE and pay SE tax; deduct half of SE tax on Schedule 1.
- Consider consulting a CPA or using reputable tax software — home‑office, QBI, state conformity, and student loan phaseouts can get subtle and a preparer will ensure you maximize benefits and meet state rules.

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers more precisely for you if you give me: MA tax withheld (or estimated MA tax), any other deductible items (property tax, mortgage interest if you have any, other charitable gifts), and whether you own or rent (you said rent, which I assumed).
- Draft a checklist and list of documents to collect before preparing/filing.
- Draft an email to a CPA (or prepare a message you can paste into tax software) summarizing these facts for review.

