Which deductions apply, and where
- Standard deduction vs itemize:
  - 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200.
  - Your visible itemizable amount (charity $1,400) is far below $29,200, so you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for federal taxes.
  - Note: student loan interest is an “adjustment to income” (above-the-line), not an itemized deduction — you can claim it whether or not you take the standard deduction (subject to limits, see below).

- Student loan interest:
  - The maximum federal deduction is $2,500 per return (for qualified student loan interest). Even though you paid $4,800 total, the deductible amount is capped at $2,500 and is subject to income phaseouts.
  - Given your combined income (~$84.9k W-2 + $8.9k self‑employment + interest ≈ $94k gross), you are well below the income phaseout thresholds and should be able to claim the full $2,500. (I can check more precisely if you want a full AGI calculation.)

- Self-employment income and expenses:
  - Your graphic design income is reported on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - Business-related expenses (supplies, client expenses, the part of those $890 that are business-related) are deductible on Schedule C and reduce self‑employment net income.
  - You will owe self‑employment tax on net SE earnings and report it on Schedule SE; half of the self‑employment tax is an adjustment to income on Form 1040.

- Unreimbursed employee expenses:
  - If the $890 are unreimbursed expenses from an employer (i.e., employee business expenses), those are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law. If they are self‑employment business expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C.

- Home office:
  - Because you have self‑employment income, you can take a home office deduction if the 155 sq ft space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
  - Two methods:
    1. Simplified method: $5 per sq ft for up to 300 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775 deduction. (No Form 8829 required.)
    2. Actual expenses method: apportions actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, etc.) by the business percentage. Your business % = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9% (~20%).
       - Example: rent $25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,010 of rent allocable to the home office. You would also apportion utilities, insurance, etc. (You’d use Form 8829 to compute and report this.)
       - Important: actual-method home office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business. If the home‑office deduction (plus other business expenses) creates a net loss, limits apply and some amounts may be carried forward. The simplified method does not create a carryforward loss.
  - Which is better depends on how many actual home expenses you can allocate and on your net business profit. Given your relatively small gross self‑employment income, the simplified method may be simpler and less likely to be limited by net profit — but if you have significant rent/utility costs and other business expenses, the actual method could yield a larger deduction. I can run the comparison if you want.

- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction:
  - You may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A) on qualified self‑employment net income after deductions. With modest net SE income, you will likely qualify for some portion of that deduction. It’s computed after QBI rules and depends on your taxable income.

- Interest income reporting:
  - Total interest $1,240 — report on Form 1040 (Schedule B is only required if interest/dividend > $1,500 or other conditions). You should receive 1099‑INT statements if issued.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and half SE tax if applicable (current 1040 layouts may integrate these)
- Schedule C — profit/loss from self-employment (graphic design)
- Schedule SE — self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method for home office (business use of your home)
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (depends on complexity/income level; many taxpayers use Form 8995)
- Attach W-2, 1099s, 1098‑E (student loan interest) as documentation and enter amounts on the return
- Keep receipts for charitable contributions, home office substantiation, business expenses, etc.

Massachusetts (state) considerations
- You will file Massachusetts Form 1 for state income tax. MA rules differ from federal in some areas (for example, treatment of student loan interest and some deductions). Check MA specifics or ask if you want me to look up state rules or estimate state tax impact.

Practical next steps / checklist
1. Gather documents:
