3. Prepare Schedule C and SE (or use tax software / preparer). Claim student loan interest up to $2,500 on the 1040 adjustments line.
4. Decide on QBI form (8995 vs 8995-A) — software will usually select automatically.

Questions I need to finalize a tailored recommendation or estimate
- Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses related to your freelance (graphic design) work or to an employer job? (If business-related, they belong on Schedule C.)
- Do you rent (you said $2,100/month — I assume rent) or do you have a mortgage? If you have mortgage interest or paid significant property taxes, that could affect itemizing.
- Do you want me to run a rough tax estimate showing how the standard deduction vs itemizing and the two home office methods affect your taxable income and tax owed/refund?

Summary recommendation
- Likely take the standard deduction ($29,200 MFJ) for 2024.
- Claim up to $2,500 student loan interest as an above‑the‑line deduction (you probably qualify for the full amount).
- Report freelance income on Schedule C, claim business expenses (including either simplified home office $775 or the actual percentage of rent/utilities using Form 8829 if that’s more favorable).
- Use Schedule SE for self‑employment tax and claim half of that as an adjustment to income.
- Keep all receipts and documentation. Consider consulting a CPA if you want help optimizing home office vs simplified method or maximizing QBI.

