I can walk through this step‑by‑step. I’m not a CPA — for a final return or complex questions confirm with a tax pro — but here’s how the rules generally apply to your situation and what you’ll likely need to do.

Summary of your numbers (from what you gave)
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $84,900
- Self‑employment (graphic design, 2 clients): $8,900 (gross)
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Charles Schwab) = $1,240
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (MOHELA)
- Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 (Northwest Harvest — you have the receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $890 (unclear whether employee or self‑employment — see note)
- Rent: $2,100/month → $25,200/year, home size 780 sq ft, home office exclusive 155 sq ft (19.87% of home)

What you can (generally) deduct
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 maximum as an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1). It is income‑limited (phases out at higher MAGI), but with your income level (about $95k before this adjustment) you will almost certainly be eligible for the full $2,500. You’ll need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer.
- Self‑employment business expenses: Any ordinary and necessary expenses for your graphic design work are deductible on Schedule C. That likely includes the $890 if those are business expenses (software, supplies, client travel, etc.). If the $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, those are not deductible for tax years after 2017.
- Home office deduction (only for your self‑employment income): Allowed if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your business.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 × actual area (up to 300 sq ft) → 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction.
    - Actual/regular method: allocate a percentage of your home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, etc.) equal to business use percentage. With 19.87% of $25,200 rent = $5,009 of rent allocable to the business (plus any utilities/insurance portion if you include them). Note: actual method requires Form 8829 and records of expenses. The home office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit (i.e., reduces self‑employment tax and income tax).
  - Important: Home office deduction is only for self‑employed people (or certain owners); as a W‑2 employee you cannot take it if the space is for your employer’s work.
- Self‑employment tax deduction: You pay self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE); half of your SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income.
- Charitable contributions: $1,400 cash gift to a qualifying charity is deductible, but only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you do not get this separate benefit.
- Interest income: Report the $1,240 interest (1099‑INT) as income.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200.
- Your identifiable itemizable items from what you provided: charitable $1,400 plus possibly state/local taxes and property tax and mortgage interest (you did not list mortgage or property tax). The largest common itemized amounts would be mortgage interest or large state/local taxes (SALT is capped at $10,000). With only $1,400 of charity and no mortgage interest listed, it’s very likely the standard deduction ($29,200) will be larger than your itemized total. Conclusion: you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have significant mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or large state/local taxes not listed here.

How the home office interacts with itemizing vs standard
- The home office deduction is a business expense reported on Schedule C and does not depend on whether you itemize. It reduces your business income and thus your taxable income whether you take the standard deduction or itemize.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 – main individual return.
- W‑2 (Riverside Hospital).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) – report your $8,900 gross, list business expenses (including either the simplified home office deduction or the actual home portion if you use Form 8829).
- Schedule SE – to calculate self‑employment tax.
- If using the actual home office method: Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home).
- Schedule 1 – for adjustments to income, including student loan interest (and half of SE tax is shown as an adjustment).
- Schedule A – only if you choose to itemize (unlikely given your data).
- Keep Forms 1099‑INT (banks), and 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients if they issued them (even if they didn’t, you still report the income).
- Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest.

State tax (Massachusetts)
- Massachusetts resident return — Form 1 (MA Form 1) or whatever MA uses for that year. MA generally taxes income and will want MA source income and adjustments; many items follow federal but MA has its own rules. Confirm specific state treatment (e.g., MA may not allow the federal student loan interest above‑the‑line in the same way). If you had MA withholding shown on your W‑2, that will go on the MA return.

Recordkeeping you should have
- 1098‑E from MOHELA (student loan interest).
- Receipts/acknowledgment for Northwest Harvest donation (cash gift receipt).
- 1099‑INTs from Marcus and Charles Schwab.
- Any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for self‑employment payments (or records/invoices if none issued).
- Documentation supporting home office (square footage calculation, photos, a copy of lease/rent and rent payments, utility bills if using actual method).
- Records for the $890 expenses (invoices/receipts) showing they were business expenses.
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital.

Other practical notes and recommendations
- Student loan interest: although you paid $4,800, you can claim at most $2,500 on federal return (assuming you meet income limits). The rest is not deductible.
- Home office method decision: compute both simplified ($775) and actual (19.87% of rent + utilities/insurance/repairs). For your rent alone, the actual method (≈$5,009 from rent) looks substantially larger than the simplified amount, but remember the actual method requires more recordkeeping and could be limited if your business shows a loss. If your net self‑employment income (after other business expenses) is small, actual method may be limited to that income (excess may carry over). I can run a rough pro‑forma calculation if you want.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and potentially income tax beyond withholding. If this continues, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments for the next year.
- If those $890 are unreimbursed employee expenses (from your W‑2 job), they are not deductible under current law.
- Consider consulting a CPA or tax preparer to confirm the student loan phaseout numbers, to calculate the optimal home office method, and to prepare state return specifics.

