Short summary / headline
- Your W-2, interest income (bank + brokerage), and your graphic-design self-employment income are all taxable.
- The home-office deduction is available for your graphic-design work (if you meet the “exclusive and regular use” rules) even if you take the standard deduction on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment (up to $2,500 per year, subject to MAGI phaseouts) only for interest paid in the tax year you’re filing; you cannot claim interest from prior years now.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (if these are expenses for a job where you’re an employee) are generally NOT deductible on your federal return under current law. If those $890 are business expenses for your freelance work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.
- Whether you should itemize depends mostly on how much state/local tax + mortgage interest + other deductible items you have; with the items you listed, you’ll most likely take the standard deduction — but the home-office deduction is separate and still useful.

1) Treatment of each item you listed
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital ($84,900): ordinary wage income; reported on Form 1040 using your W-2.
- Graphic-design income ($8,900 from 2 clients):
  - Report on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (advertising, supplies, software, a portion of rent/utilities as home-office expenses, the $890 if they’re business expenses, etc.).
  - Net profit from Schedule C is subject to income tax and self-employment (SE) tax; you’ll also file Schedule SE to compute SE tax. Half of SE tax is an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
  - You will likely be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 8995) because your total income appears to be under the high-income thresholds.
- Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): taxable interest, reported on Form 1040 (1099-INT from banks/brokerage).
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; home 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month):
  - If you use the space exclusively and regularly for your design business and it is your principal place of business, you can claim a home-office deduction on Schedule C.
  - Two methods: simplified and regular. (See section 2 below for numbers.)
- Student loan interest ($4,800 MOHELA, 2016–2020):
  - IMPORTANT: The student loan interest deduction (Form 1098-E / Schedule 1 adjustment) applies only to interest you actually paid in the tax year you are filing for (e.g., interest paid during 2024 for the 2024 return). You cannot deduct interest paid in prior years on the current year’s return. If that $4,800 was paid back in 2016–2020, it’s not deductible on your 2024 return now.
  - If you did pay interest in 2024, you can deduct up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseout for MFJ). The deduction phases out at higher incomes.
- Charitable gifts ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest):
  - Deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct these contributions on Schedule A (except special temporary provisions in some tax years; typically not available).
  - Keep the receipt.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($890):
  - If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (for an employer job), they are not deductible federally under current law (suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions).
  - If they are business expenses for your 2 clients (self-employed), they go on Schedule C and reduce your self-employment income.

2) Home-office deduction — two options, with quick math
You meet the usual facts (exclusive use, regular use) — assuming yes.

- Simplified method:
  - $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775.
  - Easy, no Form 8829 required. Just enter on Schedule C.

- Regular method (actual-expense method):
  - Business percentage = home-office sq ft ÷ total home sq ft = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
  - Rent annual = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Your share of rent = 19.87% × $25,200 ≈ $5,007.
  - You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business portion), repairs, etc., which increases the deduction.
  - To use the regular method you generally complete Form 8829 (or include the calculation on Schedule C if you’re a sole proprietor).
  - Regular method usually gives a much bigger deduction here (≈$5k vs $775).

Practical note: The home-office deduction reduces your net self-employment income (lowering both income tax and SE tax). That often makes the regular method preferable when the allocated fraction of rent/utilities is substantial — but it requires more recordkeeping.

3) Itemize vs standard deduction (federal)
- You did not list mortgage interest or property taxes; you do list $1,400 charitable gifts and $4,800 student loan interest (but that looks like older years).
- The federal standard deduction for married filing jointly is (tax-year specific). Given the typical size of the standard deduction for MFJ and the relatively small itemizable amounts you listed, you will likely take the standard deduction.
- Important: The home-office deduction is separate and fully claimable even if you take the standard deduction.
- If you have sizable state income taxes withheld or paid (state/local tax up to $10k SALT cap), mortgage interest, or larger charitable gifts, you should add those up. If your total itemized deductions (state taxes + mortgage interest + charitable + medical over threshold + other allowed deductions) exceed the standard deduction, itemize. Otherwise, take the standard.

4) Most likely outcome (based on the numbers you gave)
- Likely you will take the standard deduction on Form 1040.
- You should still claim the home-office deduction on Schedule C (probably the regular method will save you more; see the math above).
