3) Self‑employment income and business expenses
- Report the $8,900 freelance income on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deductible business expenses: your $890 unreimbursed expenses are deductible on Schedule C if they are ordinary and necessary business expenses for the graphic design work (supplies, software, advertising, professional fees, etc.). Keep receipts.
- Self‑employment tax: you will owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings; you can then deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). You’ll compute SE tax with Schedule SE.

4) Home office deduction — what qualifies and how to claim it
- Because you’re self‑employed for the design work, you can claim a home office deduction for that business if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business (you said 155 sq ft exclusive — that sounds like it qualifies).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that’s 155 × $5 = $775. Easy, fewer records.
  b) Actual-expense method: prorate actual expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) by the business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200. Rent portion allocable to business ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,009 (plus business portion of utilities, internet allocated, etc.).
