Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s likely deductible, whether you’ll probably itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and basic state) forms you’ll need. I do have a couple of quick clarifying questions at the end that will change a few specifics.

Summary up front (quick answer)
- You must report your $8,900 graphic-design income on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on the net profit. That also lets you claim business expenses (including the home‑office allocation and the $890 if those are business expenses).
- For the home office, you can choose simplified ($5/sq ft = $775) or actual method (about 155/780 ≈ 19.9% of rent and other home expenses). Actual will almost certainly give you a larger deduction here, but it requires Form 8829 (or the Schedule C simplified entries).
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year is deductible, and the maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is $2,500 (phases out at higher incomes). You mentioned $4,800 — I need to confirm that $4,800 was paid in 2024; if not, it won’t be deductible now.
- Charitable gift $1,400 is deductible only if you itemize. Because the standard deduction for married filing jointly in 2024 is large (about $29,200), you will likely take the standard deduction unless you have large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable items that bring total itemized deductions above ~$29k.
- Interest from savings and brokerage ($920 + $320) is taxable income (reported on Schedule 1), not deductible.
- If the $890 is unreimbursed employee expenses (i.e., you were an employee paying those out of pocket), those are not deductible on your federal return under current law. If they are business expenses for your self‑employment (graphic design), they are deductible on Schedule C.

Detailed breakdown

1) Income you need to report
- W-2 wages: $84,900 — report on Form 1040 (W-2 box 1 goes to line 1).
- Self‑employment income: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑K, keep them but report the full income regardless.
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 — report on Schedule 1 (interest income). Expect 1099‑INT/1099‑B from institutions.

2) Business / self‑employment taxes and deductions
- File Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against the $8,900 (e.g., supplies, part of the home costs allocated to business, the $890 if it’s a business expense).
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax; compute on Schedule SE (roughly 15.3% on net profit), although half of SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

3) Home office
- Space: 155 sq ft exclusive use, home = 780 sq ft → business percentage ≈ 155 / 780 = 0.1987 (≈19.9%).
- Rent: $2,100/month = $25,200/yr. Using actual method, rent allocation to home office ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,000 (roughly).
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
  - Regular/Actual: deduct your share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allocable home expenses. For actual you generally file Form 8829 to calculate home office deduction and allowable depreciation (if you own). Note: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed the gross income from the business (after other business expenses) for the year — excess may be carried forward.
- Practical note: with rent that high, actual method will almost certainly give more benefit if your Schedule C net income is large enough to absorb it. But because your business income is $8,900, the home‑office deduction is limited to your net profit from the business (after other Schedule C expenses). If your business expenses (including home office) reduce the net to zero or negative, the unused home office expense may carry forward — Form 8829 will show that.

4) Student loan interest
- Only interest you actually paid in 2024 is eligible, and the deduction is an adjustment on Schedule 1 (up to $2,500, subject to AGI phaseouts).
- You reported $4,800 (MOHELA, 2016–2020) — if that refers to interest paid in prior years, you cannot deduct those now. If you actually paid $4,800 in 2024, the deductible portion is capped at $2,500 (and then subject to income phaseouts). With your income (~$95k before adjustments), you are likely under the phaseout threshold for MFJ, but I can check precisely if you want.

5) Charitable gift $1,400
- Deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction ($29,200 for MFJ in 2024), you won’t separately deduct the $1,400.
- Keep receipts (you said you have one).

6) Unreimbursed expenses $890
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses (you were an employee), they are not deductible on your federal return (TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions through 2025).
- If they are business expenses for your graphic design self‑employment (supplies, software, client travel, etc.), list them on Schedule C.

7) State taxes (Massachusetts)
- You’ll also file Massachusetts Form 1 (resident return). MA doesn’t allow all the same deductions as federal; charitable deductions and student loan interest rules differ. SALT (state and local taxes) is handled on federal Schedule A if you itemize — capped at $10,000. If you rent and don’t pay property tax, SALT will largely be state income tax withheld/paid.

8) Likely whether to itemize
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly in 2024 is about $29,200.
- Your known itemizable deductions (charity $1,400) are far below that. Unless you have significant mortgage interest, large state/local tax payments up to $10,000, or other deductible amounts that push you above $29,200, you will take the standard deduction.
- So: most likely you will take the standard deduction. The home office deduction is separate — it’s on Schedule C whether or not you itemize.

9) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 — for additional income (interest) and adjustments (student loan interest adjustment).
- Schedule C — report the $8,900 business income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you choose the actual home office method (to calculate allowable home office deduction). If you use the simplified method, you don’t need 8829.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (it sounds unlikely, but include if mortgage interest or large SALT pushes you above the standard deduction).
- Keep any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑K / 1099‑INT / 1099‑B you receive.

10) Practical tax implications & cash flow
- Because of the Schedule C home‑office deduction, your self‑employment taxable net income will be reduced (which lowers both income tax and self‑employment tax).
- You may owe a small amount of self‑employment tax; if you had little or no estimated tax payments and insufficient withholding, you could owe when you file (or be subject to estimated tax payments next year). With $8,900 gross, the net tax impact is limited but not zero.

Action items / questions for you (so I can be precise)
1. Is the $4,800 student‑loan figure the amount you paid in 2024, or is it an aggregate for 2016–2020? (Only 2024 payments are deductible on 2024 return.)
2. Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses related to your self‑employment (graphic design)? Or were they employee unreimbursed expenses?
3. Do you rent (you said $2,100/month) — confirm no mortgage interest or property tax? Any mortgage interest, property tax, or large state income taxes paid in 2024? (This affects the itemize vs standard decision.)
4. Do you want me to estimate the federal tax and self‑employment tax owed using the numbers you gave (I can run approximate calculations showing both simplified and actual home‑office results)?

If you confirm the 3 quick items above I’ll:
- run a brief numerical example showing how the Schedule C and home-office deduction affect your AGI and self‑employment tax, and
- tell you whether it’s almost certain you should take the standard deduction or whether itemizing could make sense.

Would you like that?

