Thanks — I can walk through this. I’ll cover (1) what’s deductible, (2) whether you should itemize or take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office, and (4) the federal forms you’ll likely need. A couple quick clarifying questions are at the end.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self-employment (graphic design)
  - Report the $8,900 on Schedule C (business income).
  - Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work are deductible on Schedule C (examples: supplies, software, client-related travel/printing, business portion of phone/internet, etc.). If your $890 of “unreimbursed expenses” relate to this freelance work, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return (TCJA suspension through 2025).
  - Home office: see section 3 below (you can deduct a portion of rent/utilities, etc., for the exclusive-use office).
  - Self-employment tax: you pay SE tax (Social Security/Medicare) on net self-employment earnings and may deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
  - Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: if you have net qualified business income after expenses, you may be able to take up to 20% of that on Form 8995 (simple cases). This reduces taxable income (not AGI).

- Interest income
  - Report $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 interest on Form 1040.

- Student loan interest
  - The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction (Schedule 1), but it is capped at $2,500 per year and available only for interest paid in the tax year on a qualified student loan. You cannot deduct more than $2,500 even if you paid $4,800. Also subject to income phaseouts (with your income level you’re likely eligible for the full amount).

- Charitable contributions
  - Your $1,400 donation to Northwest Harvest is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.

- State & local taxes (SALT)
  - You can deduct state income taxes (or state sales tax) and local property taxes on Schedule A, but the total SALT deduction is capped at $10,000 (Married Filing Jointly).

- Unreimbursed employee expenses
  - If these $890 are for W-2 employment and not for your freelance business, they are generally NOT deductible on the federal return (suspended through 2025).

2) Itemize vs standard deduction (federal)
- Standard deduction for 2024 (Married Filing Jointly) is $29,200.
- Rough comparison using the numbers you gave:
  - Likely itemizable amounts you listed: charitable $1,400 + state income tax (approx — MA has a ~5% flat tax; on $95k income that’s roughly $4.7k) = ~5,100. That’s well below the $29,200 standard deduction.
  - Unless you have significant mortgage interest, large unreimbursed employee deductions that are actually deductible, large unreimbursed medical expenses (above threshold), or high property taxes, you’ll almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have large additional itemizable deductions you didn’t list (mortgage interest, big property taxes, substantial unreimbursed business expenses that can’t otherwise be deducted on Schedule C but are allowable on Schedule A — rare).

3) Home office treatment
- You can deduct a home office only for self-employed/business use (Schedule C). It must be used regularly and exclusively for your business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Actual-expense method: calculate the business-use percentage = office sq ft ÷ total home sq ft = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs allocable to the office, etc.). Rent: $2,100 × 12 = $25,200 yearly → home-office share ≈ $25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,010. You’d report those office expenses on Schedule C and normally complete Form 8829 to support the calculation.
- Which to pick: the actual-expense method gives a much larger deduction here (≈$5,010 vs $775). Use actual-expense if you have good records of rent/utility amounts. Keep receipts and calculations. Note: if you have losses from the business, limits on deducting home office losses can apply, and tax rules can be complex — ask if you want me to run a more precise calculation.

4) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — U.S. Individual Income Tax Return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — additional income and adjustments (student loan interest deduction appears here; half of SE tax is an adjustment).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report your graphic design income and business expenses, including the home-office business share if you use actual-expense method).
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (to compute SE tax and the deductible half).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use actual-expense method for home office). Not needed for simplified method.
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income Deduction (if you want to claim the 20% QBI deduction; Form 8995 is the simpler form for most small businesses).
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you itemize; otherwise you won’t use this).
- 1098-E — Student loan interest statement from your loan servicer (MOHELA) — use it to support the student loan interest deduction (or your own payment records if you don’t receive a 1098-E).
- Any 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC you received from clients (report even if you didn’t receive a 1099; all income must be reported).

Other practical notes
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self-employment income, you may owe additional tax beyond what’s withheld on your W-2 (SE tax + income tax). If you expect similar freelance income next year, consider making quarterly estimated payments to avoid penalties.
- Records: keep receipts for charitable donations, home utility bills, rent, business expenses, and student loan interest statements. Save calculations supporting home office percentage.
- State return: you’ll also file Massachusetts resident return (Form 1). State rules differ (e.g., state may not allow the same adjustments), so check MA rules for student loan interest and home office treatment. I can look up MA-specific rules if you want.

Quick clarifying questions
1. Is the $890 of “unreimbursed expenses” related to your freelance/graphic-design work or to your W-2 employment? (If it’s freelance-related, include it on Schedule C; if W-2 employee expenses, it’s not deductible federally.)
