Thanks — I can walk through each piece and give a clear recommendation plus the exact forms you’ll need. Quick summary up front, then details and numbers.

High-level conclusion
- Most likely you should take the federal standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (MFJ). With the items you listed, your itemized deductions are unlikely to exceed the standard deduction.  
- Report your side‑gig (graphic design) on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). Use the home‑office rules to deduct the business portion of rent and other business expenses; that deduction is taken on Schedule C (either using Form 8829 for actual-expense method or the simplified method).  
- Student loan interest: you can only deduct up to $2,500 of interest as an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (subject to income phaseouts — with your income it’s very likely you’re eligible).  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible on your federal return (for most taxpayers) since the 2018 tax law change.

Details, calculations, and forms

1) Income — how to report
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital ($84,900): report on Form 1040 (W‑2 wages).  
- Graphic design income ($8,900 from 2 clients): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑Misc for those payments, include them and keep records of all business receipts. If you have business expenses (materials, software, subscriptions, client supplies, business portion of phone/internet, etc.) include them on Schedule C.  
- Interest income ($920 Marcus, $320 Schwab): these are reported as interest on Schedule 1 (or directly on Form 1040) and should match any 1099‑INTs you received.

2) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- You will owe self‑employment tax on your net Schedule C profit. Rough estimate (before home‑office/all business expenses): SE tax = 15.3% of 92.35% of net self‑employment income. With $8,900 gross: taxable SE income ≈ 0.9235×8,900 = $8,218 → SE tax ≈ $1,256. Half of that SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1 as an adjustment to income (so you get a small above‑the‑line deduction). The exact amounts change once you subtract business expenses (including home office).

3) Home office — treatment and calculation
- You said: exclusive home office = 155 sq ft; total dwelling = 780 sq ft. Business percentage = 155/780 ≈ 19.87% (~19.9%). That qualifies if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.  
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775 deductible on Schedule C. Simple and no Form 8829.  
  b) Actual expense method: you allocate a portion of actual home expenses to the business. For rent: $2,100/month × 12 = $25,200/year; business portion ≈ 19.87% of $25,200 ≈ $5,005. You can also allocate a business portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business share), etc. If you own the home and depreciate, you’d use Form 8829 — for renters you still use Form 8829 to report the portion of rent and utilities allocated to the business. Form 8829 will flow to Schedule C.  
- Which to pick? With your rent amount the actual method will likely give a much larger deduction (~$5,000 rent share alone) vs the simplified $775; so actual method probably better. Keep bills and a floorplan or measurement proof.

Important note about home office and losses: if business expenses (including home office) create a net loss from the Schedule C, there are limits if the activity is not for profit or if you’re subject to passive activity or at-risk rules — but generally small Schedule C losses are allowed to offset other income. If you’re unsure whether the activity is a bona fide business (two clients is borderline but usually OK if you have profit motive), keep documentation of efforts to get clients.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800 claimed)
- Federal student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 per year (not $4,800) and is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1). Income phaseouts apply; with your combined income (~$95k total federal income before adjustments) you are almost certainly under MFJ phaseout thresholds, so you should be able to take the full $2,500 maximum. Keep Form 1098‑E from MOHELA or your payment records.

5) Charitable contribution ($1,400)
- Deductible if you itemize (Schedule A) and you have receipts — you do. But charitable of $1,400 alone is not enough to beat the MFJ standard deduction unless you have substantial other itemized items (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, big medical, etc.). So unless you have additional large itemizable amounts not listed, you’ll likely take the standard deduction and not itemize.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($890)
- If these are unreimbursed employee business expenses (related to your W‑2 job), they are generally not deductible for federal purposes (TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions subject to the 2% floor through 2025). If they are business expenses for your graphic design activity (i.e., incurred on behalf of that business), put them on Schedule C. Clarify which they are.

7) State (Massachusetts)
- You’ll file MA Form 1 (Massachusetts resident return). MA starts from federal AGI but has differences in allowable deductions/credits. Some federal deductions (like the federal student loan interest above‑the‑line) may not carry the same way for MA — check the MA instructions or a tax preparer. Also check MA rules for deductions and whether unreimbursed employee expenses are allowed (generally they are not). You must consider state income tax and possibly estimated payments to MA if self‑employment increases your tax liability.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).  
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax deduction flow here.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $8,900 and business expenses (including home office allocation).  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use if you choose actual expense method for home office (renter’s share of rent, utilities, insurance). If you choose the simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829.  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (for your $1,400 charity and any other itemizable items).  
- Any 1099‑NEC/1099‑INT/1098‑E that you received — attach/enter amounts as required.  
- Massachusetts Form 1 (state return) and any required MA schedules.

9) Recordkeeping & next steps
- Keep: W‑2, 1099‑NEC or client records, 1099‑INTs, 1098‑E (student loan interest), charity receipts, rent statements, utility bills, copies of floorplan/measurements for the home office, receipts for business expenses.  
- If you expect to owe tax (you likely will owe some tax on your side gig and SE tax), consider making quarterly estimated payments to avoid underpayment penalties (both federal and MA).  
- If some of the $890 are business-related, separate them into business expenses (Schedule C) vs employee expenses (non‑deductible federally).

10) Rough numbers example (to give you an idea)
- Business gross: $8,900. If you take actual home office (rent portion ≈ $5,005) and no other business expenses: net Schedule C profit ≈ $3,895. SE tax on that ≈ 0.153×(0.9235×3,895) ≈ $552 (rough). Half SE tax deductible ≈ $276. If you instead use simplified home office ($775), net profit ≈ $8,900−775 = $8,125 and SE tax would be higher (~$1,150). So actual method will likely reduce both income and SE tax substantially. (These are illustrative; exact numbers depend on final expense items.)

Caveats
- I’m not a CPA. This is general guidance based on the facts you gave. Tax laws (phaseouts, standard deduction amounts, MA specifics) can change annually — always confirm current year thresholds. If your situation includes other unlisted items (mortgage interest, property tax, large state tax withholdings, investment gains, or losses), that can change the itemize vs standard decision.

If you’d like, I can:
- Run the numbers with your exact MA state withholdings, mortgage interest (if any), property tax, and any other deductible expenses to determine definitively whether you should itemize this year, or  
- Draft an email summarizing this to a CPA (including the numbers and documents to provide), or  
- Prepare a checklist of exact documents to gather for filing.

Which of those would you like me to do next?

