- The home office can be claimed as a business expense (actual or simplified method). Based on the numbers you gave, the actual method (allocating part of your rent) will almost certainly give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Student loan interest deduction is an “adjustment to income” (up to $2,500) but is subject to income limits — I need your AGI estimate to know whether it phases out for you.
- “Unreimbursed employee expenses” are NOT deductible for W‑2 employees under current law (2018–2025). If the $890 are business expenses related to your self‑employment work, they do belong on Schedule C.

Detailed breakdown

1) Income reporting
- W‑2 wages: report on Form 1040 (attach W‑2).
- Self‑employment income ($8,900): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from the two clients, include them; even if you didn’t receive a 1099, you still must report all income.
- Interest: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 — report as taxable interest on Form 1040. (If total interest/dividends > $1,500 you’d attach Schedule B, but you’re under that threshold.)

2) Home office treatment
- You said the office is exclusive, 155 sq ft, home is 780 sq ft.
  - Office percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
  - Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200.
  - Home-office portion of rent (actual method) ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,006.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (easy; no Form 8829).
  - Actual method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs) to the business. For renters, you can deduct your portion of rent + utilities portion as business expenses. If you use the actual method you generally use Form 8829 (or follow Schedule C instructions) to compute and report the deduction.
- Recommendation: Use the actual method (you’ll likely get ~ $5,000 vs $775). Make sure the space is used exclusively and regularly for business and is your principal place of business. Keep rent receipts, utility bills and documentation.

3) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Net profit from Schedule C (gross self‑employment income minus business expenses including the home‑office share and other business costs) is subject to self‑employment tax. You calculate SE tax on Schedule SE (about 15.3% on net earnings after the 92.35% adjustment).
- Half of self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1), reducing your AGI.

Example (illustrative, using amounts you gave and assuming the $890 are self‑employment expenses):
- Gross self‑employment income: $8,900
- Home-office actual rent portion ≈ $5,006
- Other business expenses: $890
- Net profit ≈ $8,900 − $5,006 − $890 = $3,004
- Approx. SE tax ≈ $3,004 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $424 (half ≈ $212 deductible)
