Thanks — I can walk through this. Below I’ll (1) summarize what’s deductible, (2) show how the home-office deduction works (with quick numbers), (3) advise whether to itemize vs take the standard deduction, (4) list the federal (and state) forms you’ll likely need, and (5) give a few next steps / questions so I can refine the answer.

1) What you can (and can’t) deduct — high level
- W-2 wage income (Riverside Hospital $84,900): report on Form 1040 (you don’t “deduct” that; it’s reported).
- Self‑employment income (graphic design $8,900): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (supplies, software, part of your home if used for the business, etc.).
- Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): report on Form 1040. (Schedule B is only required if interest/dividend > $1,500 or other conditions.)
- Student loan interest ($4,800): you can only deduct student loan interest paid in the tax year and the deduction is capped at $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). If the $4,800 were paid in prior years (2016–2020), those are not deductible on your 2024 return. If the $4,800 was paid in 2024, you can claim up to $2,500 (and need to check the AGI phaseout rules).
- Charitable contributions ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible on Schedule A if you itemize (must have receipts).
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $890: If these are work expenses for your W-2 job (employee unreimbursed expenses), they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025. If they are business expenses related to your self‑employment (graphic design), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home-office: deductible for the self‑employment activity (see below). Not deductible for W‑2 employment unless you are an eligible performing‑artist or certain government employees (rare).

2) Home office — how to treat it and quick calculation
You said: exclusive home-office area = 155 sq ft; home = 780 sq ft.
- Business use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9% (about 20%).

You have two methods:
A) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 155 = $775 deduction (no depreciation or Form 8829 required).
B) Regular method: allocate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) by business percentage and deduct that share on Schedule C. Example with your rent:
- Rent = $2,100/month → $25,200/year.
- Allocable rent to business = $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,010.
So, with only rent counted, the regular method yields much more ($~5,010) than the simplified $775. If you add utilities and renter’s insurance, regular method increases further. To use the regular method you would compute allocations on Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and report the result on Schedule C. Note: the home office deduction for self-employment cannot be used against W-2 wages — it only applies to the business income.

Important: the home-office deduction reduces your net self‑employment income (which also reduces self‑employment tax), but it must be for the self‑employment business and the space must be used regularly and exclusively for that business.

3) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- You will owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net self‑employment earnings. Schedule SE calculates this (roughly 15.3% on net earnings, with a deduction equal to half of the SE tax allowed on Form 1040).
- Half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1).
- The home‑office deduction (and other Schedule C expenses) reduce net self‑employment income and therefore SE tax.

Quick illustrative comparison (approximate):
- If you use the regular home allocation of rent (~$5,010): net business income ≈ $8,900 − $5,010 = $3,890 → lower SE tax.
- If you use simplified ($775): net business income ≈ $8,125 → higher SE tax.
(I can run more precise numbers if you want.)

4) Itemize vs standard deduction
- For 2024, the standard deduction for married filing jointly is roughly in the high $20k range (you should confirm the exact IRS amount for 2024 when preparing). Based on what you listed:
  - Charitable gifts = $1,400
  - Student loan interest (if deductible this year) = up to $2,500
  - State/local tax (SALT) — unknown; may be deductible up to $10,000 if you itemize
  - Other itemizable amounts you didn’t list (mortgage interest, large medical expenses, property tax) = unknown
- With just $1,400 charitable + up to $2,500 student interest = ~$3,900 in itemized deductions (unless you have sizable state taxes, mortgage interest, or other deductible items), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction. Conclusion: most likely take the standard deduction, but check if you have other itemizable items that push the total over the standard deduction.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (if required)
- Schedule C (Form 1040), Profit or Loss from Business — report the $8,900 and your business expenses
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if using the regular method for home office)
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize)
- Schedule B — Interest and Ordinary Dividends (only required if interest/dividends > $1,500 or other trigger conditions)
- Keep (and attach/enter) any 1099‑NEC, 1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT, or other information forms you received.

Massachusetts state return:
- You’ll need to file the Massachusetts resident return (Form 1) and report federal AGI and adjustments as required by MA. State tax is generally not avoided and you may owe MA tax on your combined income. (I can list MA line items if you want.)

6) Records to keep / practical points
- Keep receipts for the $1,400 donation, any business receipts, and records documenting the home-office (floor plan/photo and calculation), and Form 1098 (if mortgage interest, not applicable here).
- If you didn’t receive 1099s for the $8,900 but you earned it, you still must report it.
- If you expect similar self-employment income, consider quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties.
- The student loan interest deduction is subject to income phaseouts — if AGI is above the threshold the deduction phases out.

7) Quick questions so I can be precise
- Was the $4,800 student loan interest paid in 2024 (the tax year you are filing) or during 2016–2020? Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible now.
- Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” job expenses for your W‑2 employment or expenses for your graphic‑design business? (If business-related, they’re deductible on Schedule C; if W‑2 employee expenses, they aren’t deductible for 2018–2025.)
- Do you have any mortgage interest, property taxes, or sizable state/local taxes paid in the year? (That could change the standard vs itemize decision.)
- Do you want me to run the rough tax math comparing simplified vs regular home‑office methods (I can show estimated federal tax / SE tax impact based on either method)?

If you want, I can:
- run the two home‑office scenarios and show approximate federal tax and SE tax differences, or
- draft a checklist for the documents to give to a tax preparer, or
- prepare the exact list of forms/lines for the Massachusetts return.

Which of those would you like next?

