Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll cover (1) what’s likely deductible, (2) whether to itemize vs. take the standard deduction, (3) how to treat the home office, (4) what federal/state forms you’ll need, and (5) next steps and records to gather.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- W-2 wages ($84,900): not deductible — reported as wage income.
- Self‑employment (graphic design, $8,900): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work (supplies, software, advertising, part of home costs for the dedicated office, business portion of phone/internet if used for work, etc.). Net profit is taxable and subject to self‑employment (SE) tax.
- Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240): taxable interest reported on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INTs).
- Student loan interest ($4,800 reported from MOHELA): only interest actually paid in the tax year is potentially deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment (maximum $2,500 per return, subject to income phaseouts). You must have received a 1098‑E for the tax year showing the interest paid. If the $4,800 is interest paid over multiple prior years (2016–2020), only the amount paid during 2024 is relevant this year.
- Charitable cash gift ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible if you itemize. Keep the donation receipt.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($890): generally not deductible for most employees under current law (suspended 2018–2025), unless you are in a category that still qualifies (e.g., certain reservists, performing artists). If these expenses are for your self‑employment activity, they should be claimed on Schedule C instead.
- Other common business deductions: you can deduct half of your SE tax (above‑the‑line), business mileage or vehicle expenses used for the business, equipment/supplies, software, continuing education directly related to the business, and any fees paid to tax preparers for business tax prep.

2) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For Married Filing Jointly (tax year 2024) the standard deduction is substantially larger than typical small itemized totals. Based on the items you listed, the only definite itemized deduction is $1,400 charitable. You might have state income tax paid (amount withheld on your W‑2) that would add to SALT, but SALT is capped at $10,000.
- If your total itemized deductions (charity + deductible state/local taxes + any mortgage interest/property tax or other deductible items) are less than the standard deduction, take the standard deduction. Given the numbers you provided, it’s very likely the standard deduction will be better. If you want I can compare precisely if you tell me your state income tax withheld and any mortgage interest or property tax amounts.

3) Home office treatment
You can claim a home office deduction only if:
- You use part of your home regularly and exclusively for business, and
- That use is your principal place of business or a place where you meet clients/customers.

You said 155 sq ft exclusive use in a 780 sq ft rental — that qualifies if it’s used regularly and exclusively for the graphic design work.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (maximum 300 sq ft). No Form 8829 required.
- Regular (actual-expense) method: pro‑rate actual home expenses by business use percentage. Business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9% (~20%). Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; business portion of rent ≈ $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,008. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, and other eligible home expenses. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular method. The regular method often yields a larger deduction when rent/utility costs are high (which looks true in your numbers), but requires accurate records and may have limits if it would create or increase a loss from the business.
Recommendation: based only on rent, the regular method (≈ $5,000 of rent allocation) will likely be larger than the simplified $775. But you should add other allocable expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, internet business portion). If you choose the regular method you must complete Form 8829 and attach it (or enter the amounts in your tax software).

4) Forms you’ll need (federal + MA)
Federal (typical for your situation)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments): student loan interest deduction (if allowed) and other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report gross $8,900 and business expenses (including home office deduction via Form 8829 if using regular method)
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — business use of home (if using regular method)
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (otherwise not used)
- 1099‑INTs (Marcus and Schwab), 1099‑NEC (if you received any from clients), W‑2 from Riverside Hospital, 1098‑E (student loan interest, if > $600)
- If you qualify, you may also claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) for up to 20% of qualified business income — this is calculated on Form 1040 (and may require additional worksheets). With modest self‑employment income you will likely have some QBI deduction, but it depends on taxable income and the nature of the business.

Massachusetts state
- MA Form 1 (Massachusetts resident individual income tax return)
- MA will generally tax your federal adjusted gross income with state adjustments; student loan interest and other deductions differ from federal, so check MA rules or ask a preparer.

5) Other important notes and next steps
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (~15.3% on net self‑employment earnings, after applying the 92.35% multiplier). You can deduct half of that SE tax on your 1040 as an adjustment to income.
- Estimated tax payments: if your total tax liability (after withholding) is large enough, you may owe estimated quarterly taxes for the current year. With $8,900 freelance income this year you may still owe some additional tax — especially if withholding from your W‑2 is not covering it — so consider estimated payments going forward.
- Keep/document everything: retain receipts and records for home office calculation (floor plan, measurements), rent and utilities bills, charity receipts, 1098‑E, 1099s, and records of business expenses.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for most taxpayers (suspended). If those $890 items are actually business expenses for your freelance work, claim them on Schedule C instead.
- If any of the numbers above (state tax withheld, mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses) are larger than you’ve reported, run a comparison to see if itemizing beats the standard deduction.

If you want, I can:
- Do a quick estimated tax calculation and compare “standard vs. itemize” if you give me state tax withheld and any mortgage interest/property tax amounts, or
- Draft a checklist of documents to give to a CPA or to enter into tax software.

Which would you like me to do next?

