Quick summary (high level)
- You should report your graphic‑design income on Schedule C (it’s self‑employment income). That’s where most of your business deductions belong (including the home‑office deduction).
- The home‑office deduction is available for your graphic‑design work because you said the 155 sq ft is used exclusively and regularly for business. You can use either the simplified method or the actual‑expense method (see below).
- Student loan interest: you can generally deduct up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest as an adjustment to income (not an itemized deduction), subject to income phaseouts. You reported $4,800 paid — the deductible portion, if eligible, is limited to $2,500.
- Charitable giving ($1,400) is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $890) are generally NOT deductible for W‑2 employees under current tax law — but if that $890 relates to your graphic design business it should instead be claimed on Schedule C as a business expense.
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (tax year 2024) is large (about $29,200). With only $1,400 in known itemizable deductions, you’ll most likely take the standard deduction unless you have substantial mortgage interest, property tax or state/local taxes paid (SALT) that raise your itemized total above the standard deduction.
- You’ll probably owe self‑employment tax on your net self‑employment profit (Schedule SE) and can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.

What you can deduct (and where)
- Schedule C (business income & expenses)
  - Report all client income ($8,900) and related deductible business expenses: supplies, software, advertising, business insurance, professional fees, bank/merchant fees, travel for business, and any of the $890 that were business expenses.
  - Home‑office deduction (see next).
  - Equipment purchases: either expense (Section 179/bonus depreciation) or depreciate — recorded on Schedule C/Form 4562 as applicable.
- Home office
  - Actual‑expense method: allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., to the office based on square footage. Your percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Example: annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; 19.9% of that ≈ $5,013 would be allocable to the home‑office (plus your share of utilities, etc.). If you use actual expenses you typically complete Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home).
  - Simplified method: multiply the allowed office square footage (up to 300 sq ft) by the IRS simplified rate (simplified method is simpler — no Form 8829). This is easier but often gives a smaller deduction for large rent amounts. (If you want, I can compute both and show the difference.)
  - Important: the home office deduction is only available if you’re self‑employed (Schedule C) and the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
- Student loan interest
  - Up to $2,500 may be deductible as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1), but it phases out at higher modified adjusted gross incomes. Check phaseout limits — if your MAGI is above the limit you may not be able to claim the full $2,500.
- Charitable contributions
  - $1,400 is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct it.
- Interest income
  - Report $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 interest income on Form 1040. If total taxable interest and dividends exceed $1,500 you’ll also complete Schedule B (likely not needed at $1,240).

Other items to be aware of
- Self‑employment tax (Schedule SE): you will pay Social Security & Medicare on net self‑employment earnings. You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to a 20% deduction on qualified business income from your Schedule C, subject to limits. It’s calculated on Form 8995/8995‑A and depends on your taxable income and the nature/size of the business.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to cover income and SE tax unless you have sufficient withholding from W‑2 wages.
- 1099s & records: report all self‑employment income even if you didn’t get a 1099. Keep receipts, 1098‑E (student loan interest from MOHELA), receipts for the charitable donation, bank statements showing the interest, and documentation for rent/utilities if you claim the home office.

Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (individual return)
  - Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — where student loan interest and half SE deduction may appear)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your graphic design income & expenses
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual‑expense method for the home office; if you use the simplified method, you report the deduction directly on Schedule C and do NOT file Form 8829
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize instead of taking the standard deduction
  - Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) only if taxable interest + dividends exceed $1,500 or other conditions apply
  - Possibly Form 8995/8995‑A (QBI deduction) depending on taxable income and eligibility
- State:
