Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical plan. Below I’ll (1) summarize what’s deductible, (2) compare itemizing vs the standard deduction and give a recommendation, (3) explain the home‑office treatment and which method likely gives the bigger benefit for you, (4) list the tax forms you’ll need, and (5) flag a few follow‑ups I need to finish exact numbers.

1) What you can (likely) deduct
- Self‑employment business expenses: Report your graphic‑design income and any business expenses on Schedule C. That reduces your business profit and therefore your income subject to income tax and self‑employment tax. If the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the design work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible there. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible (suspended under current law).
- Home‑office deduction: Allowed for your self‑employment (graphic design) if the 155 sq ft space is used regularly and exclusively for business and is your principal place of business. See section 3 for more on methods.
- Student loan interest: You paid $4,800, but the above‑the‑line deduction limit is $2,500 (you cannot deduct more than $2,500). Also this deduction phases out above certain MAGI thresholds — with your reported income it’s likely you are under the phaseout, but we can check once you confirm final AGI.
- Charitable contributions: Your $1,400 gift to Northwest Harvest is deductible if it’s cash to a qualified charity and you itemize. There is no (routine) above‑the‑line deduction for cash gifts for 2024; so this helps only if you itemize.
- Interest income: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) = $1,240 — reportable as taxable interest on Form 1040 (Schedule B is not needed unless total interest/dividend > $1,500).
- State/local taxes (SALT): If you paid MA income tax or property tax during the year (withheld on W‑2 or paid separately), that can be included with itemized deductions up to the $10,000 SALT cap. You did not list these amounts; include them if you have withholding/payments.

2) Standard deduction vs itemizing (recommendation)
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200. (Confirm with IRS/current year tables if you want a double check.)
- Your known itemizable amount right now: charitable $1,400 + any SALT you actually paid. Unless you have substantial state tax withholding, mortgage interest, large property taxes, or other itemizable items, your itemized total will be far below $29,200.
- Recommendation: Most likely take the standard deduction ($29,200) and still claim the home‑office/business deductions and student‑loan interest (both reduce your AGI regardless of whether you itemize). The charitable gift only helps if you have enough other itemizable expenses to exceed the standard deduction.

3) Home office — how to treat it and which method to use
- You qualify for a home‑office deduction for the graphic‑design business if the space is used exclusively and regularly for business and is your principal place of business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (simple, no Form 8829).
  b) Actual (regular) method: compute percentage = 155 / 780 = 19.87% (≈19.9%). Apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, repairs, etc.). Example using rent only: annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; business portion ≈ 25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,012. Add the business portion of utilities and other allocable expenses and you’ll likely exceed the simplified $775.
- Important limitation: The home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business. If your business profit (before the home‑office deduction) is less than the full home‑office allocation, the excess may be limited and possibly carried forward (special rules apply for carryforwards). Because your reported gross self‑employment income is $8,900, after ordinary business expenses (e.g., that $890 if it’s a business expense) and before home‑office you probably have enough profit to take the larger actual deduction — we should run the numbers to be sure.
- Which to choose: The actual method almost certainly gives a bigger deduction here (since just the rent allocation alone ≈ $5k), but only if you have enough net self‑employment income to absorb it. If your profit is low or you prefer simplicity, the simplified method is available.

4) Forms you’ll need (most likely)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and half of self‑employment tax (the half‑SE deduction) are reported here.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report graphic‑design income ($8,900) and business expenses (advertising, supplies, that $890 if business‑related).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — computes self‑employment tax on net profit and the half‑SE deduction.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for home office; its result flows to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, follow the Schedule C instructions (no Form 8829).
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (to claim your $1,400 charity, SALT, etc.). Otherwise you’ll use the standard deduction.
- Schedule B — only if total interest/dividends > $1,500 (not needed at $1,240).
- Form 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC: you should have any 1099s your clients issued; report all self‑employment income even if you didn’t receive a 1099.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (Qualified Business Income deduction) — you may qualify for a QBI 20% deduction on your net self‑employment income; this is computed separately and can reduce taxable income. With modest business income this can help; we can check eligibility and compute it.

5) Other items and practical next steps
- Check whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses — if so, put them on Schedule C. If they are employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible.
- Confirm whether you had MA state tax withholding or paid state taxes — that affects whether itemizing could be worthwhile.
- Confirm the rent figure ($2,100/month) and any utility amounts that should be allocated to the home office.
- Student loan interest: confirm the lender’s 1098‑E or total interest paid; only up to $2,500 is deductible and it’s an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Estimated tax: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax / additional tax beyond W‑2 withholding. If you expect a balance due over $1,000 when filing, consider making quarterly estimated payments for the next year.

If you’d like, I can:
- Run the numbers for you (estimate taxable income, self‑employment tax, and show the difference between simplified vs actual home‑office methods). For that I’ll need: confirmation whether the $890 is business expenses, the exact rent and any utilities/insurance numbers you can share, and any state tax withheld or mortgage interest/property tax paid.
- Or I can draft an email to your tax preparer summarizing these items and listing the documents to bring.

