Below is a practical, plain-language summary of how to treat the income and expenses you listed, whether you should itemize, and which tax forms you’ll likely need. I’m not your tax preparer — this is general guidance. If you want exact tax figures or a return prepared, I can help run the numbers if you give me a few more details (state withholding, other deductions, exact business expenses, etc.), or you can ask a CPA.

1) Summary of your 2024 taxable items (from what you listed)
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital: $84,900 — reported on Form 1040 using the W‑2.
- Self‑employment (graphic design) income: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest income: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Charles Schwab) = $1,240 — report as interest on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INTs if > $10).
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 to MOHELA (2016–2020 payments noted) — treated as an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line) up to IRS limits (see below).
- Charitable cash gifts: $1,400 to Northwest Harvest — potentially deductible if you itemize and if you have receipts.
- Unreimbursed expenses: $890 — deductible only if they are business expenses associated with the self‑employment work; not deductible if they were unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses (those miscellaneous employee deductions are currently suspended at the federal level).

2) Home office (155 sq ft exclusive use; rental unit = 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month)
- Eligibility: Only available to self‑employed taxpayers (Schedule C). You cannot take a home office deduction for being a W‑2 employee unless you are in a very narrow exception (which generally does not apply now).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft → 155 × $5 = $775. No Form 8829 required; you put it on Schedule C as the home office deduction.
  b) Regular (actual expense) method: allocate a portion of your actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs in home office, etc.) to business. Your office is 155/780 = 19.87% (≈ 19.9%) of the home. If you only have rent as an eligible expense: annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200; allocated to business = $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,007. If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., add the business portion of those as well. The regular method needs Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute and attach to Schedule C.
- Practical note: the regular method here (rent allocation ≈ $5,007) exceeds the simplified $775, so the regular method probably gives a larger deduction — BUT you’ll need to include any other allowable shared home expenses and keep records/receipts. The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net income and thus reduces income tax and self‑employment tax.

3) Self‑employment tax and related items
- Your Schedule C net profit (8,900 minus business expenses, including the home office portion and other business costs) is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). The self‑employment tax is calculated on Schedule SE; roughly 15.3% on net earnings (subject to adjustment rules). You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- You may also qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) — this depends on taxable income and the type of business. If your taxable income is modest, you will likely be eligible for some QBI deduction. That is computed on Form 8995 or 8995‑A.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800 paid)
- The federal student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction up to $2,500 per return per year (subject to income phaseouts). It is taken even if you do not itemize (reported on Schedule 1).
- There are income limits (phaseouts) based on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI). Because you’re married filing jointly, the phaseout thresholds apply to MFJ limits; if your MAGI is above the phaseout range the deduction is reduced or eliminated. You’ll need to calculate MAGI to know whether you can take the full $2,500. Also check whether MOHELA issued a Form 1098‑E for student loan interest paid — that helps support the deduction.

5) Charitable gift ($1,400)
- Cash gift to a qualifying charity is deductible only if you itemize. You must have a bank record or written acknowledgement for donations of $250 or more. Since standard deduction (see next) is likely larger than your itemized total, the charitable gift might not help unless you have other itemizable deductions (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, etc.).

6) Standard deduction vs itemizing (likely outcome)
- For 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200.
- From the items you listed that go into itemized deductions, you only have $1,400 in charitable donations (student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction, not itemized). You did not list mortgage interest, large property taxes, or other major deductible items. With only $1,400 of itemized deductions, you are almost certainly better off taking the standard deduction of $29,200.
- Conclusion: take the standard deduction unless you have other significant itemized deductions (large state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses after threshold, etc.).

7) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to report adjustments like student loan interest deduction (and other adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $8,900 self‑employment income and business expenses (including home office deduction).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular (actual expense) method for the home office. Not used with the simplified method.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8995 or Form 8995‑A — to compute Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction if applicable.
- Form 1099‑NEC or reporting note: you should report all self‑employment income even if you did not get a 1099‑NEC from clients; conversely, expect any 1099‑INTs (from Marcus/Schwab) and include those amounts.
- Supporting documentation: Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA (if provided), receipts/acknowledgements for the $1,400 charity donation, rent receipts/utility bills for home office if using regular method.
- State: Massachusetts Form 1 (MA resident income tax return). State rules for deductions (student loan interest, etc.) differ — check MA instructions or I can look them up if you want.

8) Treatment of the $890 “unreimbursed expenses”
- If those expenses were for your graphic design self‑employment, include them as ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (they reduce self‑employment tax and income tax).
- If they were unreimbursed expenses related to your W‑2 employment, they are not deductible at the federal level under current law.

9) Practical next steps / checklist
- Gather documents: W‑2, 1099‑INTs, any 1099‑NEC from clients (or records of payments received), MOHELA Form 1098‑E if issued, bank/credit card receipts for business expenses, rent receipts/utility bills, charity receipt/acknowledgment.
- Decide home office method: simplified ($775) vs regular (~$5,007 using rent only). If you have only rent, regular likely yields a larger deduction, but it requires careful recordkeeping (and Form 8829).
- Compute Schedule C net income, then compute Schedule SE (self‑employment tax) and the half‑SE‑tax deduction on Schedule 1.
- Compute MAGI to see actual student loan interest deduction amount (up to $2,500) and QBI eligibility.
- Likely outcome: take the standard deduction, report Schedule C income and home office deduction (if self‑employed), claim student loan interest up to the allowable limit as an adjustment to income.

10) Offer
- If you want, I can:
  - Run an estimated federal tax calculation for you (I’ll need state withholding, any other income/deductions, and confirmation whether the $890 and other expenses are business-related).
  - Prepare a draft list of entries for your tax software or a checklist to bring to your preparer.
  - Look up the exact MAGI phaseout thresholds for the student loan interest and QBI limits for 2024 and tell you whether you qualify.

Would you like me to:
A) estimate your federal tax/payable using the numbers you gave (I’ll make reasonable assumptions, or you can provide additional numbers like MA withholding, other deductions), or
B) prepare a step‑by‑step checklist of documents to hand to a CPA or tax-prep software?

