Thanks — I can walk you through this. Below I’ll summarize what is (generally) deductible, how the home-office deduction works, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, the federal/state forms you’ll need, and a few clarifying questions so I can run the numbers more precisely.

High‑level summary
- You should report the Riverside W‑2 ($84,900) plus your self‑employment income ($8,900) and interest income ($920 + $320) on your 2024 Form 1040.
- Your graphic‑design work is self‑employment income — report it on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax on the net profit (Schedule SE). That also lets you deduct business expenses (including a home‑office deduction if you qualify).
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (potentially deductible up to the allowable limit) only for interest you actually paid in the tax year and subject to income limits.
- Charitable contributions ($1,400) are deductible only if you itemize; there is no broad above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025. But unreimbursed expenses that are business expenses for your self‑employment work are deductible on Schedule C.

Do you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200. (If you don’t have substantial mortgage interest, large state & local taxes, or big charitable gifts beyond the $1,400 you listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.)
- Based on the items you listed (only $1,400 charitable), it’s very likely the standard deduction is larger than your likely itemized total. If you have other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, SALT up to $10k cap, larger charitable gifts, big medical expenses above the AGI threshold), tell me and I’ll compare.

Treating the home office
- Because your graphic design work is self‑employment, you can take a business home‑office deduction if the space is used exclusively and regularly for the business.
- Two methods:
  1. Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that is 155 × $5 = $775. This is easiest and usually recommended when actual expenses are not huge.
  2. Regular (actual) method: deduct your business portion of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). You allocate by the percentage of your home used for business: 155 ÷ 780 ≈ 19.9%. If you pay $2,100/mo in rent ($25,200/yr), the rent portion attributable to the office would be about $25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,000 (plus any allocable utilities, etc.). Important limits:
     - The home‑office deduction may not create or increase a net loss from the business for the year. The allowed home‑office deduction is limited by your net profit from that business (excess may be carried forward in some circumstances).
     - Use Form 8829 when you use the regular method to calculate and claim the deduction. The simplified method does not require Form 8829.
- Recommendation: given your self‑employment income is $8,900, the simplified method ($775) is a safe, simple starting point. If you expect to report higher business income (or have large actual allocable expenses), consider the actual method — but you’ll need to check the net‑profit limitation.

Other deductions & tax effects to note
- Self‑employment tax: You’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings (calculated on Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Business expenses: The $890 in “unreimbursed expenses” — if these are expenses for your self‑employment (supplies, software, business mileage, etc.) they belong on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed expenses related to your W‑2 employment (employee expenses), they are generally not deductible through 2025.
- Student loan interest: Only interest you actually paid in the tax year is potentially deductible (up to the limit, generally $2,500) and is phased out at higher income levels. If your $4,800 figure is interest paid in previous years (2016–2020), those prior‑year payments are not deductible on your 2024 return — only 2024 payments count.
- 1099s: Even if you didn’t receive a 1099‑NEC from a client, you still must report self‑employment income. Keep records/receipts.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — adjustments to income (e.g., deductible part of self‑employment tax, student loan interest if eligible).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your graphic‑design income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — only if you use the actual method to claim business use of your home (not required for the simplified method).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not needed for you unless you have other big itemizable deductions).
- State: Massachusetts Form 1 (MA income tax return). MA has its own rules for deductions/credits.

Practical next steps I can help with
1. Clarify a few items so I can do a quick numeric estimate for 2024 and confirm whether itemizing would ever be better:
   - Did you pay $4,800 in student loan interest in 2024, or is that an aggregate amount from prior years (2016–2020)? Only interest paid in 2024 can be deducted on the 2024 return.
   - Are you renting (I assume yes based on $2,100/mo) and is the 155 sq ft space used exclusively for your design work? Is that space used only by you and only for business?
   - Are the $890 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for the graphic‑design work, or were they employee expenses related to your W‑2 job?
   - Any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, state/local income or property taxes, large medical expenses, other charitable gifts, retirement plan contributions, IRA/SEP/SIMPLE contributions, alimony, etc.)?
   - Is your spouse also working / have separate income items to include?
2. If you want, I can run through the math (estimate AGI, taxable income, SE tax, and compare standard vs itemizing) once you confirm the items above.
3. If you choose the actual home method, gather rent/utility bills and Form 8829 info; if simplified, you only need the sq ft for the office.

Caveat
- This is general guidance. For complex situations or if you want to minimize taxes or confirm phaseouts (student loan interest phaseout thresholds, etc.), consider working with a CPA or tax preparer or provide permission for me to run exact calculations and I’ll show the numbers and which forms/lines to fill.

If you’d like, reply with the answers to the clarification questions and I’ll compute estimated taxable income, self‑employment tax, and show whether itemizing could ever beat the standard deduction in your case.

