Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — here’s a clear, practical summary of what you can likely deduct, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions are below so I can be more precise.

Main points and assumptions
- I’m assuming you’re filing Federal tax year 2024 (married filing jointly) and that the amounts you listed (W-2, self-employment, interest, rent, donations, student-loan interest, unreimbursed expenses) are payments that occurred in the tax year you’re filing for. If some are from earlier years, tell me which so I can adjust.
- I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance. For final filing or complex situations (e.g., large depreciation, sale of house later, or questions about state treatment) check with a tax pro or the MA Dept. of Revenue.

1) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- The 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200.
- Your easily documented itemized deduction shown is $1,400 charitable cash — well below the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment (not a Schedule A item), so it doesn’t count toward itemizing.
- Conclusion: Unless you have additional itemizable deductions (large state/property taxes, mortgage interest, big medical expenses, etc.) that raise your total itemized deductions above $29,200, you should take the standard deduction.

2) What you can deduct and where to report it
- W-2 wages ($84,900): Reported on Form 1040 (you don’t deduct these; they’re income).
- Self-employment income ($8,900 — graphic design with 2 clients):
  - Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see below for home office & your $890 expenses).
  - Net profit from Schedule C flows to Form 1040 and to Schedule SE.
- Self-employment tax:
  - You’ll owe Self-Employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self-employment income; report and calculate on Schedule SE.
  - You may deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Interest income ($920 Marcus + $320 Schwab = $1,240):
  - Report this as interest income on Form 1040 (reported from any 1099-INTs you received).
- Student loan interest ($4,800 listed):
  - The maximum federal student loan interest deduction is $2,500 per return (subject to MAGI phaseouts). You can deduct up to $2,500 as an adjustment on Schedule 1 if you paid qualifying interest during the tax year and your MAGI is within the allowable range.
  - Important: Only interest paid in the tax year you are filing for is deductible. If the $4,800 represents interest paid across earlier years (2016–2020), you cannot deduct past-year interest now — only interest paid in the current tax year counts. Also, the deduction phases out at higher incomes — if your MAGI is above the phaseout range you may not be eligible for the full $2,500.
- Charitable donation ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest):
  - Deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct this on federal return (except limited special rules from prior pandemic years — not generally applicable).
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $890:
  - If these are ordinary and necessary business expenses related to your self-employed graphic design work, they are deductible on Schedule C.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (for which you receive a W-2) those are not deductible on federal returns for tax years after 2017 (TCJA suspension) unless you are in a very limited category of employees.
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive use; home = 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month):
  - You are a self-employed person for your graphic design work, so you can take a home office deduction for the portion of your home used exclusively and regularly for business.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft (up to 300 sq ft) = 155 * $5 = $775.
    - Regular method (actual expenses): allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the business. Your business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87% (~20%).
      - Rent: $2,100/month × 12 = $25,200 × 19.87% ≈ $5,007 allowed as a business expense.
      - You can also allocate a business portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allowable home expenses (but not mortgage interest or property taxes if you rent — those are for owners).
    - For a renter, the regular method almost certainly yields a bigger deduction here (≈$5,000+) vs the simplified $775.
  - Report the home office deduction on Schedule C as a business expense. Because you rent, you do not claim depreciation on the residence itself (depreciation primarily applies to owners).
  - Note: taking the actual method (regular) can have later tax considerations if you own and sell the home (depreciation recapture) — not an issue for renters now.

3) Quick illustrative numbers (rough example)
- Self-employment gross: $8,900
- Less home office (regular method, rent portion): ≈ $5,007
- Less other business expenses (if the $890 are business-related): −$890
- Rough net business income ≈ $8,900 − $5,007 − $890 = $3,003
- Self-employment tax ≈ 15.3% of net income → ≈ $460 (approx); half of that (~$230) is deductible on Form 1040 as an adjustment.
- These are illustrations — actual tax depends on exact deductible amounts and any additional expenses/credits.

4) Forms you will generally need (Federal)
- Form 1040 — main individual return.
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — to report self-employment income (graphic design) and business expenses, including home office deduction.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax.
- Schedule 1 — for certain additional income and adjustments (business income flows through here; student loan interest deduction also shown on Schedule 1/adjustments).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (unlikely given the standard deduction amount versus your listed itemized items).
- 1099-NEC/1099-MISC and 1099-INT — supporting forms you’ll receive from clients and banks; keep them and report the amounts.
- Keep receipts (charity, business receipts, rent statements, 1098-E for student loan interest if available).

Massachusetts state return
- File MA Form 1 (resident return).
- MA generally starts with federal adjusted gross income and applies state-specific adjustments and exemptions. Massachusetts has its own rules for deductions and exemptions — check MA Dept. of Revenue guidance or ask a tax preparer for specifics (e.g., MA treatment of student loan interest and home office expenses may differ).
- You’ll also report MA withholding shown on your W-2 on your MA return.

5) What I need from you to be more precise (and recommended next steps)
- Clarify whether the $4,800 student loan interest was paid in the tax year you are filing for (or were those earlier years?); do you have a 1098-E for the tax year?
- Confirm whether the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” are expenses for your graphic design business (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job.
- Confirm whether you have any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, property tax, large state income tax payments, medical expenses, etc.).
- If you want, I can:
  - Run a simple tax effect estimate (approx. taxable income and tax) if you give me the missing clarifications and any MA withholdings or other deductions.
  - Draft an email to your tax preparer with the facts and ask for a review.

6) Practical tips & records to keep
- Keep 1099s, 1098-E (student loan interest), bank 1099-INT, receipts for charitable donations, rent receipts or lease showing rent amount, utility bills (if using regular home office method).
- If you didn’t receive 1099-NEC for the $8,900, you still must report it.
- If you expect to owe tax on self-employment income, consider estimated tax payments quarterly to avoid penalties.

If you want, I can:
- Estimate whether the regular or simplified home office method gives a larger deduction using the exact percent and amounts you give.
- Calculate an approximate federal tax liability and self-employment tax given your inputs.
- Or draft a short summary email you can send to a tax preparer (I can draft it and, if you want, save as a draft email). Which would you like next?

