Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll summarize what’s likely deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office and self‑employment income, what forms you’ll need, and next steps. A couple of quick clarifying questions are at the end because a few answers change the recommendations.

What’s deductible (at a glance)
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital $84,900): reported on Form 1040 via the W‑2.
- Interest income (Marcus $920 + Schwab $320 = $1,240): taxable interest. Report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only needed if total interest > $1,500 or other triggers).
- Self‑employment income (graphic design $8,900): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against this income (see below).
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive use in 780 sq ft home): deductible as a business expense for your Schedule C if it meets the regular-and-exclusive-use test. You can choose:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.
  - Regular/Actual-expense method: allocate a share of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Your share = 155 / 780 = ~19.87%. Annual rent = $2,100 × 12 = $25,200, so rent portion ≈ $5,006. Add proportional utilities/insurance. Use Form 8829 to compute actual-expense deduction (if using actual method).
  - Which method is best depends on your actual expenses — actual method usually yields more when your allocated rent/utilities is larger than the simplified amount.
- Business expenses (the $890 listed as “unreimbursed expenses”): If these are business expenses for the graphic design work, include them on Schedule C (they reduce self‑employment tax too). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (you incurred them as an employee of Riverside), they are not deductible for tax years through 2025 (TCJA suspension).
- Student loan interest ($4,800 note): only up to $2,500 of student loan interest can be deducted as an adjustment to income (above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1), and it’s limited by your MAGI and other rules. Also, the deduction applies to interest paid in the tax year you’re filing for — you can’t deduct interest paid in prior years on the current-year return. You need the Form 1098‑E from your servicer showing interest paid that year. Note: married filing separately makes you ineligible for this deduction.
- Charitable contributions ($1,400 to Northwest Harvest): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Make sure you have the written receipt.
- State income taxes / SALT: you may deduct state income taxes paid (or state/local sales tax) up to the SALT cap of $10,000 if you itemize. If you had MA withholding or estimated payments in the year, include them in your itemized total.

Standard deduction vs itemizing
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200 (confirm the tax year you mean; if you mean filing in 2024 for tax year 2023 the standard deduction was $27,700). Compare your total itemized deductions (charitable contributions + deductible state taxes paid + mortgage interest / property tax if any + qualified medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI, etc.) to the standard deduction.
- Based on the numbers you gave (charity $1,400 + maybe limited SALT and no mortgage interest mentioned), it’s likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized total — so most likely standard deduction will be better. Exceptions: if you have substantial state tax paid or mortgage interest or large medical expenses, itemize.

Self‑employment and taxes you’ll need to pay/file
- Report graphic design income and business expenses on Schedule C (Form 1040).
- Pay self‑employment tax via Schedule SE (this is Social Security and Medicare on net self‑employment income). You can deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an above-the-line deduction.
- You may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) if your combined withholding doesn’t cover your expected tax liability or if you will owe $1,000+ after withholding/credits.
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) — a possible 20% deduction of qualified business income depending on your total taxable income and the nature of the business. This is calculated on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) and can reduce taxable income.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- W‑2 (from Riverside Hospital).
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income such as student loan interest deduction — Part II).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $8,900 graphic design income and related expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual-expense method to calculate the home-office deduction. If you use the simplified method, you won’t file Form 8829 (you still report the deduction on Schedule C).
- Form 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC if you received one from a client(s) — you must report income even if not issued.
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) to support any student loan interest deduction; if you don’t have a 1098‑E for the year, you cannot claim the deduction for prior-year interest payments.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you decide to itemize (to claim charity, SALT up to $10k, mortgage interest, etc.).
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) only if interest/dividend thresholds apply (interest > $1,500 or other triggers).
- Form 8995 (QBI) if you claim the qualified business income deduction.

Other practical points & recordkeeping
- Keep receipts for charitable donations, business expenses, home-office calculation records (floor plan, sq ft calculation, bills showing rent/utilities), 1099s and 1098s.
- If some “unreimbursed expenses” were business expenses, classify them and put them on Schedule C. If they were employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible through 2025.
- Student loan interest: double-check which year(s) the $4,800 was paid. You can only deduct interest paid during the tax year for the current return and up to the yearly limit ($2,500 max) subject to income phaseouts.
- If you expect to owe additional tax due to self-employment income, consider making estimated payments to avoid penalties.

Quick example sketch (very rough)
- Total income before adjustments ≈ W‑2 $84,900 + interest $1,240 + self‑employment gross $8,900 = $94, + something. If your net self‑employment profit (after business deductions) is, say, $8,000, you’ll owe SE tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net) and income tax on the combined taxable income after deductions. The home‑office actual method (rent portion ≈ $5,006) would substantially reduce self‑employment taxable income compared with the simplified $775, but you must include other proportional expenses properly. Use tax software or a CPA to run the numbers and compare simplified vs actual.

Questions I need to finalize advice
1. Which tax year are you filing for — tax year 2024 (returns due 2025) or tax year 2023 (returns due 2024)?
2. Did you actually pay the $4,800 in student loan interest during the year you’re filing for, or were those payments in prior years (2016–2020 as you wrote)? If the latter, those cannot be deducted on the current return.
3. Are the $890 “unreimbursed expenses” employee expenses (related to your hospital job) or business expenses for your graphic design clients?
4. Do you rent (you said $2,100/month — I assume yes) and do you pay any mortgage or property tax? Do you have significant state income tax withholding or estimated payments for MA?
5. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC from your graphic design clients? Any 1099‑INTs or 1099‑DIVs from Marcus/Schwab?

If you want, I can:
- Run the rough math and compare standard deduction vs itemizing using estimated state taxes withholding if you give the MA withholding amount and confirm the student loan interest timing; or
- Draft an email to your CPA with these details and questions; or
- Prepare a checklist of documents to collect before you file.

