Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, and which federal/state forms you’ll likely need. Before I give a firm recommendation on itemizing I’ll need one or two numbers from you (see “What I need from you” at the end). Below is a clear summary and practical next steps.

1) Quick summary of the main items you listed
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $84,900 — report on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment income (graphic design): $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest income: $920 (Marcus) + $320 (Schwab) — report as interest income on Form 1040 (you’ll get 1099‑INT or 1099‑B statements).
- Student loan interest paid: $4,800 (MOHELA) — student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income but limited to $2,500 maximum (and phases out at higher MAGI). You’ll likely be eligible for up to $2,500 given the income shown, but we should confirm final MAGI and phaseout thresholds for 2024.
- Charitable gift: $1,400 to Northwest Harvest — deductible only if you itemize (must be to a qualified charity and you should keep the receipt).
- $890 unreimbursed expenses — important distinction: if these are employee unreimbursed work expenses (you are an employee for someone else), they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 because miscellaneous itemized deductions were suspended. If they are business expenses related to your self‑employment (graphic design), they belong on Schedule C and ARE deductible.
- Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive use, home = 780 sq ft; rent $2,100/month ($25,200/year). Treatment explained below.

2) Home office — how to treat it (self‑employed)
Because your graphic design income is self‑employment income, you can use either:
- Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (no Form 8829 needed).
- Regular/actual method: allocate a portion of actual home expenses to the business. Your office percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Applied to annual rent: $25,200 × 19.9% ≈ $5,009 that can be deducted as a Schedule C business expense (you can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, etc. if used for the business). For renters you don’t depreciate the building; for homeowners depreciation rules differ.

Which to pick? Based on the rent amount, the actual method is likely far more beneficial here (approx $5,009 vs $775). Using the actual method also reduces your self‑employment net income (and thus your income tax and self‑employment tax). If you use the actual method you typically complete Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) or include the calculation on Schedule C (software/tax preparer will guide you).

Requirements: the space must be used regularly and exclusively for the business and must be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, or a separate structure). Keep records (floor plan, photos, lease, utility bills, receipts).

3) Self‑employment tax and other self‑employment deductions
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). Net earnings are roughly (gross self‑employment income − business expenses including the home office deduction). The SE tax rate is about 15.3% on net earnings (with a computation using 92.35% of net earnings); one-half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.
- The self‑employed can also deduct health insurance premiums (if eligible), retirement plan contributions (SEP IRA or Solo 401(k)), and other ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C.

4) Itemize vs standard deduction
- You’ll compare the standard deduction for married filing jointly (for the 2024 tax year use the IRS published standard deduction amount for 2024) to your total itemized deductions (Schedule A).
- Itemizable items you may have:
  - State and local taxes (income tax withheld + any state estimated payments + property taxes) — BUT limited to $10,000 total (SALT cap).
  - Charitable contributions ($1,400).
  - Mortgage interest (if you had any — you said you pay rent so likely none).
  - Medical expenses deductible only to the extent they exceed 7.5% of AGI.
- Items you cannot deduct on Schedule A for 2018–2025: unreimbursed employee expenses (the $890) — these are disallowed unless they are business expenses for your self‑employment (then they go on Schedule C instead).
- Given the numbers you provided (no mortgage interest, only $1,400 charitable, and SALT likely limited by your state tax withholding/property tax), you may well be better off taking the standard deduction. However — because your home office deduction lowers your taxable self‑employment income (and thus your AGI), and because the home office gives you a large business deduction (if actual method), your federal taxable income may be lower regardless of whether you itemize. The key comparison is total Schedule A vs standard deduction.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C — report business income and expenses (graphic design).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — use if you choose the actual method for the home office (not needed with simplified method).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize.
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑INT / 1099‑B — include income reported on these (you’ll receive statements from clients/banks).
- Documentation: Form 1098‑E for student loan interest (if issued) to substantiate interest paid; receipts for charitable gifts.
- State: Massachusetts Form 1 (MA resident return). Massachusetts has its own rules for deductions/exemptions — you will report your federal AGI and then follow MA instructions. (SALT cap is a federal limitation; MA will still let you claim state taxes on the MA return as allowed under state law.)

6) Specific points and limits to note
- Student loan interest: maximum deduction is $2,500 (not the full $4,800 you paid). There is a MAGI phaseout for married filing jointly — since your combined income (approx $94k before adjustments) is below typical phaseout thresholds you’re likely eligible for the full $2,500, but I’ll confirm with exact MAGI calculations for 2024.
- SALT cap: state + local income tax + property tax deductible on Schedule A is capped at $10,000 for married filing jointly.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 employment) are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.
- Keep receipts and records for the home office, business expenses, charitable gifts, and student loan interest.

7) Practical recommended next steps
- Confirm whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses related to your graphic design work (if yes, include them on Schedule C; if no, they’re not deductible).
- Confirm state income tax withheld (from your W‑2) and any property taxes you paid in 2024 — needed to determine whether itemizing could beat the standard deduction.
- Decide whether you prefer the actual or simplified home office method. Based on the rent you reported, the actual method is likely larger (approx $5,009 vs $775).
- If you want, I can:
  - run the numbers and compare “itemize vs standard” for you if you give me state tax withheld and any property taxes or other itemizable expenses; or
  - prepare a checklist of documents to send to a preparer; or
  - draft an email with these details for your CPA.

What I need from you to finalize a recommendation
- State income tax withheld in 2024 (from your W‑2) and any other state/local taxes or property tax you paid in 2024. OR
- Permission for me to estimate using typical MA withholding (I recommend you provide the actual number).
- Clarify whether the $890 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the graphic design work or employee unreimbursed expenses.

If you want, I can now:
- (A) run an itemize vs standard comparison using the numbers you gave and a conservative estimate for MA withholding, or
- (B) prepare a tidy checklist of documents and amounts to give to your tax preparer, or
- (C) draft the exact words to email your CPA with these numbers and questions.

Which would you like me to do next?

